.TH SNAJPAGENT 1 "September 2026" "snajpagent" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snajpagent \- terminal coding agent with built-in IRC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ initial-prompt " ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-\-resume
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ session-id " | -N NAME | --last]"
.RI "[-- " follow-up " ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-\-attach
.RI [ --dotdir " PATH]"
.RI [ session-id " | -N NAME]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-e
.RI [ options ]
.RI "[-- " prompt " ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent \-l
.RI [ N ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.B login
.RI [ provider ]
.RI [ --openai-device-auth " | --meta-device-auth | --with-api-key | --with-access-token]"
.br
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.B login status
.RI [ provider ]
.br
.B snajpagent
.RI [ options ]
.B logout
.RI [ provider ]
.br
.B snajpagent remote
.RI [ --config " PATH]"
.RI [ --dotdir " PATH]"
.RI [ -- ]
.RI [ COMMAND " ARG ...]"
.br
.B snajpagent vm
.RI [ --dotdir " PATH]"
.RI [ -N " NAME | --resume WORKSPACE | --last | -l [N]]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snajpagent
is a coding agent for the terminal, with a built-in IRC server and client.
The model can read and edit files and run commands.  The program runs in the
foreground and keeps resumable local sessions; there is no resident service.
.PP
An interactive session can independently host one single-room IRC server and
maintain up to 16 outgoing
IRC connections.  Agents and channel operators share the room; the runtime
owns joining, history, and reconnection.  Startup flags and runtime slash
commands control this native networking without changing session identity.
.SH "Getting started"
.SS Documentation and build identity
This manual describes the source revision it accompanies.  The web manual
tracks current source; each downloadable release includes a matching manual.
Run
.B snajpagent \-V
to identify the executable on disk.  A running session retains the code with
which it started.  After an update, exit normally and use its printed resume
command to launch the new code; a rebuild alone does not replace a live process.
.PP
Check the downloads page for the latest stable release; this manual matches the
release it ships with.  Later source commits are listed under Unreleased in
CHANGELOG.md and do not replace the immutable release assets.
.SS Choose a download
The downloads page at
.I https://agent.snajpa.net/downloads.html
has Linux, macOS, Windows, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD panels. Choose the
architecture and OS/ABI shown in the row, including its experimental status.
The standalone-build reference below explains each target's requirements.
.PP
Download to a user-owned directory. Rename the selected executable to
.B snajpagent
(or
.B snajpagent.exe
on Windows). In the examples below, first compare the displayed SHA-256 with
that file's download row or the release's SHA256SUMS. Run the remaining commands
only after it matches and you trust the source. If a legacy system lacks a
SHA-256 utility, verify on a trusted newer machine and transfer over a trusted
channel; MD5, SHA-1 and plain CRC checksums are not substitutes.
.PP
For an archive, hash the archive before extracting it; its published checksum
is not the checksum of the executable inside. Runnable debug archives contain
an executable; separate production symbol archives only diagnose the matching
production download. No compiler, Nix installation or debug symbols are needed
to run an official executable.
.SS "Install on Linux"
Choose x86-64, AArch64, i686, ARMv6/ARMv7 hard-float, RV64GC/LP64D,
POWER8 little-endian or 32-bit big-endian PowerPC to match your system.
The legacy i686 download is separate from modern i686. Application libraries
and TLS roots are included in these self-contained executables.
.PP
.nf
sha256sum ./snajpagent
chmod +x ./snajpagent
\&./snajpagent
.fi
.PP
Legacy i686 requires procfs and secure OS entropy; PTY commands also require
devpts. Choose the download's documented kernel/CPU baseline, not just a
matching word size. Android has experimental source support, but no production
download target; these Linux downloads are not an Android installation recipe.
.SS "Install on macOS"
Choose Apple Silicon, Intel, or the universal executable containing both
architectures; the downloads target macOS 11 or later. These experimental
executables are not Developer ID signed or notarized.
.PP
.nf
shasum \-a 256 ./snajpagent
chmod +x ./snajpagent
\&./snajpagent
.fi
.PP
A quarantined browser download can be blocked at first launch with
.BR "Killed: 9" .
After checking the checksum and only if you trust the file, remove quarantine
from that executable and launch again:
.PP
.nf
xattr \-d com.apple.quarantine ./snajpagent
\&./snajpagent
.fi
.PP
This is a per-file security exception. Keep system-wide macOS protections
enabled; execute permission alone does not clear quarantine.
.SS "Install on Windows"
Choose the experimental native x64 or ARM64 .exe. Application libraries and
TLS roots are included; ARM64 also needs the operating system's UCRT.
The standalone-build reference records the limited Windows qualification.
In PowerShell, from the directory containing the renamed file:
.PP
.nf
Get\-FileHash .\esnajpagent.exe \-Algorithm SHA256
\&.\esnajpagent.exe
.fi
.PP
In Command Prompt, where certutil is available:
.PP
.nf
certutil \-hashfile .\esnajpagent.exe SHA256
\&.\esnajpagent.exe
.fi
.PP
Keep the .exe extension. PowerShell requires the explicit .\e prefix for an
executable in the current directory. To work elsewhere, add its user-owned
folder to your user PATH or invoke its full path. PowerShell uses the call
operator for a quoted path, for example:
.PP
.nf
cd C:\ework\emyproject
& "C:\eUsers\eAlice\eTools\esnajpagent.exe"
.fi
.PP
Commands in this manual otherwise use POSIX shell syntax. The Windows tool
shell defaults to cmd.exe, even when the agent is launched from PowerShell;
configure tools.shell explicitly to choose another shell. State defaults to
HOME/.snajpagent when HOME is set, otherwise USERPROFILE/.snajpagent.
Use \-\-dotdir DIR to choose another private location.
.SS "Install on FreeBSD"
Choose the amd64 download for the listed OS/ABI. The FreeBSD 5.1/5.5 legacy
executable requires libc.so.5 and libc_r.so.5; the other variant targets the
libc.so.7/libthr.so.3 ABI. Application libraries and TLS roots are included;
the OS loader and matching base libraries must be present.
.PP
.nf
sha256 \-q ./snajpagent
chmod +x ./snajpagent
\&./snajpagent
.fi
.PP
If an older installation lacks sha256, use the trusted-machine verification
procedure above. Renaming or copying a newer executable cannot change its ABI.
.SS "Install on OpenBSD"
Choose the separate amd64 download for OpenBSD 7.9, 5.9 or 3.5. Their native
loader/libc/thread ABIs differ; use the exact listed variant. Application
libraries and TLS roots are included. Use sha256 where available; on older
installations without it, verify on a trusted newer system before transferring
the executable, then grant execute permission and launch.
.PP
.nf
sha256 \-q ./snajpagent
chmod +x ./snajpagent
\&./snajpagent
.fi
.SS "Install on NetBSD"
Choose the amd64 download for NetBSD 10.1 (libpthread.so.1) or the legacy
2.0/5.2.3 variant (libpthread.so.0); both use libc.so.12. The OS loader and
matching base libraries are required. Application libraries and TLS roots
are included. On NetBSD 5.2.3 and 10.1:
.PP
.nf
cksum \-a SHA256 ./snajpagent
chmod +x ./snajpagent
\&./snajpagent
.fi
.PP
NetBSD 2.0's base cksum lacks SHA-256. Verify on a trusted newer system before
transferring the file to 2.0, then grant execute permission and launch it.
.SS Put the executable on PATH
On Linux, macOS and the BSDs, after verification, a user-local installation
avoids administrator privileges. Run this from the download directory in a
POSIX shell (start sh first if you normally use csh or tcsh):
.PP
.nf
mkdir \-p "$HOME/.local/bin"
cp ./snajpagent "$HOME/.local/bin/snajpagent"
chmod 755 "$HOME/.local/bin/snajpagent"
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
snajpagent
.fi
.PP
Keep the PATH assignment in your shell's startup configuration to use it in
future terminals. The Windows instructions above explain user PATH and full
paths. New sessions use the home directory as their working directory,
regardless of the shell directory used to launch the program.
Use the source-build instructions below for other build environments; an
unlisted OS or ABI is not a qualified binary target.
.SS Provider setup
On a fresh interactive launch without configuration or existing environment
credentials, choose ChatGPT/Codex, Meta subscription, OpenRouter, OpenAI, or a custom provider.
Complete device login or enter a masked API key (blank selects an environment
variable), optionally fetch that provider's catalog, and choose a model.
Setup creates a minimal private configuration and separate credential store,
then continues into the ordinary session.  Cancellation leaves prior settings
and credentials intact.
.PP
For manual configuration on POSIX systems, create a private state directory:
.PP
.nf
install -d -m 700 "$HOME/.snajpagent"
.fi
.PP
Create
.IR $HOME/.snajpagent/config.ini ,
choosing a model supported by your provider:
.PP
.nf
[agent]
provider = openai
retry_auto = true
model = gpt-5.5

[provider openai]
base_url = https://api.openai.com
api_key = ${OPENAI_API_KEY}
.fi
.PP
Export the named credential, change to the project directory, and run:
.PP
.nf
export OPENAI_API_KEY='your-key'
cd /path/to/project
snajpagent
.fi
.PP
Enter submits your text.  An empty or whitespace-only prompt leaves its line
visible and opens a fresh prompt, like a shell, in both rollout and chat.
To ask the model to continue, type your request and press Enter.
Type
.B /help
for command syntax and keys; square brackets mark optional arguments.
.PP
On Windows, first-run setup creates the directory itself. For manual setup,
create the private dotdir under HOME or USERPROFILE as described above and
save the same config.ini contents there as plain UTF-8 text. Keep credentials accessible only to your account.
Set a credential for the current shell before launching. In PowerShell:
.PP
.nf
$env:OPENAI_API_KEY = 'your-key'
& "C:\eUsers\eAlice\eTools\esnajpagent.exe"
.fi
.PP
In Command Prompt:
.PP
.nf
set "OPENAI_API_KEY=your-key"
C:\eUsers\eAlice\eTools\esnajpagent.exe
.fi
.PP
These shell assignments last for that shell and its child processes.
Interactive masked-key entry or device login avoids putting a secret in shell
history. The provider configuration syntax is the same on all platforms;
POSIX export/chmod/install commands are not Windows commands.
.SS Start a task
After installation and provider setup, start snajpagent. New sessions begin
with the home directory as their working directory. The model can call
.B get_cwd
to inspect it and
.B cd
to change it; absolute paths work anywhere the process can access.
File and command tools run with your OS permissions, without per-call approval.
Tool calls are hidden at the default verbosity.
.PP
Without network roles, startup opens rollout: the local view of your
conversation and the model's work.  Describe a task and press Enter:
.PP
.nf
Fix the empty-input bug. Keep the public API unchanged.
Run the tests and summarize the change.
.fi
.PP
This prompt and the work it starts make up a turn.  The model may need several
responses and tool calls before it gives a final answer.  The prompt marker is
.B ›
while idle and
.B »
while active.
.PP
Text starting with
.B /
is handled as a command by snajpagent.  Type
.B /help
and press Enter to see the controls.
.B /verbose 1
shows compact tool activity alongside model text;
.B /verbose 2
adds previews of tool inputs and results.  These controls also work during a turn.
.SS Correct this task or queue the next one
Typing during work opens an unsent draft; it does not interrupt the model.
The rollout composer shows the steer marker once the turn starts, even before
the provider has accepted a request. Enter then supplies steering. For example,
"Use the existing parser; do not add a dependency" changes the current task.
The model's response is interrupted and continued from its delivered text.
An already-started command stays alive for the model to collect or stop.
.PP
The submitted line is shown immediately. The active prompt remains visible
while a request is prepared, sent, retried or continued; a submitted steer
before provider acceptance is handled at a safe request boundary. A foreground
slash command temporarily hides the prompt until that command finishes, and
snajpagent does not execute another app command meanwhile. Ctrl-C and exit
remain priority controls.
.PP
The active prompt is visible and editable throughout the turn, even when empty
or while a response is unready. Only a foreground slash command hides it until
completion. Enter submits a steer for admission at a safe turn boundary.
.PP
For a separate follow-up, type "Then add regression coverage" and press Tab
at the end while work is active.  That message waits in the queue until the
current turn ends.  Queued prompts run oldest first, one turn at a time in the
same session.  The rollout prompt's
.B (N)
counts waiting turns, excluding current work.
.PP
Use
.B /queue
to inspect waiting prompts,
.B /queue 2 edit
to revise the second one, or
.B /queue 2 delete
to remove it.  Editing keeps its place and holds queue draining while the
editor is open.  Enter saves the replacement; Ctrl-C abandons the edit.
.B /queue pop
removes the newest item;
.B /queue clear
removes all waiting items.  Current work continues.
.PP
Queue arming survives exit and resume.  Explicit cancellation, a terminal
failure, a new direct prompt or an open queue editor pauses it.  Idle queue
additions start paused.  Use
.B /next
to arm it: at idle the oldest item starts immediately; during active work it
starts after the current full turn.  The remaining entries drain in order.  Queued prompts take precedence over automatic goal continuation.  The acknowledgement
.B "queued (/next or /q c) ›"
marks a submitted follow-up.  At execution, the prompt appears again in the
transcript with the normal rollout prompt, current local timestamp and effective
model settings.  The timestamp reflects dispatch; stored input provenance keeps
the original receipt time.  Retries within that turn do not repeat its prompt.
An unfinished draft retains its text and original prompt timestamp.
.B /next
continues a paused queue;
.B /q c
clears waiting prompts without stopping current work.
See Provider failures for the new-input/retry handoff case.
.SS Commands while work is active
Enter slash commands in either view while a turn runs.  A setting that affects
later responses can still be changed now. Inspection and display commands
run when accepted; deferred controls acknowledge the boundary they await and
retain accepted intent for resume. A running foreground slash command owns the
prompt. Bytes typed during it wait and are interpreted only after its result.
In rollout, an ordinary active-turn submission is echoed before the engine
handoff, so it remains visible even if that handoff blocks the next composer.
The steering record becomes durable when the engine admits it; successful
admission does not print the text a second time. Slash commands and queued
controls keep their own acknowledgement timing.
Empty-draft Ctrl-C interrupts an active operation.
.PP
Queue editing keeps command input separate from replacement text.  Enter on
/status inspects the session, and /exit preserves it; use // for a literal
leading slash in replacement text.  Delete confirmation also accepts commands,
but only the displayed session prefix authorizes deletion.  Ctrl-C cancels the
confirmation; opening the queue editor cancels deletion and opens that editor.
The external configuration editor takes exclusive terminal input until it exits.
.SS Read history and its length
.B /history
replays up to one recent retained turn by default. Use /history 10 to request ten,
or /history 0 for the total turn count without reading the journal. Replay ends
with shown turns, completions among those shown, and the total session turn count.
Unfinished, failed and interrupted work is included. Automatic resume uses the
same replay and defaults to one turn. The scan covers at most 4 MiB plus one
atomic journal record. A partial oldest turn is explicitly marked; omitted input
is not rendered as user text. When the scan ends early, its continuation sequence
locates older records through the model's read_session_history tool. IRC references
outside the window may remain unresolved.
.PP
Final voice transcripts and voice-initiated action results in that window are
included, even when the session has no coding turns. Speaker labels distinguish
ASR input from generated replies; action rows follow tool verbosity settings.
Voice records do not increase the coding-turn totals. Replay displays retained
text and outcomes without activating the microphone, playing speech or executing
actions. /history 0 still reports only totals.
.PP
Conversation replay is separate from the session's prompt-entry history used by
Up, Ctrl-P and Ctrl-R.  Compaction reduces model context, not the full local
conversation log available to /history.
.SS Page a local file
.B /cat PATH
in the classic terminal opens a regular file with the configured pager and returns when
the pager exits.  For example,
.B /cat ~/notes.txt
opens a file under the home directory. Relative paths use the current working
directory; absolute paths and
.B ~/
paths also work.  Spaces in a path are literal; you may enclose the whole
path in single or double quotes.  Shell expansions in PATH are not evaluated.
The file is passed to the pager, without adding its contents to the session
conversation or sending them to a provider.  This command also works while a
turn is active.  It requires an interactive terminal and an enabled pager.
The default
.B pager = on
uses $PAGER, or less \-FX when $PAGER is unset and less is available.
If no pager is available, set $PAGER or choose a
.B pager
command in the configuration. A missing or non-regular file reports an error.
.PP
In a Vim workspace, /cat retains a private file snapshot and opens it as a report.
It uses the owner's working directory and the same path syntax. Pager settings
do not affect this display. Repeat /cat to capture a fresh version; existing
reports retain their earlier bytes even if the source changes or is deleted.
NORMAL-mode Ctrl-C can interrupt copying. A detected source change aborts the copy;
retry /cat to read it again. File contents remain outside model context.
Older owners may use the whole-terminal pager adapter for this command.
.SS Complete names with Tab
Command completion starts with a slash at the beginning of the draft.
Type
.B /sta
and press Tab to get
.B /status
followed by a space.  Press Enter to run the command.  Completion applies
while the cursor is in or at the end of that first command word, not in its
arguments.  Ordinary words do not trigger command completion.
.PP
In chat, begin a nickname word with
.B @
and press Tab while the cursor is in or at the end of that name.  If builder
is the only match,
.B @bu
becomes
.B @builder
followed by a space.  The nickname need not start the whole message:
"please ask @bu" also completes.  A unique completion adds or reuses a space
and leaves the cursor after it, ready for you to finish writing.
Channel completion uses that conversation's current members. A private query
offers its peer and your local identity. Matching follows the endpoint's case
rules. Departed members disappear; a stale membership supplies no candidates.
With conversation tabs open, an unmatched nickname lets Tab cycle views while
retaining the draft.
.PP
At the end of a finished message, outside a command or nickname being completed,
Tab queues a local follow-up while your model is active.  In chat this still
does not send to the room; Enter does that.  While idle, Tab inserts spaces
instead. With an empty draft, Tab cycles through rollout, connected rooms and
opened private queries. Once conversation tabs exist, chat Tab also cycles with a draft
present, after command/nickname completion. Shift-Tab cycles backwards from any
view with its draft intact. Each conversation retains its own draft.
.PP
Completion itself never submits, sends or queues.  Multiple matches expand
their common beginning; another consecutive Tab lists the choices.  No match
leaves the draft unchanged, not queued.  Full cursor and command exceptions
are listed under INTERACTIVE INPUT.
.SS Edit or stop
Ctrl-J inserts a newline.  A completed bracketed paste appears immediately and keeps pasted newlines in the draft
instead of submitting each line.  Up/Down move through wrapped or multiline
draft rows, then prompt history at the start or end of the text.  Ctrl-A/Ctrl-E
jump to the start/end of the whole prompt.  Ctrl-P/Ctrl-N go directly through
history; Ctrl-R searches it.  Ctrl-C clears a nonempty draft without interrupting the turn.  On an
empty draft it interrupts active work.  Ctrl-D on an empty draft exits.
.SS Continue toward a goal
A normal final answer ends the turn and returns control to you.  A goal asks
snajpagent to keep taking turns toward an explicit result:
.PP
.nf
/goal fix the parser, add tests, and validate the change
.fi
.PP
It continues until complete, paused, cancelled or blocked.  Waiting operator
prompts run before the next automatic goal turn.
.B /state goal status
shows its state and any blocker.
.B /state goal pause
pauses automatic continuation at a turn boundary;
.B /state goal resume
starts it again.  Pausing does not kill a command already running.
Ordinary errors retain the goal and recover automatically.  Policy stops and
provider refusals pause automatic goal continuation.
Explicit turn interruption pauses automatic goal work.
.SS Leave and resume a session
On Linux, interactive ANSI terminal sessions keep running when their terminal disconnects,
including when an SSH connection drops. Open a new SSH connection and attach to the same session.
Use
.B /s d
to return to the shell while requests, commands, IRC and goals continue.
Graceful exit and detach deliver pending terminal output before returning to the shell.
Detaching prints a shell-quoted --resume command with the full session ID and
any nondefault --dotdir. Run it to reattach with the retained session settings.
Reconnect with
.BR "snajpagent --attach SESSION_ID" ;
.B \-A
is the short option. Without an ID, select from the running-session list.
Name a new session with
.BR "snajpagent -N lead" ,
then reconnect with
.BR "snajpagent --attach -N lead" .
Use
.B /session name lead
inside an existing session to give it a name.
Only one terminal controls a session at a time. The session keeps its unsent
draft, selected view and destination; attachment displays bounded recent
history before restoring the composer.
Typing and terminal resizing preserve the draft and previously displayed conversation.
Buffered typing is displayed as a completed edit while resize controls remain responsive.
Resizing while terminal output is blocked retains all newly typed command bytes.
An unfinished terminal file transfer ends when its controlling terminal
disconnects; queued downloads retain their saved state.
Local voice, dictation and playback stop when that terminal detaches,
disconnects or suspends. Reconnecting does not restart capture.
.PP
New editors and pagers use the attached terminal's TERM and GNU Screen profile.
An already-running external program keeps its launch environment and receives
the terminal resize notification. Owner configuration, including color policy,
stays unchanged.
.PP
A session saves its name, current working directory, conversation, tool results,
model selection, queue and goal on disk. Normal exit prints a command to reopen
that exact session. You can also list sessions with
.B snajpagent -l
or reopen the newest session with
.BR "snajpagent --resume --last" .
Listing shows all attached sessions, then detached sessions, then sessions whose
attachment status is unavailable, then the 10 most recent stored sessions. Use
.B snajpagent -l 25
to include 25 stored sessions, or
.B snajpagent -l 0
for running sessions only. Each group shows the latest saved activity first.
Use
.B snajpagent --resume -N lead
to attach to a running session or resume a stored one by name.
.PP
Exit is not goal pause.  No work runs after exit, but a saved active goal
continues when resumed.  Use
.B /state goal pause
first if you want it to remain paused.  Other goal states remain unchanged.
.B /state
shows session state including the goal and its actions;
.B /goal
is an alias for
.BR /state " goal" .
When a turn leaves a paused or blocked goal with nothing pending, the
terminal prints an idle line naming the state. Blocked goals name their recorded
wait channel: operator, IRC endpoint/nick, timer, process handle, or external
dependency. Only an explicit operator wait names the operator. An armed timer
is reported separately; a wait label alone does not schedule one. Older blocked
records without a wait channel show it as unspecified.
Armed waiting prompts continue after recovered active work.  Paused prompts
need
.B /next
and cannot be bypassed by automatic goal work.  Resume restores recorded
context and accounts for lost command owners; old OS processes and handles
are not revived.
.PP
Resuming a stopped session retains its saved listener, clients, nicks and room.
IRC history includes local nickname changes, including when networking is
disabled on resume. An IRC history restore error identifies the journal sequence.
Saved connection and channel boundaries clear stale peer membership before
subsequent nickname changes are replayed.
Explicit startup options replace the corresponding setting before work continues.
For example, reopen a session with a hosted IRC server and no
outgoing client connections:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent --listen=localhost:6667 --no-client \e
  --resume SESSION_ID
.fi
.PP
Listen, client, nick and room overrides apply before resumed work starts;
resume options may appear before or after the session id, while
follow-up text still requires
.BR -- .
the live network state is shown locally and supplied to the model.
A fresh snapshot on interactive resume also records offline and client-only
state, replacing the previous process's topology as the latest network context.
A requested listener that cannot bind reports a startup error.
.PP
Direct input is recorded before preparation, credential acquisition or model
requests.  A preparation failure retains the pending text, accepted attachments,
read-only mode and selected provider/model/effort.  Resolve the reported dependency and use
.B /retry
or resume the session to continue.  Ctrl-C cancels pending input.  A later
prompt is queued behind retained input rather than replacing it. Attachment
replay uses the retained bytes and does not reread or reconvert the original files.
.PP
Same-turn recovery retains the original working-document paths.  The files
remain external dependencies: their contents are read when needed, and moved
or missing files require inspection.  New full turns discover their own entry
points.  Session records contain paths rather than automatic file snapshots.
.SS Silent SSH network loss
A network outage can leave the server's SSH connection and terminal frontend
alive until SSH detects the missing client. If attachment reports that a terminal
is still present after an outage, wait for the SSH timeout and reconnect.
Configure the SSH server's sshd_config with:
.PP
.nf
ClientAliveInterval 15
ClientAliveCountMax 3
.fi
.PP
Validate with sshd -t and reload the SSH service. New connections then release
unresponsive clients after approximately 45 seconds; responsive idle clients
remain connected. Existing connections keep their original liveness settings.
The detached native agent owner continues running. Client-side ServerAliveInterval
can detect a dead server, but server-side checks release the abandoned terminal.
.PP
Interactive --resume selects the live owner's attachment path, including by ID,
-N NAME, --last or the picker. A terminal still held by another connection is
reported as occupied; attachment does not displace it.
.SS Read this reference
Use
.B man snajpagent
for the installed reference.  From a checkout,
.B man -l ./snajpagent.1
opens the same source.  The hosted edition is
.IR https://agent.snajpa.net/manual.html .
The website and README introduce workflows; the sections below define all
options, commands, configuration, tools, files, limits, failures and security.
.SH "Native IRC"
.SS Host and join a room
IRC is the built-in chat protocol.  Each session has an operator nickname
(yours) and a model nickname.  In separate terminals, start a host and client:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent -s -n builder -o alice -r work
snajpagent -c -n reviewer -o bob
.fi
.PP
The first hosts room #work at localhost:6667; the second joins it on the same
machine.  The -n option names the model and -o names the operator.  This shares
conversation, not files, credentials or command processes.  Use separate Git
worktrees when agents should have independent checked-out files to edit.
.PP
Networked startup opens chat, the shared room conversation.  Enter sends as
your operator nick.  Address a model, for example "@builder check the failing
test", to start work or steer it at a safe boundary.  Ordinary conversation
is background context, not a new task.  Models publish chosen replies through
.B irc_send
while their other text and tool activity remain in rollout.
.PP
Empty Tab cycles through rollout, each room and opened private queries without stopping work.
Returning displays output
that arrived while the view was hidden.  To complete a nickname, type @bu and
Tab, then finish your message after the added space.  Enter sends it to the
selected conversation. With conversation tabs open, chat Tab switches tabs; otherwise
Tab at the end queues it locally while your own model is active.
.B /chat
and
.B /rollout
select either view directly.  Runtime
.B /server start
and
.B /connect
change network roles without restarting or switching the current view.
.SS Routing, identity and history
Listener mode creates one room.  Its default name is the hostname and its initial
topic is the server agent's absolute starting working directory.  snajpagent clients join
the advertised room automatically.
.PP
The local operator and ordinary IRC clients receive channel mode
.BR +o ;
the model nick does not.  Current channel operators may change the topic; on a
room this session hosts, the model link may set the topic as well.
Links declare their role at registration: the model link requests the
.BR snajpagent/agent
capability and sends the USER realname
.BR "snajpagent agent" ,
and the operator link sends
.BR "snajpagent operator" ;
the host derives the link role from either declaration.  Agents are never
opped; operator clients and the operator link are.
Only direct mentions of the model nick are urgent; ordinary messages from
local or channel operators remain background context.  A model whose exact
[model-limit] entry sets steering to
.B all
admits every room message as steering instead.
Urgent input is coalesced and admitted at the earliest safe boundary;
generation reaches a safe stream boundary and commands are handed back alive.
.PP
Other messages, joins, leaves, mode changes, and topic changes enter model
context when convenient and do not force a reply.  They wait for an active turn;
background traffic never admits a second input while one is live, so another
worker joining, being opped or leaving can never end or interrupt this session.
First join and successful
compaction inject bounded room history.  The runtime reconnects outgoing links automatically.
Pending room messages and notices retain their original journal event across
compaction and resume, including on plain IRC servers without catch-up stream IDs.
Later admission presents each saved input once.
Recovering a retained source uses a targeted, verified journal lookup so
request preparation does not replay the entire journal for that input.
Older admissions shifted by an inserted checkpoint can recover on resume when
the adjacent saved IRC event exactly matches the admission's recorded identity.
The journal remains unchanged; a missing or mismatched source still fails.
.PP
Implicit outgoing nicks form zero-based sequences:
.BR agent0 ,
.BR agent1 ,
and so on, with the same rule for the operator's default.  When an explicit
preferred nick is already present, the client handles the server's normal
.B 433
reply by appending
.BR 1 ,
.BR 2 ,
and so on until registration succeeds.  The accepted nick is retained across
reconnects.
.PP
The terminal starts in the timestamped
.B chat
view.  Model text and tools belong to the
.B rollout
view regardless of verbosity.  Chat input goes to the selected room as the
local operator.  Multiple destinations receive short source/prompt labels;
a hosted room counts once regardless of how many clients joined it.  After a
submission the composer returns once that turn has begun processing rather than
at once, so a ready-looking prompt never appears under a line nothing has acted
on yet; keys typed in that window are buffered and appear with the composer,
while an empty, slash-command or display-only submission still repaints
immediately.  A turn that never reports processing returns the composer after a
brief bounded delay (250 ms).
Explicit numbered sends and broadcasts also work from rollout, but ordinary
rollout input stays local:
while idle it starts a model turn, and during active work it steers that turn at
the next safe boundary.  It never emits an IRC message.  Admitted room traffic
is not echoed as submitted input either: the chat view shows the room events and
the durable trail records the admission.
Incoming room traffic is not automatically bridged between servers.
Model response text remains local;
.B irc_send
is the only model-authored path to a room message or notice.  If a
local-operator mention turn reaches a terminal response without a successful
.B irc_send
message, the model receives one reminder.  Notices do not satisfy that reply
requirement.  Other room traffic never forces a reply.
Each requirement stays with the input or turn that admitted it. Resume restores
those belonging to the current pending input or active turn.
External channel requests retain the agent's channel ID, connection generation
and membership at receipt time. Each request lists its outstanding reply targets.
A successful message to that exact membership satisfies the requirement;
messages in other channels and replacement memberships after rejoin or reconnect
leave it pending. An unavailable agent membership is shown as unavailable.
The model must explain that missing reply path instead of choosing another room.
Shared channels admit one copy of incoming traffic. Channels joined only by the
agent also reach model context.
.PP
The accepted per-server nick drives mentions, echo suppression, and model
instructions.  Live IRC nick changes retain op status and produce room
notifications.  Chat uses the selected destination's accepted operator nick.
Use
.B /nick
to inspect or change the operator nickname. Standard IRC clients can use
.BR NICK .
.PP
Nicks are at most 30 bytes and local model/operator preferences must differ
under IRC case folding.  Room names are at most 50 bytes excluding
.BR # .
The server implements registration, join/part, messages/notices, topic,
names/who, nick and mode changes, liveness, and bounded history, not the entire
IRC protocol. Clients negotiate supported capabilities before completing
registration and use the advertised room, or the explicitly configured room
on an external server. A connection without a selected room remains connected.
Each server-confirmed channel retains its own membership, topic and names.
PART and KICK remove membership in that channel; channel errors appear with their
target and preserve the connection. Reconnect rejoins channels still retained
by the client, excluding those explicitly left or kicked from. Session resume
also restores retained channel membership for each local identity; the server
must confirm the new join before that membership is active. Channel lifecycle
metadata stays outside model input and replayed channel history.
Numbered destinations continue to select the configured default room.
Oversized state summaries are labelled abbreviated; live membership remains
complete. Nickname matching and agent mentions follow the endpoint's advertised
CASEMAPPING. An unknown mapping uses exact spelling.
.PP
Registered external IRC clients can send private messages and notices to each
other through the built-in server, including after leaving its room. Delivery
goes to the named recipient and, when echo-message is negotiated, the sender.
Private bodies are excluded from the host session's room history and raw
transport traces. A missing recipient produces an error for PRIVMSG; NOTICE
failures produce no automatic reply. Direct messages to the session's operator
and agent nicknames form separate queries. Operator queries remain in operator
history and are excluded from provider input, IRC compaction and model-facing
history reads. Agent queries enter model context with their recipient and
conversation ID. A live private message requires a reply in that query; NOTICE,
historical messages and nickname changes add context without requiring a reply.
Pending reply obligations survive resume. Opened queries appear in the classic
Tab cycle, including queries addressed to the agent. Enter replies as the
operator in operator queries; agent queries are read-only. Incoming traffic
preserves the focused view. Each private query
keeps its draft while this process runs. Drafts and queued submissions retain
their original recipient across nickname changes; a stale send is rejected and
an entirely unsent draft is restored. Resume restores query tabs; use /history
for retained messages. Use /query NICK to open or select an operator query.
Vim private buffers and their controls are described under Private conversation windows.
.PP
The development query transport retains a separate send result for each chunk.
It records pending before transmission, written after the complete frame, and
acknowledged after local delivery, an ordered native-server echo, or an exact
labeled server response. External servers offering labeled-response and batch
receive a unique label for each chunk. ACK replies and matching echoes acknowledge
server handling; batched replies wait for the enclosing batch to finish, and
server errors produce failed results. An acknowledgement does not prove the peer
read the message. An external unlabeled write remains unconfirmed.
Model-facing outgoing history includes the delivery state. A written result
means the complete frame reached the socket; acknowledged records mean the
server or local recipient accepted it.
Nick changes, disconnection, loss of the required capabilities and endpoint
removal cancel unwritten chunks and mark started, unconfirmed sends uncertain.
Classic and Vim conversation views print the body once with a pending send ID,
followed by compact delivery-state rows for that ID. When the server changes the
text or action, its result includes an explicit server-text correction, retained
in history. Labels and action framing count toward the client's line budget;
UTF-8 characters remain intact across chunks.
Private queues are never replayed on reconnect. Model sends use the exact query
captured when their request started. A changed nickname or connection generation
rejects that send, with no channel fallback; inspect irc_state before choosing
a fresh target. Direct-message records use the new session/checkpoint format;
older binaries refuse to resume a session containing them.
.PP
The built-in IRC subset has no TLS or authentication.  A non-loopback listener
is suitable only for a trusted network or a separately secured tunnel.
.SH "Options"
.TP
.BR \-s " [" endpoint "]", " \-\-listen" [= endpoint ]
Host the built-in IRC server in this foreground process and select its
listener.  This does not detach.  A missing endpoint means
.BR localhost:6667 .
An address already in use is a startup error, visible without verbosity flags.
It does not try another address or increment the port after that collision.
.TP
.BR \-c " [" endpoint "]", " \-\-client" [= endpoint ]
Add an outgoing IRC connection.  Repeat for up to 16 distinct endpoints.  A
missing endpoint means
.BR localhost:6667 .
.TP
.BR \-\-no-listen , " \-\-no-client"
Independently suppress a configured listener or clear configured outgoing
connections.  Each conflicts with its positive startup flag; both negatives
may be used together.  Nick and room preferences remain usable while offline.
.TP
.BI \-n " nick, " \-\-model-nick " nick"
Set the model's preferred IRC nick.  Default:
.BR agent0 .
.TP
.BI \-o " nick, " \-\-operator-nick " nick"
Set the local operator's IRC nick.  The default is a valid
.B USER
value plus
.BR 0 ,
then
.BR operator0 ,
or
.B localop0
when needed to remain distinct from the model nick.
.TP
.BI \-r " room, " \-\-room-name " room"
Set the preferred room for current or future hosting.
A leading
.B #
is optional.  The default is derived from the hostname.
.TP
.BI \-d " directory"
Advertise an additional local working-document root.  Repeat for multiple roots.
Each directory must contain a regular file, or a symlink to a regular file, named
.B AGENTS.md
or
.BR AGENTS.override.md ;
the override takes precedence. Relative option paths resolve against the
shell directory used to launch snajpagent. Canonical duplicate paths are
collapsed. These roots are saved with the session; omitted roots on resume retain
them, while explicit -d options replace the saved set. They remain explicit pointers even
when automatic discovery is disabled.  They do not grant unrelated write
permission or synchronize documents between hosts.
.TP
.BI \-m " [provider/]model[/effort]"
Select the model for the next request on start or resume, including an unfinished
turn. With an omitted
provider, use the first provider defined in the configuration (or an explicit
.BR \-\-provider ).
With two components, a configured first component means
.IR provider/model ;
otherwise the components mean
.IR model/effort .
With an omitted effort, use the first effort listed for that model in the
local cache, then its cached default, then the configured reasoning effort.
.B \-\-effort
explicitly overrides the selector's effort.  Typed model IDs need not be cached.
A number, or \fB#\fR followed by a number, selects the entry at that position in the
numbered model listing, so a model copied from the listing selects what the
listing
showed; an index outside the catalogue is an error rather than a literal model
name.
Each component must be nonempty.  Wrap a component in matching single or
double quotes to keep embedded slashes, for upstream IDs such as OpenRouter's
vendor/model form:
.B openrouter/"openai/gpt-4.1-mini"
or
.BR openrouter/"openai/gpt-4.1-mini"/high .
Protect those quotes from the shell, for example
.BR \-m \(aqopenrouter/\(dqopenai/gpt-4.1-mini\(dq\(aq .
A quoted vendor/model designator still honours a configured provider prefix,
so
.B \(dqopenrouter/openai/gpt-4.1-mini\(dq
selects the same model.  A provider-local model name in the configuration
remains available for IDs used often.
The provider/model/effort selection becomes the session preference until changed.
It survives later turns and session resume, including when you exit before running
the next turn. An already active turn or admitted pending input retains its saved
selection through recovery; new turns use the selected preference. This option
does not write the configuration file; use
.B /model SELECTOR save
to make a selection the configured startup default.
.TP
.B \-\-update-model-cache
Refresh every configured provider's model catalog.  The refresh runs before
model selection and session startup, regardless of option order.  Combine with
.B \-l
for a noninteractive refresh without starting a turn.  A refresh failure stops
startup and leaves the previous cache intact.  Help and version exit without
refreshing.  Uses the same authenticated endpoints as
.BR "/model cache" .
.TP
.BI \-\-effort " level"
Set the session's reasoning effort for the next request, until changed. It
survives later turns and session resume; an already admitted request retains
its effort. It does not write configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-dotdir " directory"
Use an absolute private application directory instead of
.IR $HOME/.snajpagent .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read an absolute configuration path.  This does not move sessions or the model
cache.  Unlike the default path, an explicit file must exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-provider " name"
Select a configured provider. On resume this explicitly changes the saved
selection after local recovery, without rewriting past events. A missing name
is an error. Combine with -m MODEL when changing models too.
.TP
.BR \-v
Set verbosity to the number of flags. New sessions start at 0 without flags;
resume retains the saved level when flags are omitted.
More than six flags is an error.  The runtime command
.B /verbose N
sets the same process-local level.  See
.BR DISPLAY .
.TP
.BR \-\-color [= when ]
Set color to
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Bare
.B \-\-color
means
.BR always .
The value may follow a space or
.BR = .
.TP
.BR \-\-no-color
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-color=never .
.TP
.BR \-\-markdown
Render supported model Markdown on terminals.  This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-markdown
Show model Markdown literally.
.TP
.BR \-\-resume " [" session-id "]"
Interactively attach to a running native session, or resume a stopped durable
session. Live attachment retains the owner's configuration, model and draft;
startup overrides are not applied. A command-line follow-up is not submitted on
attachment; a notice asks you to enter it after reconnecting. One-shot
.B \-e
execution still requires a stopped session. Without an ID or
.B \-N NAME
or
.BR \-\-last ,
interactively select a saved session.
Omitted configuration path, documentation roots, color, Markdown, verbosity and
IRC options retain the session's saved values. Explicit options replace only
the corresponding setting; --no-listen and --no-client keep those roles disabled.
The exit hint supplies only the session selector and a nondefault --dotdir.
Default ~/.snajpagent is omitted, including when passed explicitly.
Config files are reread through their saved paths; credentials are not copied
into session settings. Older sessions recover IRC settings from their last
recorded topology; an unrecorded custom config path or documentation root must
be supplied once. Live attachment keeps the already-running owner's settings.
.PP
An ID is a unique prefix of 1 through 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters.
Displayed short IDs work in every session ID selector, including both pickers
and /session attach. Ambiguous prefixes list matching full IDs.
The writer lock prevents a second owner.
.TP
.BR \-A , " \-\-attach [" session-id "]"
Connect a terminal to a live native session owner on Linux. Without an ID,
or
.BR "\-N NAME" ,
select a running session. Accepts a unique 1..32-character ID prefix and
.BR \-\-dotdir ;
the owner retains its existing configuration and model selection. Ambiguous
prefixes list full matching IDs. An occupied terminal is never taken over.
Attachment skips provider setup, including when the owner started with an
explicit configuration file outside its state directory.
Requires terminal stdin, stdout and stderr, with a nonempty TERM other than
.BR dumb .
TERM and STY each accept at most 255 bytes. Unsupported replacement terminals
are refused before activation. Ordinary startup with an empty or dumb TERM,
or a known width below 20 columns, retains direct-terminal operation, as do
other hosts until their native backend is available. An existing native owner
keeps its control keys when a terminal becomes narrower, including after an
editor or pager returns.
.TP
.BR \-N , " \-\-name NAME"
Give a new session a saved name, including an empty interactive session.
With --attach or --resume, select an existing session by its exact,
case-sensitive name. Choose one selector: an ID, a name, or --last.
Names use nonempty UTF-8 without control characters, within the 16 KiB CLI
argument bound. Quote names containing spaces in the shell.
Names are labels independent of the IRC model nick set by -n.
Duplicate names are allowed; ambiguous selection prints matching full IDs
and requires an ID to choose one.
Use /session name NAME in an existing session to set or replace its name.
.TP
.BR \-\-last
Resume the most recently used saved session.  Requires
.BR \-\-resume .
.TP
.BR \-e
Execute one turn and exit.  The prompt must follow
.B \-\-
or arrive on non-terminal standard input.  Network options are not accepted.
.TP
.BR \-l " [N]"
List attached sessions, detached sessions, running sessions with unavailable
attachment status, then up to N stored sessions and exit. N defaults to 10 and
must be a nonnegative integer; zero shows running sessions only. Put N immediately after -l, before other options.
Each status group sorts by latest saved activity, newest first; equal timestamps
sort by session ID descending. The count applies to stored sessions only.
Resume pickers and /session list include all saved sessions.
Only
.BR \-\-dotdir ,
.BR \-\-config ,
color options, Markdown presentation options, and
.B \-\-update-model-cache
may accompany it.  Markdown
presentation has no useful effect on session-list output. The table has headings
for session ID, name, model, turn count, status, last prompt and IRC.
The last two columns share the remaining terminal width equally. Long cells end
with an ellipsis; session IDs stay intact and expand beyond eight characters
when needed to distinguish sessions. Widths account for wide Unicode characters. Redirected output
is tab-separated with previews of up to 80 display columns per cell.
Whitespace and terminal controls become spaces; saved text retains its full contents.
IRC lists comma-separated entries: s/NICK@ENDPOINT for the hosted server first,
then c/NICK@ENDPOINT for each outgoing client. NICK is the agent nick recorded
for that endpoint; older snapshots fall back to the recorded session nick.
Later recorded nick changes update the displayed identity, including sessions
whose owner retained an older nickname snapshot.
An unknown nick leaves only s/ENDPOINT or c/ENDPOINT. A dash means no recorded
endpoints. Stored sessions show their last recorded topology and nicks. An IRC prompt shows
ENDPOINT: MESSAGE, resolving saved event references to the latest message
in that prompt. Ordinary prompts show the latest saved user text.
An unnamed session shows a dash in the name column.
The STATUS column shows attached while a terminal client is connected,
detached while the session runs without one, running when the owner is live but
its attachment status is unavailable, and stored when only saved history remains.
A suspended terminal retains its attachment. A connection still reserving or
repainting its terminal becomes attached after activation. Listing queries the
owner without reserving a terminal or changing saved history. Status queries
support owners started before a binary upgrade and share a one-second deadline
per owner. A failed query appears as running; retry the list after the owner
responds again.
Use --attach to reconnect to a running native session. --resume attaches if
running and starts the session if stored.
.TP
.BR \-h
Print short help and exit.  It must stand alone.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Open the installed
.BR snajpagent (1)
manual with
.B man
where available.  If the viewer or manual is unavailable, print short help.
On Windows, print short help.  It must stand alone.
.TP
.BR \-V
Print build identity and exit.  It must stand alone.
.PP
An endpoint is
.IR host ,
.IR host:port ,
.IR [IPv6] ,
or
.IR [IPv6]:port .
The default port is 6667.  In networked mode initial chat text must follow
.BR \-\- .
.SH "Terminal"
.SS Editing keys
The prompt word-wraps while typing, moving fitting words intact to the next row
and hard-wrapping words wider than the terminal.  Wrapping preserves the submitted
text.  Long drafts scroll within the editor to keep the cursor visible.
Refreshing prompt history preserves the cursor when the recalled entry is unchanged.
.TP
.B Enter
An empty or whitespace-only draft leaves its prompt line in the transcript and
opens a fresh prompt.  This is local: it starts no model request, sends no chat,
adds no history or queued turn, and leaves active work and goals unchanged.
Queue editing and confirmation prompts retain their own blank-answer behavior.
For nonempty text, submit while idle.  In rollout during model generation,
interrupt the response and steer from its delivered prefix.  A running command is handed back alive;
it is not killed. In operator chat, send to the selected room or private peer.
Agent queries are read-only. A model-nick mention
steers at the next safe response/tool boundary without truncating generation.
.TP
.B Tab
With an empty draft, cycle rollout, connected rooms and opened private queries,
including offline.
Otherwise, completion requires one of these prefixes and cursor positions:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A draft beginning with
.B /
(not
.BR // ),
with the cursor in or at the end of its first word: complete a command name or
numeric room destination.  For example, /statu becomes /status followed by a space.
.IP \(bu 2
In chat, a nickname word beginning with
.B @
at the cursor: complete a current room member.  The word can occur later in a
sentence.  This also works in the text of explicit /N TEXT and /all TEXT sends,
including from rollout.  The cursor may be inside the nickname or immediately
after it, but not past its following space.
.RE
.IP
A unique completion adds a trailing space (or reuses an existing one) and moves
the cursor after it.  Multiple matches expand their common prefix; a second
consecutive Tab lists choices while preserving the draft and cursor.  No match
leaves the draft unchanged.  Completion never submits, sends or queues text.
.IP
Outside a completion, chat Tab cycles with its draft intact once conversation
tabs exist. Otherwise, Tab queues a later local turn while active, or inserts
spaces while idle. In rollout, Tab at the end of a finished ordinary message
queues it; Tab at the end of /statu or @bu still completes that word first.
Queueing remains local even in chat.  Use Enter to send to the room or run a
slash command.  Commands are not generally queueable: /ro QUERY is a read-only
future turn and /verbose applies immediately.  In the queue editor, a queue
action saves the replacement instead of adding a new item; completion still
applies and empty text is rejected.  Enter on a slash command runs that command
and retains the original queue item.  Use // for a literal leading slash or
/ro QUERY to replace the item with a read-only query.
.TP
.B Shift-Tab
Cycle backwards through rollout, connected rooms and opened private queries,
preserving the draft. This also works with nonempty rollout input.
.TP
.B Ctrl-J
Insert a newline without submitting.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Leave the visible draft in scrollback, append literal
.BR ^C ,
start a new line, and open a clean prompt.  With an empty active composer, also
interrupt the turn and pause automatic goal continuation. While the engine settles
the interruption, the prompt shows "Cancellation requested; waiting...".
Further empty-draft interrupts keep that pending prompt on the same line.
Cancelling a queue edit restores the active prompt and leaves the turn interruptible.
Context preparation checks cancellation between journal events. The next idle prompt
reflects the settled goal state; its active goal flag is cleared before the pause
notice redraws it. Empty Enter afterwards stays local and leaves the goal paused.
Ordinary queued IRC updates remain pending across this boundary and session
resume; only a direct mention remains eligible for urgent automatic admission.
Five consecutive presses within two seconds exit, including when terminal
output is stalled; other input resets the sequence.  A transient empty read
from an open raw terminal does not end input; an actual terminal hangup or
end-of-file still does.  Ctrl-D on an empty draft or
.B /exit
while idle also exits.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D
Delete at the cursor when text remains.  On an empty draft, interrupt active
work and exit without waiting for the model or command to finish.
.TP
.BR Up , " Down"
Move within the displayed rows of a wrapped or multiline draft, preserving the
preferred terminal column, including in recalled prompts.  At the first or last
row, move to the start or end of the text.  Up at the start selects one older
prompt; Down at the end selects one newer prompt. History visits the session's
entries newest first, then the global archive newest first. Returning to the
newest local boundary restores the unsent draft.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-P , " Ctrl-N"
Move directly through prompt history, including from inside a multiline draft.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-Left , " Ctrl-Right" , " Option-Left" , " Option-Right"
Move to the previous word start or next word end.  Words are separated by spaces,
tabs or newlines, as with Ctrl-W.  Common PC control arrows, Mac option arrows
and Meta-b/Meta-f sequences are accepted, including Escape-prefixed and
application-cursor arrows; the terminal must transmit them.
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
Start case-sensitive incremental reverse search over local session history,
then the global archive. Press it again for the next match in that order;
search does not wrap. Searches scan files in bounded chunks so editing and
cancellation remain responsive even with a large archive. A navigation or search
keeps fixed file boundaries; starting another one can see entries merged by
programs that exited meanwhile.
.TP
.B Ctrl-G
Abort reverse search and restore the exact original draft and cursor.  Escape
accepts the current match without submitting it; editing and movement keys
accept it before applying their normal action.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-A , " Home"
Move to the start of the whole prompt, across wrapped rows and explicit newlines.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-E , " End"
Move to the end of the whole prompt.  Ctrl-A/Ctrl-E use the Control key on both
PC and Mac keyboards.
.TP
.BR Left , " Right" , " Delete" , " Backspace"
Edit the UTF-8 draft.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Clear the draft.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the preceding word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Redraw the current composer, preserving completed output and scrollback.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Z
Suspend after restoring normal terminal state; redraw after continuation.
.PP
During output, editing opens a live composer without stopping the provider.
Visible output resumes after
.B typing_pause_ms
of inactivity.  Bracketed paste inserts pasted newlines without submitting.
.PP
Direct prompts accept up to 1 MiB of valid UTF-8; steering and queued turns
accept up to 256 KiB.  The queue holds pending turns within 16 MiB; pending
steering text is bounded to 8 MiB in a turn.  Interactive EOF interrupts
active work and exits, preserving the saved goal and queue arming state.
The session remains resumable.
.SS Views and prompt
Startup without network roles opens rollout; startup with roles opens chat. Empty Tab,
.BR /chat ,
and
.B /rollout
switch views at idle or during work, including offline.  Runtime networking
changes preserve view, draft, history and verbosity.  Offline chat retains its
transcript; Enter rejects unsendable input and restores the draft, without
starting a private turn. Hidden-view output is buffered for display on return.
Both views append to terminal scrollback;
neither is a full-screen TUI.
.PP
Additional operator channels and private queries join the Tab cycle after the
endpoint default rooms. Shift-Tab moves
backwards and preserves a nonempty draft. In chat, Tab with a nonempty draft
also cycles once conversation tabs exist; command and nickname completion
still take precedence. With one operator channel and no query, the chat prompt
uses the plain operator name. Multiple channels or queries add a destination
label; agent conversations always show their read-only identity. Classic prompts
omit unread counts. Tab selection never sends or queues its draft.
Rollout, each channel and each query keep separate process-local drafts.
.PP
All default prompts share an activity slot, a goal slot, a space, the local
HH:MM:SS timestamp, and another space, in that order.
The rollout prompt continues with provider/model/effort, a space, the context
percentage space-padded to four columns including its percent sign, an optional
pending-queue count (N), and its idle or
active marker:
.B ›
while idle and
.B »
while active.  The percentage uses the latest comparable provider-reported
input count and resolved input budget, rounded up.  A full-request server count
or completed response input usage supplies this value; local byte/token
estimates are never used.  It describes the last measured request, not cumulative
session usage, generated output, or text/tool results added since that request.
A fresh session with no submitted turns shows 0%.
Unknown counts or budgets after a turn, including an incomparable state, show
.BR ?% .
Without exact preflight, the value stays unchanged while another request runs.
A provider/source/model/effort change or compaction invalidates incompatible
measurements.  Actual reported usage can decrease without local compaction.
.PP
The default chat prompt is
.IR "   HH:MM:SS " OPERATOR_NICK@MACHINE_HOSTNAME " : "
when idle.  Its first two spaces reserve the indicator slots; one literal
space separates them from the clock in every activity state.
Submitted command lines remain in scrollback with their output below them.
This applies in both views, during active work and at idle, including invalid
commands.  Each line retains the prompt label visible at submission.
Every nonblank prompt, command, chat message, steering instruction or queued
submission is painted at this submission boundary, before engine admission can
wait.  The ready composer stays hidden until the engine acknowledges the action,
while later input remains live as retained type-ahead and appears in the next
real composer.  Empty or whitespace-only ordinary Enter is presentation-only:
it creates no turn, command, queue item, chat message, goal event or provider
request.  Modal confirmations and queue editing keep their documented blank
answer behavior.
.PP
The timestamp is local time captured once per composer.  Editing, view changes,
and status redraws retain it; submission or Ctrl-C cancellation ends that capture.
The goal flag is independent of the activity slot.  Tool calls take priority
over model/provider work in the same activity column; idle reserves one space.
The provider indicator runs during response, token-count, compaction and model-list
operations, including their transport waits. A retained turn or retry delay alone
does not animate it. The active marker still identifies an unfinished, cancellable
turn, including one parked after a policy stop. Empty-draft Ctrl-C cancels that
turn; Ctrl-C with a draft clears the draft first.
Unused context digits also remain spaces. Indicators stop when their work unwinds;
there is no separate interruption glyph. At verbosity 1 and higher, terminals
also print one-shot wait lines naming the wait and its bound: managed commands
report the tool and wait cap, provider responses report the model. Verbosity 0
and one-shot execution omit these status lines. Wait and warning notices are
separated from following model output by one empty row, including literal
rendering with Markdown disabled. The template and spinners are configurable under
.BR [ui] .
.PP
Startup/resume headers, history headings and counts, and installed-update notices
wrap at word boundaries using terminal display-cell widths.  A word, path or URL
wider than the terminal can still hard-wrap; information is retained rather than
truncated.  Banner output preserves an existing draft and does not change saved
conversation text.  Redirected diagnostics keep their original bytes.
.SS Verbosity
At level 1, tool rows include rejected and otherwise unexecuted attempts with
a short status reason. These private rollout rows appear live and in history;
full arguments and output stay at their existing higher detail levels.
Provider-hosted web searches appear as those same private rows: the search
action and query, then the provider's terminal status and retained sources.
They stay display evidence executed by the provider, never a local call, and
a resumed session replays them.
.PP
Each model request includes a snapshot of the local verbosity, current
rollout/chat view and effective tool-detail visibility, derived from the same
presentation policy as the UI. The snapshot also identifies the terminal
output cap and one-shot stdout/stderr routing. It describes available detail,
not proof that the operator read every row or an untruncated result.
.PP
When tools are hidden, the model is guided to provide concise commentary before
meaningful work and at progress/outcome checkpoints. Brief rows or previews need
explanations of their significance; fuller traces need less duplicate per-call
narration. Important decisions, blockers, risks and final outcomes remain part of
the conversation at every level. This changes progress communication, not task
scope, diligence, permissions, requested answer length or private reasoning rules.
.PP
A new request snapshot reflects live display changes, including changes made
during a tool wait. Already-built or in-flight requests remain unchanged;
display changes alone neither interrupt the model nor start a new request.
Resume uses the newly running process's settings. The current snapshot supersedes
old display hints in summaries. Chat view hides private rollout regardless of
verbosity; selecting it never authorizes publishing local details to IRC or
changing destinations.
.PP
Verbosity controls local rollout and diagnostics, not chat-message visibility
or which view is selected.  All participants see the same room messages and
retained history at every level, including their own sends.
Each message carries up to 4096 UTF-8 bytes (at least 1024 Unicode code points).
Longer text splits at word boundaries when possible.  The extended IRC wire
limit is 8192 bytes including CRLF, advertised as LINELEN=8192;
peers must support that extension for long messages.
.TP
.B 0
Model text in the rollout; timestamped room traffic in chat.  Rollout milestones
include the colored bullet lines
.BR "• Compacted" ,
.BR "• Goal set" ,
and
.BR "• Goal cleared" ,
as well as updates, pause/resume, blocked and wording-lock changes.  An automatic
pause retains its reason in that single notice.  Consecutive bullet notices form
a group with one empty row around it, and none between members.
Errors and local-command results appear in either view.
.TP
.B 1
Compact tool start and outcome rows, each carrying the same short call reference
in the same column, with a short generic argument prefix.  No output body.
.TP
.B 2
Add previews of up to 1024 argument and 512 output characters per invocation.
Count terminal-safe Unicode code points; a dim
.BR "[…]"
marks display content that was cut or hidden by the current level.
.TP
.B 3
Full retained tool arguments, effective execution context and results, subject
to existing capture limits and
.BR "[tool] max_output_bytes" .
.TP
.B 4
Add runtime decisions, token accounting, synced event identities and connection
diagnostics.  Session identity and explicit command replies are always complete.
.TP
.B 5
Add sanitized provider protocol and IRC commands.  This exposes prompts,
source, tool output, and model content.
.TP
.B 6
Add transport diagnostics.
.PP
Levels 4 through 6 are live diagnostics in visible rollout; they are not queued
behind chat.  Unseen retained work uses the current level on entry to rollout.
Increasing the level does not replay visited history; lowering it stops now-hidden
detail between bounded slices.  A dim
.BR "[…]"
marks display content that was shown and then cut or hidden by the current
level; it is display-only, and complete command output remains in the durable
session journal.  Redaction of known credentials is not a guarantee
that source, prompts or tool output contain no secrets.
.SS Presentation
.PP
Terminal model text uses color and compact Markdown presentation when enabled.
Headings, lists, quotes, fenced code, pipe tables, inline code, emphasis,
strikethrough, and links are supported. Pipe tables retain aligned columns and
wrap long cells at word boundaries within the terminal width. Short columns keep
their natural width; long columns share the remaining space while preserving
whole-word minima. Wrapped grids separate logical records with horizontal rules.
When those minima cannot fit, the table becomes labeled vertical rows, with a
left border on every continuation line. Inline styles, header emphasis and
column alignment remain active across wrapped lines. Redirected and durable text
retains the model's exact bytes.
.PP
Some providers wrap web-search references in private-use citation blocks.  On a
terminal each recognized block is painted as one compact reference listing its
distinct integer turns in ascending order, with consecutive turns coalesced
into ranges, for example
.BR "[cite: turn 0-2]"
or
.BR "[cite: turn 1, 4]" .
The page index within a turn is omitted and a repeated turn appears once.  The
rule is independent of Markdown.  Unknown, malformed, oversized or
unterminated blocks are painted unchanged, and redirected one-shot output,
provider traffic and durable events keep the original bytes.
Citation fragments remain buffered across streaming deliveries, including when
the closing marker arrives on its own.
.PP
Plain-text fences labelled text, txt or plaintext omit the language label.
Other fence labels remain visible.  Indentation and Markdown-looking content
inside a fence are displayed literally, including pipe tables.
.PP
Top-level prose paragraphs are bulleted, with one empty row above and below,
even while a paragraph is streaming.  Adjacent paragraphs share that row;
headings, lists, code, tables and prompts use the same paragraph boundary.
All terminals wrap streamed model prose explicitly at whole-word boundaries.
The renderer holds an unfinished word until whitespace or the end of an item,
keeping attached punctuation together across provider chunks and Markdown
style changes. Words that fit a row wrap as a unit; longer tokens stream in
bounded pieces. Intervening messages flush pending text in order.
The editable draft also wraps whole words when they fit a terminal row; longer
words use terminal wrapping.  These displayed wraps do not insert submitted
newlines.  Explicit newlines and leading whitespace remain part of the draft.
Any prompt after non-prompt content has one empty row above it.  Consecutive
submitted prompts may be adjacent.
.PP
Provider-supplied and renderer-generated prose continuation lines align two
spaces below the paragraph text. These presentation newlines and spaces can
appear in terminal copies, but are not part of stored model text.
Code-block whitespace and the provider's original text are unchanged.  The live prompt is displayed
immediately on cursor-capable terminals, without waiting for a quiet period.
Before new output it is erased
and then redrawn with the unsent draft intact; work-in-progress prompts are not
left in scrollback.  Submitted input remains in the transcript.
.PP
Inside GNU Screen, a terminal resize can make Screen redraw recently visible
lines.  An outer terminal that saves those redraws in its own scrollback may
show repeated completed lines even when snajpagent did not send them again.
Screen can also redisplay after recovering from blocked terminal output.
Its full-screen erase before redisplay can cause an outer terminal to save the
old viewport into history before receiving the replacement viewport.
The outer terminal's alternate-screen and status-region scrollback behavior
can affect whether such redraws enter its saved history.  This is separate from
the session's durable transcript and from Screen copy mode.
.PP
Timestamped IRC chat messages do not receive the synthetic paragraph bullet;
genuine Markdown list items retain their structural bullets.
.PP
Ordinary chat operator nicks use cyan and model nicks blue.  A message
mentioning this viewer's accepted room-local operator or model nick highlights
its timestamp, sender nick and message separator in bold magenta.  A sender
mentioning only its own nick does not trigger highlighting.  The message body
and its Markdown styles are unchanged.  This display rule
applies to live and retained chat; it does not change mention admission or
turn history into fresh instructions.  Color-disabled output has no escapes.
.PP
.SH "Terminal file transfers"
.SS "Remote terminal mode (snajpagent remote)"
On the POSIX workstation, wrap the connection with the native client:
.PP
.nf
    snajpagent remote ssh -t target snajpagent
    snajpagent remote mosh target snajpagent
.fi
.PP
The workstation wrapper owns file selection and the download destination.
Use /session detach in the agent to return to the shell while work continues.
Reconnect to the running agent through the wrapper:
.PP
.nf
    snajpagent remote ssh -t target snajpagent --attach SESSION_ID
    snajpagent remote mosh target snajpagent --attach SESSION_ID
.fi
.PP
Omit SESSION_ID to choose from running sessions.
Keep the workstation wrapper outside SSH when reconnecting so file transfers
use the current workstation terminal.
.PP
The syntax is
.B "snajpagent remote [--config PATH] [--dotdir PATH] [--] [COMMAND ARG...]".
Arguments after the client options are passed literally to a child PTY;
shell expressions require an explicit shell command. With no command, the
client starts SHELL, or /bin/sh when SHELL is unset. Resize, terminal signals
and the child's exit status propagate through the wrapper. When the child exits,
the wrapper drains ready output and restores the terminal, including when a
background descendant retains the child terminal. Ordinary agent
options and prompts retain their existing meaning outside this subcommand.
.PP
For a directly wrapped mosh command, the client inserts an option separator
before the hostname so application options such as --resume reach the remote
program. Put Mosh options before the hostname. Full option names, short options
and their values are recognized; abbreviated or unfamiliar options retain Mosh's
own parsing and require an explicit -- before the hostname. An existing
separator is preserved. Argument text remains literal.
For a direct snajpagent vm remote command, Mosh prediction defaults to never:
the workspace echoes typing within its pane boundaries. Explicit --predict,
-a, -n or MOSH_PREDICTION_DISPLAY settings take precedence. For a workspace
behind a remote shell, Screen or tmux, use mosh --predict=never explicitly.
This uses confirmed remote rendering, so keyboard echo includes network latency.
.PP
Client mode reads only the workstation configuration's terminal section. It
starts no agent, provider, tools, IRC connection, media preparation or session
journal. Configuration lookup uses --config, then --dotdir/config.ini,
then ~/.snajpagent/config.ini. An explicit --config path must be absolute.
Client mode does not use SNAJPAGENT_DOTDIR.
A missing default file uses
client defaults. Downloads default to ~/Downloads; override them on the
workstation, for example:
.PP
.nf
    [terminal]
    download_dir = ~/Incoming files
    clipboard = native
.fi
.PP
Clipboard policy is native (the default), osc52 or off. Native publication uses
macOS plain-text pasteboard access, wl-copy in a Wayland session or xclip in an
X11 session. osc52 explicitly sends a terminal clipboard sequence and reports
its acceptance as unconfirmed. off disables clipboard requests at this wrapper.
Only the outer wrapper publishes when remote wrappers are nested; its policy
is authoritative. Each clipboard operation requires an explicit Vim yank.
The remote host remains trusted to originate these requests; the protocol
has no clipboard-read operation.
.PP
The download directory accepts an absolute path or one beginning with ~/. The client creates
the final directory if its parent exists, resolves it before receiving, and
preserves existing files through collision-safe names. Actual landed paths
appear in download receipts in the remote conversation, with the prompt and
editable draft restored afterward. Receipt paths also enter the saved tool
result for model-triggered sends.
Transfers keep the conversation visible. The native wrapper displays a single
progress line with direction, byte count and percentage, then restores the prompt.
Byte progress reaches 100% before final verification; the receipt confirms completion.
.PP
The wrapper negotiates a fast byte-stream client over SSH. Stock Mosh carries
terminal screen state instead of a byte stream: with the same outer wrapper,
the agent negotiates a short title-state frame and keyboard acknowledgement
channel. File bytes, checksums and receipts use the existing terminal transfer
protocol over that channel. The wrapper consumes the title frames before
forwarding terminal output, so protocol data stays out of the conversation,
draft and scrollback. Mosh transfers use smaller blocks and take longer than
the SSH route; they use the current Mosh session, with no separate file
connection. The executable name, path and TERM value do not select a mode.
.PP
Keep the outer workstation wrapper active across SSH hops and screen
reattachments. An inner native wrapper probes for an upstream native client
and relays to it, preserving workstation endpoint ownership. A local screen
can contain the workstation wrapper. For two nested screen backends, place
an inner native wrapper between them to relay passthrough to the outer client.
GNU screen passthrough keeps transfer
frames out of its display and scrollback; its settings remain unchanged.
.SS "Files through tmux"
Keep the workstation wrapper outside the connection and start or attach tmux
on the remote host:
.PP
.nf
    snajpagent remote ssh -t target tmux new-session snajpagent
    snajpagent remote mosh target tmux attach
.fi
.PP
Focus the agent pane and keep one writable tmux client viewing it for the
transfer. Other panes may continue producing output. The native wrapper supports
/receive, /send and model send_file through tmux without changing its options.
SSH also supports dropping a local file into the composer; on Mosh use /receive.
A workstation tmux pane may contain the outer wrapper too. Place the wrapper
at the workstation boundary; nested remote wrappers inside tmux are unqualified.
.PP
The agent uses tmux's client terminal for checked transfer frames, preserving
pane history and normal rendering. Byte-stream connections negotiate larger
frames; Mosh retains short checked title updates. Multiple writable clients
viewing the agent pane make the destination ambiguous: detach the extra clients
and retry. Detached panes and control-mode clients cannot receive files.
Keep the pane focused until the transfer finishes. Cancellation removes the
native client's unpublished temporary file and restores input.
.PP
Use compatible updated binaries at both ends. Already-running session owners
keep their loaded executable; exit and resume the agent normally to load
updated in-session transport and paste handling. Native attachment metadata
now includes the tmux server and pane; earlier attachment wire drafts require
an owner restart before a new frontend can attach.
.SS "Alternative workstation client: trzsz-go"
The Go trzsz wrapper remains usable with the target's /receive and /send
commands. On macOS:
.PP
.nf
    brew install trzsz-go
    trzsz --dragfile ssh target
.fi
.PP
If Homebrew's Python trzsz package is already installed, run
.B "brew uninstall trzsz"
first: both packages provide trz and tsz. The trzsz-go package also supplies
the trzsz wrapper; --dragfile belongs to that wrapper. Set
DefaultDownloadPath in the workstation's ~/.trzsz.conf for automatic saving.
The target needs no trz or tsz executable. Native-client availability and
automatic outbox delivery use the native wrapper; with trzsz-go, explicitly
request /send PATH and remove any corresponding pending export afterward.
.SS Receive from the workstation
Type
.B /receive
for native selection, then enter one regular local file path; an empty path,
Escape or Ctrl-C cancels. With current native wrappers and receivers, drop one
regular workstation file into the composer, or paste its absolute path.
Literal, shell-quoted and bracketed-paste paths are accepted without shell
expansion. The file becomes an unsent attachment; existing draft text and active
work are preserved. Ordinary text, missing paths and input to other programs
pass through unchanged. Update both endpoints for native drops; use /receive
with older receivers. Drop files one at a time.
.PP
If the transport process exits while the native file picker is open, the wrapper
closes the picker and restores the local terminal. Reconnect the tunnel to continue
using the remote session. Existing unsent attachments remain with their owner.
.PP
With trzsz-go, select files or drop regular files
into the composer. A stock Go-client drag sends Ctrl-C followed by trz;
that Ctrl-C can cancel a typed draft or interrupt active work. Save needed draft
text first. Directory mode (trz -d), directories and zero-byte uploads are rejected.
.PP
The receiver verifies the complete batch before retaining and listing files as
unsent attachments. Review
.B /attachments
and submit them with the intended private prompt, queued turn or steer.
Receiving a file alone submits no message. Preparation failure removes new files
from that batch and retains existing unsent attachments. A batch has at most eight
files; retained files are at most 256 MiB each, and prepared image content uses
the existing 12 MiB input budget.
Native uploads through tmux negotiate 8 KiB blocks on byte-stream connections.
Other native uploads use acknowledged blocks of at most 1 KiB to preserve input
through GNU screen, including screen boundaries hidden behind a relay. The
Mosh title-state mode limits blocks to 256 bytes, then divides each wire line
into short checked title updates. A stale or repeated update cannot append
the same bytes twice.
.SS Send to the workstation
Type
.B /send PATH
or ask the model to send a file through its
.B send_file
tool. For example:
.PP
.nf
    /send ./report.md
    /send /home/user/report with spaces.pdf
    /send asset:0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
.fi
.PP
Paths are literal, absolute or relative to the session's current working
directory. Symlinks and directories are rejected. Empty regular files can be
downloaded. An asset:ID must identify a file already accepted by this saved
session; its retained bytes are verified before sending. Downloads stream in
bounded blocks and do not inherit attachment-size limits.
.PP
The client receives the basename. Retained assets without a saved display name
use their asset ID as the basename. Completion requires the client's digest
acknowledgement and final EXIT. Native receipts identify actual landed paths;
file bytes stay outside model tool output. Transfers intentionally send the
selected file bytes to the workstation without tool-output redaction.
.SS "Pending downloads and reattachment"
The model's send_file journals an export in the existing remote session before
attempting delivery. An interactive session probes for the fast client, then
tries a screen-state handshake when the fast probe has no reply. A positive
reply permits immediate delivery through the selected mode. One-shot runs
and detached sessions report \(lqqueued; not delivered yet\(rq and emit no
file-transfer frames. An unsupported interactive client leaves the export
pending and reports queued intent; no file data starts before the
screen-state reply.
.PP
Wrapped reattachment over the fast byte-stream route discovers the client
and delivers pending files at an idle render boundary. A Mosh reattachment
keeps previously queued exports pending; request their delivery again from
the active session. Unwrapped attachment leaves exports pending. Only
acknowledged completion removes an export. Interrupted or uncertain transfers
remain pending, so a reconnect may produce a collision-safe duplicate if a
prior client landed the file but its completion acknowledgement was lost.
.PP
Each export has a stable ID, source path, byte count, timestamp and SHA256.
A changed or unavailable source remains pending and reports an error rather
than exporting different bytes. Ask the model to inspect its download_queue
with action=list, remove one ID with action=remove, or use action=clear.
An optional reason records why an export was cancelled. Queue removal changes
only export intent; source and completed workstation files remain intact.
Read-only mode permits inspection and rejects send_file and queue mutations.
Exports have no automatic age expiry.
.SS Cancellation and recovery
Transfers take exclusive terminal input until completion, cancellation or error.
Cancel the workstation selector or press Ctrl-C during the transfer. Ordinary
commands and steering are unavailable while the transfer owns input. Per-frame
I/O waits are bounded at 20 seconds; a missing wrapper returns a transfer error.
Transfers through GNU screen use 1 KiB DATA blocks to keep input frames within
its terminal input burst capacity; Mosh screen-state uses 256-byte blocks and
checked, retransmitted short title frames. Native tmux byte streams use 8 KiB
blocks with checked envelopes. Other connections use up to 64 KiB
blocks. An interrupted or lost screen frame fails without publishing an
incomplete file.
The native client removes unpublished temporary files on transfer failure;
a process crash can leave a hidden .snag-upload-ID staging file. Other clients
can leave partial files: inspect them before use. Only acknowledged completion
reports delivery.
.PP
The terminal lease restores modes and display on success and failure, then
replays keyboard bytes received after the final EXIT. Source files are unchanged.
If the source's size or modification timestamp changes while being sent,
the transfer fails. Interactive terminal transfers require POSIX hosts. One-shot send_file queues
its export for wrapped attachment. Read-only model turns reject send_file.
.SH "Vim workspace"
The development workspace opens with
.BR "snajpagent vm" .
It provides session/workspace pickers, retained transcript browsing, prompt
editing, owner creation/resumption and persistent split layouts.
Workspace browsing does not start model turns or IRC connections.
Runtime activation is explicit; ordinary startup retains its terminal interface.
.PP
Transcript panes use the standalone Markdown and tool formatter at the pane's
width: prose bullets, emphasis, code, tables and tool detail follow the same
presentation settings. New transcript and conversation views start at verbosity0;
saved views retain their selected level. Use :verbosity to change it.
IRC traffic appears in its channel or query view, separately from the rollout.
Workspace colors follow [ui] color and NO_COLOR. Split separators and
full-width status bars identify pane boundaries and the focused window.
The focused status bar uses the terminal's normal reverse colors; other bars
use light blue when color is enabled.
Resizing reflows text to the available width. FOLLOW fills the available height
with recent history, reading across journal pages as needed; HOLD preserves the
selected source position.
Pending history loads in other panes continue after resize or catalogue refresh.
Scrolling stops at the first or last visible output. Pages containing only
hidden bookkeeping preserve the visible transcript while extending its known boundary.
Transcript-only restore reads the displayed sessions; opening :sessions loads the
session catalogue. Pane status begins with the session name when available.
Each session pane keeps its normal prompt visible, including an empty draft.
In FOLLOW, the focused pane places the cursor at its prompt immediately,
including after restore, pane navigation and resize. Moving through history or
selecting transcript text puts the cursor at that source position; G returns
to FOLLOW and the prompt.
Current owners supply their configured prompt, timestamp, effective model,
context meter and activity state. Prompt colors, word wrapping, multiline
indentation and mouse hit positions share the standalone composer.
Older running owners use the workspace configuration and available model state;
unavailable context and queue counts appear as ?. Updating the installed binary
preserves running owners; their next normal restart enables the full prompt state.
.PP
.B vm -N NAME
creates a named workspace.
.B vm --resume WORKSPACE
restores an exact workspace name or a unique ID prefix;
.B vm --last
restores the most recently active workspace.
An open workspace remains exclusively owned by its frontend. A second resume
reports that ownership without stealing or stopping it.
Workspace names and IDs are separate from agent-session names and IDs.
.PP
On supported POSIX hosts,
.B :session detach
or
.B :session d
saves the workspace layout and drafts, releases all agent controllers and
returns to the shell. Running agents continue. Exit waits for owner draft and
detach acknowledgements; the shell displays a copyable workspace resume command.
.PP
In the colon command line, Tab completes command names and fixed options;
repeated Tab cycles matches and Shift-Tab cycles backwards. Enter executes the
completed command. For example, type :session d and press Tab to complete
:session detach. Ctrl-L redraws the command line and workspace.
.PP
.B vm -l [N]
lists all open workspaces and up to N stored workspaces, newest activity first.
The default stored count is ten; zero keeps only open/error rows.
Listing, help and version leave terminal modes unchanged and perform no
provider startup. Interactive use requires a capable terminal on stdin/stdout.
.PP
Use j/k or arrows to select, gg/G for the first/last row, Ctrl-D/U to move half
a window and / to filter picker metadata. R refreshes the lists; selection
follows the same full ID when activity reorders agent rows. The session picker
uses the classic list's attached/detached/running/stored ordering and data.
Enter in the session picker resumes the selected agent: it attaches to a live
workspace-capable owner or starts a stopped one. An older live owner leaves
readable history and an attachment diagnostic; :classic opens its full terminal.
In the workspace picker, Enter restores the selected workspace. A failed open
or invalid snapshot keeps the current workspace usable.
.PP
Press o in the agent picker, or use
.B :history SESSION_ID
with a full ID or unique prefix, to browse retained history without taking the
session's writer lock. gg and G load the oldest and newest source ranges;
j/k, arrows and half-page movement cross source-page boundaries. Opening history
or pressing G enables FOLLOW at a fresh tail. Moving away from the tail enters
HOLD: incoming output updates the newer-output indicator while the viewport stays
in place. R refreshes the current range while retaining the selected source
anchor and FOLLOW/HOLD state. Live workspace-capable owners supply committed
journal positions to read-only observers; browsing leaves their attachment
status and controller unchanged. The status line labels these views committed.
Older or stopped owners use the last complete snapshot, labelled snapshot.
Both modes check for updates once per second. An unchanged committed position
needs only file-identity checks, including while the owner is writing an
unfinished record. An unchanged complete snapshot also emits no frame.
If an owner notification temporarily trails an already displayed snapshot,
that snapshot remains visible until the owner catches up.
Open buffers retain verified descriptors across focus, attach and detach changes. A replaced,
truncated or invalid journal stops automatic observation with a visible error;
G or R explicitly retries after the source is repaired. A snapshot uses the last complete record and shows a partial-tail label
when an unfinished record follows it. The journal stays unchanged.
.PP
.B :verbosity 0..6
re-renders the current historical range. Source anchors retain the position
through verbosity and width changes; when a block becomes hidden, selection
moves to the next visible event. Restoring detail returns to the original anchor
until a navigation command selects another position. Redaction and binary escapes
retain offsets into the original text, including when a source page starts midway
through output. Older workspace positions are converted when loaded.
Level 0 shows conversation, level 1 adds tool
headings, level 2 adds arguments and result previews of up to 512 characters,
and level 3 adds retained process bytes.
Higher levels also show event markers. Binary output uses visible byte escapes;
terminal controls remain text. Provider continuation and private compaction
payloads are omitted. Tool names and arguments are recovered from earlier
responses when needed, and level 2 reads referenced output even when it precedes
the current page. These cancellable background reads preserve the page and its
source anchor. A call without matching retained metadata keeps its call ID.
Use level 3 and history navigation to read output beyond a result preview.
Completed and corrected response text is reconciled across source pages: each
streamed range shows its part of the final text, and the terminal record adds any
remaining text. Failed or interrupted responses retain their recorded public
text and state; text absent from their terminal snapshot is labelled unconfirmed.
The current history snapshot determines these labels and contents. R refreshes
held output when the response later completes.
.PP
In a transcript or report, /TEXT searches forward and ?TEXT searches backward;
n repeats the previous direction and N reverses it. Empty search input reuses the
previous query. Search is literal UTF-8 and case-sensitive. Use
.B :set ignorecase
for Unicode full case folding, or
.B :set noignorecase
to restore exact case. Folding uses Unicode 17 default mappings, including
multi-character mappings such as sharp s to ss; text normalization is unchanged.
.PP
Search covers the active view at its current verbosity, including headings and
unloaded retained pages. Adjacent chunks of one response or output stream retain
their overlap. A report searches only that report. The background scan shows
source-byte/event progress and checks a fixed history boundary; n can include
new output on its next scan. Other visible panes continue to show live output
while a search, copy or distant cursor motion scans retained history.
Wraparound is reported. Ctrl-C cancels a running
search before requesting a model interruption. Navigation or a verbosity/view
change cancels obsolete work and retains the query. Corruption reports an
incomplete search. Search moves only the focused window and shows its cursor at
the matched source position, including after resize.
.PP
The composer command
.B /search TEXT
invokes this local search and displays the entered command with its result.
.B /search
without text opens search entry. These commands also work while reading a stored
session. Search text stays outside model prompts and session history.
.PP
Transcripts and reports support h/l, w/b/e, 0/^/$, j/k for logical lines and
gj/gk for wrapped rows. Counts continue across retained pages. Numbered gg or G
jumps to that one-based logical line, including visible headings; plain gg/G
reach the oldest/newest text. H/M/L move within the visible window, zz centers
the cursor, Ctrl-D/U move by half a window and Ctrl-B/F by a full window.
Cold navigation runs in the background with progress. Ctrl-C or Escape restores
the previous cursor and viewport. Keys typed during a move or yank wait in order;
this includes bracketed paste. Canceling discards those pending keys.
Redaction placeholders move as whole source-addressable units.
Use v for character selection, V for line selection or Ctrl-V for a rectangle.
y copies the selection into the workspace register; yy copies the current line,
and y followed by a motion copies that range. p/P inserts the copied text into
the session composer. The operator can edit the draft before submitting it.
.PP
Yanks preserve logical line breaks and whole Unicode graphemes. Soft wrapping
adds no copied newlines. Line selections include displayed bullets and indentation;
character selections copy their text across wrapped rows.
Rectangles use display columns, expand selected tabs
and pad short lines. Pasting a rectangle inserts each row at the draft's display
column, expands an intersected tab, pads short lines and adds missing lines.
A count repeats the rectangle horizontally; undo reverses the whole insertion.
Protected values stay redacted and terminal controls stay
visible text. Selection pins the displayed history boundary and survives resize;
new output cannot extend its range. Changing verbosity ends selection and retains
the previous register. Escape leaves visual mode.
.PP
Copy runs in the background with source-byte/event progress. Ctrl-C cancels it
before interrupting an owner. An empty motion, canceled copy or failed copy
preserves the previous register. Large registers use private temporary files; copying has no history-size
quota. Pasting retains the composer's existing input-size limit. Registers belong
to the current frontend and are shared by its session composers.
.PP
An explicit yank also attempts a workstation clipboard copy according to
[terminal] clipboard. Deletes and changes affect only the workspace register.
Preparation, transfer and publication run alongside editing and owner activity.
A newer yank cancels an earlier uncommitted copy and queues the new selection.
Ctrl-C cancels clipboard work before interrupting the model. Cancellation accepted
before publication leaves the previous clipboard intact; publication already
underway reports its actual result or an uncertain outcome. External failure
preserves the new workspace register.
.PP
Run remote workspaces through the workstation wrapper, for example:
.PP
.nf
    snajpagent remote ssh -t host snajpagent vm
    snajpagent remote mosh host snajpagent vm
.fi
.PP
The wrapper stages sequenced UTF-8 chunks privately and verifies length and SHA256
before publishing. Retries recover the existing result without copying twice.
Transfer byte progress remains visible, including on the slower Mosh title-state
path. Clipboard protocol input is kept out of commands and drafts.
With no responding wrapper, a local workspace uses its native backend; an SSH or
Mosh environment requires the wrapper for native workstation access. Explicit
osc52 policy permits direct terminal output, whose size and acceptance depend on
the terminal. Its status says "Clipboard sequence sent; terminal acceptance
unconfirmed". Confirmed native writes say "Clipboard written" or "Workstation
clipboard written". Short capability waits precede local fallback.
During a direct terminal sequence, repainting waits for its complete emission;
input, owner activity and retained output continue. Quit, suspend and classic
handoff settle or cancel a pending copy before transferring terminal ownership.
.PP
.B :sessions
opens the agent picker;
.B :workspaces
opens the workspace picker;
.B :help
shows the current controls.
.B :split
or :sp divides horizontally; :vsplit or :vsp divides vertically.
In NORMAL mode, Ctrl-W s/v also splits. Ctrl-W w or Ctrl-W Ctrl-W cycles focus.
Ctrl-W followed by h/j/k/l, an arrow, or Ctrl-H/J/K/L selects a neighboring
window in that direction. Escape leaves INSERT before window navigation;
Ctrl-W in INSERT retains Vim's delete-word behavior. Ctrl-W also interrupts
pending history navigation so focus can move immediately.
Ctrl-W = equalizes proportions, +/- changes height and >/< changes width.
Terminal shrink temporarily hides windows while preserving their layout.
Mouse reporting is enabled inside the workspace. Click an attached, writable
session pane to focus it and enter INSERT. A click on its prompt also positions
the draft cursor; a body or status click uses the remembered draft position.
Dragging transcript text enters VISUAL selection. Read-only history and report
clicks place the source cursor. Hits on a wide character,
combining sequence, escaped control or tab retain the whole source unit.
Drag transcript/report text to select it, then press y to copy. The drag stays
in its original window; release leaves the selection available for keyboard
adjustment. A single click ends the previous selection and cancels unfinished
colon/search input. Empty and loading panes receive focus immediately.
The wheel scrolls the hovered window without changing keyboard focus.
Drag a split separator to resize it; proportions survive workspace resume.
Resize, focus loss or keyboard input ends an in-progress drag.
.PP
.B :set nomouse
disables mouse reporting for terminal-native selection;
.B :set mouse
enables it again. The terminal's selection-bypass modifier also remains available.
The terminal must allow click reports: in iTerm2, enable Profiles > Terminal >
Report mouse clicks & drags as well as mouse reporting.
Exit, suspension and classic terminal handoff restore mouse modes; returning
to the workspace reapplies the current setting.
.PP
In a read-only transcript, picker or help view,
.B :q
or :close closes the window; the last window exits the workspace.
In an attached rollout transcript, :q requests normal owner shutdown and waits for its
acknowledgement; :qa requests shutdown of every owner controlled by this workspace,
then exits. Unsent drafts or unresolved submissions block these commands.
:q! and :qa! discard the affected drafts and request the same normal shutdown.
.B :close
closes only the window; the last view of a session releases control and preserves
its owner. Terminal loss also releases control and preserves owners.
Detaching after a quit request leaves the wait; the requested shutdown may finish.
.B :workspace
shows its name, ID and save status;
.B :workspace name NAME
renames it and :workspace save writes immediately. Quote names containing spaces.
Layout, filter and draft changes save at one-second intervals; clean exit,
switch and suspend also save. A save failure stays visible and can be retried.
Leaving an untouched initial picker creates no saved workspace.
After a successful save and terminal restoration, normal workspace exit prints
its shell-quoted snajpagent vm --resume command. The command uses the current
workspace's full ID, including after a rename or workspace switch, and retains
any nondefault --dotdir.
.PP
Snapshots are private files under $DOTDIR/workspaces/WORKSPACE_ID/workspace.json.
They retain the layout, focus, selection, filters, transcript source anchors,
verbosity, FOLLOW/HOLD, per-conversation drafts, command transcript references
and unresolved submissions. Version13 snapshots read earlier workspace versions;
older binaries cannot resume a newly saved version13 workspace.
Unsent classic-attachment input retains its destination session too.
Restoring a controlled view attempts to reattach its existing owner; a busy or
unavailable owner leaves the draft intact. Other transcript views read history
without taking control; FOLLOW restores at the current tail.
Corrupt or newer unsupported
state is reported and preserved. Bracketed paste inserts literal text in command
or filter input; it cannot execute a command by itself. Escape returns to normal
mode, Ctrl-L redraws and Ctrl-Z suspends on supported platforms. Clean exit and
handled shutdown signals restore cooked input, cursor, mouse and screen modes.
.SS "Create and resume agent sessions"
.B :new [NAME]
starts an agent using the current configuration in the workspace's dotdir.
Quote a name containing spaces. The agent name belongs to the new session;
.B vm -N
names the workspace itself.
.PP
.B :session [SESSION_ID]
attaches a live owner or starts a stopped one with its saved resume options,
provider/model selection, working directory and ordinary recovery behavior.
With no operand, it selects the current transcript or highlighted agent row.
.B vm --session SESSION_ID
performs the same explicit action at workspace startup, optionally alongside
-N, --resume or --last. An active goal or queued work can continue according to
the normal agent resume rules. Browsing history and restoring a workspace alone
leave stopped owners stopped.
.PP
On supported POSIX hosts, startup runs asynchronously. Other windows, commands
and drafts remain usable.
When ready, the owner opens in the requesting window. Selecting another buffer,
closing that window or switching workspaces leaves the new owner detached;
find it in :sessions. Closing or losing the frontend during startup also leaves
the owner running. Startup errors appear in the workspace; correct the reported
problem and explicitly retry the command. If the startup
report is lost, inspect :sessions before issuing another :new command.
Saved workspaces never replay launch commands. New owners keep their own private
terminal and can later be reached through ordinary snajpagent --resume or :classic.
Native owner launch requires a supported POSIX host.
.SS "Live workspace sessions on Windows"
The experimental Windows workspace runs one live agent inside its own process.
.B :new
creates it; :session or vm --session starts a stored session with its saved options.
Splits share this agent and can also display other stored histories. Closing its
last window keeps the agent and drafts hidden; :buffers reopens them. Closing the
last workspace window returns to the picker while the agent remains live.
.PP
Use :q in the agent transcript to quit it, or :qa to quit it and the workspace.
Unsent drafts block shutdown; :q! and :qa! explicitly discard them.
Workspace switching requires the live agent to finish shutting down first.
Closing the console or terminating the workspace ends its in-process agent;
resume the stored session explicitly to continue.
.PP
Persistent attachment to other processes, :detach, :classic and additional live
agents are unavailable. Commands requiring a whole terminal keep their text for
recovery; use the ordinary interface for its file-transfer dialogs and editors.
The remote wrapper is available on supported POSIX hosts. Stored workspace resume
restores layout and drafts; it starts an agent only with an explicit session action.
.SS "Attach and compose"
.B :attach SESSION_ID
opens and controls an existing workspace-capable owner. With no operand,
:attach reconnects the current transcript or query. All buffers of a session
share one controller; splits displaying the same conversation share its draft
and undo history. Different conversations keep separate drafts.
:detach releases control for all those views and preserves
the running owner. In NORMAL, Ctrl-C requests the owner's ordinary turn interruption.
Attached window status shows the provider, model, effort and working/idle state
reported by the owner.
.PP
The focused status bar names the current mode:
.B NORMAL
for navigation and draft editing,
.B INSERT
for entering prompts and slash commands in that pane, and
.B COMMAND-LINE
for workspace command or search entry. Type : from NORMAL for Ex commands such
as :vsp or :session detach. Escape returns to NORMAL with the session draft intact.
A colon typed in INSERT is ordinary draft text. VISUAL identifies transcript selection.
.PP
Click an attached writable pane, or press i, a, I or A in a transcript, to enter
the composer in INSERT. The keys insert before or after the remembered cursor,
at the first nonblank character, or at the end of its logical line. INSERT accepts
text, literal bracketed paste, arrows, Home/End and Backspace/Delete.
Ctrl-U deletes to the logical line start; Ctrl-W deletes the preceding word.
Enter submits; Ctrl-J inserts a newline. Submitting returns the pane to FOLLOW
and appends the prompt or command and its output to the transcript. Commands
keep the composer in INSERT; :report and :reports open retained command output
explicitly. Background control completions join the originating transcript too.
Escape, Ctrl-[ and Ctrl-C leave INSERT,
preserve the draft and place the cursor on the preceding grapheme within the line.
In NORMAL, Tab switches transcript/composer focus.
Escape followed immediately by text leaves INSERT or command input before
interpreting that text in NORMAL, including a fast Escape-colon.
Navigation typed while a report or history view first loads is retained in order.
Escape cancels pending navigation; pane switching and composer entry remain available.
Drafts wrap within the window and survive split changes, detach and workspace resume.
.PP
Composer NORMAL controls:
.TP
.B h j k l, w b e, 0 ^ $, gg G
Move by grapheme, logical line or word, to line boundaries or to the first/last
line. [count]G selects a one-based line. Counts apply to motions and operators;
2d3w deletes six words. Words group Unicode letters, numbers and underscore,
or runs of other nonblank graphemes. Unicode separators and whitespace end words.
.TP
.B gj gk, H M L, zz
Move by wrapped row, to the top/middle/bottom visible composer row, or center
the cursor. Ctrl-U/D move half a composer page; Ctrl-B/F and PageUp/Down move
a full page. Logical vertical movement preserves the desired column.
.TP
.B i a I A o O, x, d c y, dd cc yy, p P
Insert before/after the cursor, at the first nonblank/end of line, or on a new
line below/above. x deletes whole graphemes. d/c/y delete/change/yank a supported
motion; doubled operators use whole lines. p/P put the workspace register
after/before the cursor, or below/above the line for a whole-line register.
The register is shared by session composers and splits within this workspace.
.TP
.B u, Ctrl-R
Undo/redo within the current draft. Typing, or a change followed by typing,
forms one edit until movement or leaving INSERT. Each bracketed paste forms
its own edit. Submission, recovery and adopted owner text reset undo history.
Undo history and the register remain in memory until the workspace exits;
saved draft text remains available through workspace resume.
.PP
Unsupported composer commands report a message and leave text unchanged.
Mouse focus changes cancel pending counts and operators.
.PP
Owners advertising draft support retain an acknowledged copy while detached.
On attachment, the workspace compares both copies with their last shared text.
A change on one side is retained automatically. If both texts changed, the
workspace keeps both and shows a draft conflict. Choose
.B :draft local
to keep the workspace copy or
.B :draft owner
to use the retained owner copy. Both conflicting texts stay saved across reconnect,
even if another controller changes the owner draft again.
The choice also works while detached; the next attachment checks the live owner again.
Recover any retained submission before resolving its
draft conflict. Owners without this capability use the workspace's saved copy.
Clean detach, last-window close, workspace switch and suspend flush the final
edit before releasing control. A connection failure leaves unacknowledged text
in the workspace snapshot; unresolved conflicts remain saved there too.
The saving notice remains until the owner acknowledges detach; the completed
detach notice means another client can claim the controller.
It includes :attach SESSION_ID to reconnect from the workspace.
Draft synchronization does not submit text or add it to model history.
.PP
Before transmitting, the workspace saves the request ID, text and owner identity.
With draft support, it waits for that text's revision acknowledgement before
submitting. New typing belongs to the next draft and survives older acknowledgements.
The composer then accepts the next draft. A durable admission receipt clears
only that submitted copy. Reconnecting queries its receipt; an unknown result
or changed owner leaves the submission retained without automatic retransmission.
Inspect history, then use
.B :recover
with an empty draft to recover its text if needed. A refused submission also
remains recoverable. Each conversation retains its own pending submission and
frozen recipient. A nick or connection change preserves the old draft and rejects
sending it to a different recipient.
.SS "IRC connection and conversation windows"
Open a known connection with :buffer SESSION/ENDPOINT/ or :vsp SESSION/ENDPOINT/.
The trailing slash selects status and server notices for both local identities.
Use :buffers for its channel and private conversations, or enter /connections
in the composer for the current connection directory. /query and /join inherit
the pane's endpoint. Ordinary text requires an explicit target and stays in the
draft. Connection panes share the session's existing owner and sockets.
.PP
In an INSERT-mode conversation composer, Ctrl-N and Ctrl-P complete and cycle
matching @nickname words forward and backward. Candidates come from that
channel or private query. Completion preserves surrounding text and stays in
the current window; it adds no trailing space or submission. Owners that do
not provide a current roster report no matches.
.PP
Enter /query NICK or /query ENDPOINT/NICK in an attached composer to open an
operator private conversation in that window. A successful command changes the
requesting window while it is still focused, displaying that composer, and its
next draft is empty. Otherwise the conversation remains available in :buffers.
Private replies go to the captured peer without adding model input.
After a connection loss, enter /query ENDPOINT/NICK without message text to
select a query on the current connection. A command that includes message text
retains the original connection and refuses a stale send.
Use /chat ENDPOINT/#CHANNEL for a known channel or /join ENDPOINT/#CHANNEL
to join a channel as the operator. The built-in server offers its configured
room. Each pane keeps its exact channel
membership; PART, KICK and reconnect reject stale sends. Use /chat or /join
explicitly to select the current membership before composing a new send.
.PP
To retry an uncertain IRC message, inspect its retained send results first;
the peer may already have received it. Select the body in the old conversation
with v or V and copy it with y. Reopen the intended peer with /query ENDPOINT/NICK,
return to NORMAL and use P to put the copied text in its composer. Review the
recipient and text, then enter INSERT and press Enter to send. Copying, reconnecting
and restoring a workspace never resubmit these chunks. A later nickname change
rejects the recovered draft and leaves it available through :recover.
.PP
Use /query SESSION/ENDPOINT/NICK to address another session by its saved name
or unique ID prefix. First attach that session in another split with :attach ID.
Qualified /msg, /notice, /chat, /join, /part and /connections commands use the same
dispatch. The workspace sends through the addressed owner's existing controller
and preserves its draft. A session controlled elsewhere remains read-only;
refusal keeps the command in the originating composer.
Older running owners accept exact saved names and full session IDs; abbreviated
IDs in forwarded slash commands require the updated owner parser.
.PP
Selections and command output return to the originating window while it remains
focused with an empty next draft. New typing preserves that window and its draft.
Successful /msg and /notice preserve the selected buffer. Saved forwarded
submissions reconcile their receipts on resume; use :recover in the original
composer to restore an uncertain or rejected command without sending it again.
.PP
.B :buffers
lists the workspace's rollout, connection, channel and private conversation buffers, including
operator and read-only agent conversations. Rows group by session and endpoint,
with connection views above their channels and private queries. Each row shows
its buffer ID, identity, unread count, connection state, drafts, pending submissions
and last message time in local time. Use / to filter by name, qualified address,
identity or ID, then Enter to open the selected buffer in the current window.
Retained drafts keep their captured destination and show old route after a nick,
membership or connection change. Empty obsolete routes disappear from the directory
when no window displays or selects them.
.PP
Unread counts include incoming messages and notices since this workspace last
displayed the conversation's tail in a focused FOLLOW window. Held history,
background splits and the directory preserve unread counts. Terminal focus reports,
when available, also preserve them while another application is focused.
Public channels and connection views share read positions across local identities;
private queries keep separate operator and agent read positions. Workspace resume
retains these markers. Historical replay and outgoing messages do not add unread counts.
A question mark means the owner or an older checkpoint lacks complete accounting;
reading the tail establishes a new position when the owner supplies counters.
.PP
.B :buffer ADDRESS
or :b ADDRESS opens a known operator buffer by session/endpoint/nick or
session/endpoint/#channel address, or a connection at session/endpoint/; session names
and ID prefixes are accepted. :buffer ID accepts a unique ID prefix from :buffers,
which also distinguishes retained routes after a nick or connection change.
An ambiguous address leaves the selected buffer unchanged.
.B :bnext
(:bn) and :bprevious (:bp) cycle the current session's buffers.
Tab retains the history/composer focus control; classic mode uses Tab/Shift-Tab
for its destination cycle.
.PP
.B :vsp [ADDRESS_OR_ID]
and :sp [ADDRESS_OR_ID] create vertical or horizontal splits and select a known
buffer there. Without an argument they display another view of the current
buffer. Enter /query or /chat in a split's composer to open a conversation there.
A failed buffer lookup leaves the new split displaying its previous buffer.
.PP
Query history, gg/G, search, visual selection and copy read only that connection,
conversation and local identity across retained connection generations.
A search announces its match after the viewport is loaded; following navigation
and selection keys wait for that read. Beginning a new composer edit interrupts
a pending viewport move and preserves the draft.
Channel history includes public events from both local identities and compatible
legacy room records. Case comparison follows the captured server rules. Other
rooms, endpoints and private messages stay outside that channel view.
The operator can inspect agent conversations; their composers are read-only.
Conversation windows and their drafts survive named-workspace resume.
:q closes a conversation window and preserves its owner; :qa retains its explicit
shutdown meaning for all controlled owners. :history returns to the rollout.
.PP
Successful /msg, /notice and /me commands preserve the conversation
window. Their command reports remain available through :reports.
.SS "Read command output"
Submit a slash command from the transcript composer. Supported commands include
/help, /status, /history, /model, /fast, /effort, /context, /state, /goal,
/steering, /banner, /configure, /compact, /yield, /verbose, /cat, /msg,
/notice and /session list or name.
The command echo and immediate result open a retained report when that transcript
is still focused and its next draft is empty. Newer typing remains in the composer;
use
.B :reports
to list retained output, or
.B :report [ID_PREFIX]
to open the latest report or an exact unique prefix. Enter opens a selected report.
.PP
Reports support gg, G, j/k, page movement and splits. Use
.B :history
to return to the associated session.
.B :q
closes a report window and preserves its owner; :qa still requests owner shutdown.
Report references and positions survive named-workspace resume, including after
an owner has stopped. Reading saved reports never starts an owner.
.PP
:reports and :report read the private session catalogue in the background, so a
fresh workspace can discover output from stopped or restarted owners. R refreshes
the report list. A damaged complete catalogue entry reports an error and preserves
the loaded list or report; an incomplete final entry leaves the complete prefix
available. Older reports remain accessible through their saved workspace references.
.PP
Each report is a private immutable session file. Background loading verifies
its length and digest, redacts known secrets and renders terminal controls inert.
Changed or missing files display an error; a previously loaded view remains readable.
R rereads and verifies the retained snapshot. Explicit session deletion removes
its reports and catalogue.
Reports stay outside model context. Deferred controls such as /configure,
/model cache and /compact initially report acceptance, then add a separate
completion report. A notification makes it available through :reports while
preserving the selected window and newer typing. Reconnect to the same owner
recovers reports created while the workspace was disconnected. Coalesced
requests produce one completion report for the operation they share.
.PP
/fast confirms ON or OFF in its report; an attached transcript also shows FAST
when the owner selects priority service. With a supporting owner, commands needing
terminal interaction automatically open its whole terminal and execute there.
Both the workspace draft and the classic prompt's existing draft remain intact.
/config, /send, /receive and /attach return automatically after their editor,
transfer or file preparation finishes. Their receipt remains pending until
completion; /config's report includes the deferred configuration result.
A replacement terminal attachment remains open
when an earlier command finishes. Other terminal commands use /s d to return.
New typing in the workspace before the handoff remains saved in its draft.
.PP
Use /receive in a rollout composer to upload workstation files through
snajpagent remote, or /attach PATH for a file on the owner's host. The whole
terminal handles selection and transfer, then returns to the workspace on
completion or cancellation. /attachments lists pending files in a retained
report; /detach N|all removes them. Files remain with that session's rollout
until its next private prompt, queue or steer is admitted. IRC messages and
transfer commands do not submit them to the model. Older owners may require
/s d after file preparation to return to the workspace.
.PP
Terminal resizing during file selection carries through to the workspace on
return. After a disconnected frontend exits, resume the named workspace to
recover its saved draft and reconcile the interrupted command's receipt. Resume
does not repeat the upload; use /attachments to inspect retained files.
.PP
When the workspace stays running inside Screen or tmux, reconnect to that
multiplexer. Press Ctrl-C to end an interrupted file dialog and return to the
workspace. Its draft stays saved; inspect /attachments before explicitly retrying
/receive. Reattaching and cancelling the old dialog do not submit a model prompt.
.PP
The handoff references the already submitted command and live owner instance;
repeated references cannot execute it twice. A command recovered from a saved
workspace waits for an explicit :classic or :recover. Uncertain attachment outcomes
are reconciled through the existing command receipt, without automatic replay.
Owners lacking terminal-command support require :recover and manual execution
through :classic. An owner without semantic command support preserves the draft.
.SS "Use an owner's full terminal"
.B :classic [SESSION_ID]
opens an existing native owner's terminal, including owners started before
workspace support. With no operand, it uses the current transcript or selected
session. It saves the workspace and flushes attached drafts before releasing
all workspace controllers. The classic terminal occupies the whole screen;
its commands, pager, transfers and Ctrl-Z suspension behave as in --attach.
Use
.B /s d
to detach and return. An owner exit also returns to the workspace. The saved
layout and drafts remain, and visible views reconnect with their previous
control or observation choice. A stopped or busy owner reports the failure;
this command never starts another owner.
.PP
The classic prompt and workspace composer currently keep separate drafts.
Keys already read after the :classic command's Enter reach that owner after
attachment completes, before later terminal input.
Input remains raw during both directions of the terminal handoff.
If attachment fails,
unsent keys remain associated with that session. :classic retries that
destination; :close saves them, and :q/:qa require recovery or explicit ! discard.
.PP
After workspace resume, saved classic input has an uncertain delivery outcome
and is never replayed automatically. Inspect the session history, then use
.B :recover
to append it to that session's unsent workspace draft. Return becomes a newline;
other control bytes and invalid UTF-8 are shown as literal \[rs]xHH text.
Review the recovered draft before attaching and submitting it. Pending classic
input takes precedence over ordinary submission recovery. Recovery exceeding
the draft limit leaves the original queued input saved.
.SS "Native semantic endpoint"
With workspace support compiled in, new POSIX native owners publish
.B view.sock
beside their private terminal endpoint. Same-user peers can observe committed
history boundaries and reserve the single controller shared with classic
terminal attachment. Observation leaves session attachment status unchanged;
a bound workspace controller counts as attached. Connection loss releases
control and leaves the owner running.
.PP
The current backend supports plain rollout submissions, durable admission
receipts, cancellation, detach and normal owner shutdown. Reconnect can query
a receipt without repeating a prompt. A changed owner instance with an unknown
receipt requires explicit reconciliation against history.
.PP
Typed command requests use the same serialized owner queue and reconnect receipts.
Completed dispatch returns an immutable report containing the command and its
immediate output. Reports stay outside model context, survive owner restarts,
and are removed by explicit session deletion. Deferred controls acknowledge
scheduling before their later completion. Commands needing a terminal transaction
return that requirement before execution. The workspace retains report references
and opens their contents on its background reader. The reports capability adds
ordered report notifications and replay on reconnect to the live owner. A
terminal-required command can transfer to a bound
classic attachment using its advertised terminal_commands capability. The owner
admits that saved reference before accepting following keyboard input. The current
handoff returns automatically after finite /config or /send operations;
other commands return on /s d.
.PP
The backend offers revisioned rollout and operator-conversation drafts. Bound controllers can read,
replace and subscribe to a draft, using its expected revision to reject stale edits.
Revision-aware admission clears only the submitted revision; newer drafts survive.
These drafts stay in owner memory and do not enter model history until submitted.
The workspace composer reconciles these drafts with its saved local snapshots.
Classic terminal editor drafts remain separate from these semantic drafts.
.PP
The irc_queries capability publishes compact query metadata and accepts private
routes containing the connection and conversation IDs, connection generation,
local identity and peer nick. Operator sends preserve this exact recipient;
agent queries remain read-only. Independent routes keep separate drafts and
revisions. A changed nick or connection generation leaves an older draft
available under its original route, with stale sends rejected. Private sends
stay outside model input, including during an active turn. Their receipts
acknowledge durable local admission; delivery status remains in IRC events.
Messages consisting only of empty or stripped lines are rejected with their drafts retained.
.PP
The irc_channels capability adds channel catalogs and routes with room,
membership, endpoint and case mapping. Public history spans local identities;
sends validate the captured operator membership. Older owners retain their
advertised capabilities and report unsupported channel control.
.PP
/query, /chat and /join return a selection route to the requesting frontend
without changing the classic tab. /msg and /notice send explicitly; /me uses
the supplied conversation.
Commands requiring a whole terminal must originate from the rollout.
Existing owners retain their advertised capabilities. Builds using
.B WITH_VM=0
omit this endpoint; Windows currently lacks the native-owner backend.
.SH "Commands"
Commands normally occupy a single submitted line.
Each entered command stays visible with its original prompt before its feedback,
including while the engine is busy. The displayed line confirms terminal input;
the command's acknowledgement reports acceptance or completion.
.BR /ro ,
numbered sends and
.B /all
also accept multiline message text.  Prefix text with
.B //
to submit a literal leading
.BR / .
Commands can be submitted when the application displays a prompt. A foreground
slash command completes before the next app command is accepted; typeahead is
held without a second ready prompt. Model selection changes the next response
in an active turn and remains selected for later turns and resume. Effort-only
selection applies to later requests after a safe response boundary. Configuration, cache
refresh, compaction, retry and delete run at safe request boundaries;
their accepted intents in an established session survive restart and retain
admission order.  Configuration editing and cache discovery before the first
work item preserve lazy session creation.  Repeated
requests for the same pending control coalesce.  Delete still requires explicit
confirmation. Commands typed while another foreground control runs are
considered after it returns. A policy-stopped turn remains parked
while commands run; provider continuation still requires task clarification.
Presentation, steering and tool yield act on current work.  Text received at an
idle rollout prompt just before work starts retains its future-turn meaning;
following commands remain responsive.
.PP
Public response text is saved incrementally before display.  Recovery continues
the original turn from saved inputs, public output and tool results.  Started
calls without durable results are reported as outcome unknown; unstarted calls
are reported as not run.  Saved process output remains available, while old
process handles cannot be reused.  Terminal drafts and submissions awaiting
engine acknowledgement are not yet durable work; the submitted-text or control
acknowledgement follows event-log acceptance.
.SS Session and model
.PP
Informational command reports use the configured
.B pager
on an interactive terminal. This includes /help, /status, /state, goal status,
/queue, /session lists, /history, /model lists, /context, /effort, /verbose,
/banner, /attachments, /voice devices, /names, /topic, /nick, /steering and
/server status, including their aliases. Each command opens one report;
/state combines session status, goal status and goal actions in that report.
Commands that display an updated report after a setting change also page it.
Quit the pager to return to the composer. Brief acknowledgements, errors,
view switches and ongoing model output remain in the normal terminal display.
While a pager is open, provider turns, managed processes and IRC continue.
New session output waits until the pager exits, then appears in order before
the current prompt. Model-requested file downloads stay queued while the pager
owns the terminal and can be delivered to a wrapped client after it closes.
During active work, keyboard /verbose queries also stay in that display for an
immediate response; idle queries use the pager.
.PP
With the default
.BR "pager = on" ,
an unset $PAGER or
.B PAGER=less
selects
.B less \-FX
when less is available. The \-F option returns automatically when the report fits
on one screen; \-X keeps its text visible after exit. Longer reports wait for
the pager's quit command.
Explicit pager options are preserved; an empty $PAGER disables paging.
Set $PAGER on the remote host when using Mosh. Use the pager's scrolling and
search keys to read reports taller than the terminal. Disabled paging, unavailable
less with the default selection, and a pager that cannot start use direct output.
Noninteractive CLI output stays direct.
Paged reports contain
plain text; /history retains its turn labels and displays Markdown source.
.TP
.BR /help , " /?"
Show commands grouped by settings, models/context, goals/queue, sessions,
files/media and network chat, followed by usage notes and essential keys.
Square brackets mark optional parts, a vertical bar separates alternatives,
and uppercase words are values to supply. Required goal and queue subcommands
are listed explicitly. Interactive help uses the configured
.BR pager ;
when paging is disabled, unavailable or fails, it prints directly with ordinary
foreground text and bold section headings where color is enabled. Long lines
wrap at word boundaries. Pager text and redirected output are plain text.
.B /state goal help
shows the same goal forms separately.
.B /goal
is an alias for
.BR /state " goal" .
.TP
.B /status
Show session, model and fast-mode selections, queue, verbosity, capacity, token accounting, requested
network roles and actual connection state.
.TP
.BR /session , " /s"
Print the full current session ID followed by running sessions. A new unsaved
session is identified as not yet saved; listing does not start a model turn.
.TP
.BR "/session list" , " /session l" , " /s list" , " /s l"
Print the current session ID and all saved sessions,
with column headings and their attached, detached, running or stored status,
in that order. Running means the attachment status is unavailable.
Each group shows the latest saved activity first.
This does not attach to or resume a session.
.TP
.BR "/session attach ID" , " /session a ID" , " /s a ID"
Switch the terminal to a live session on Linux. A unique short ID is accepted.
The source stays attached and accepts input until the destination accepts the
connection. Input read alongside destination acceptance stays with the source.
Queued source frames finish before new input enters the destination; refusal,
timeout or an unavailable owner before acceptance leaves the source usable.
After acceptance, the client waits for the source relay to consume queued input
and display acknowledgements before releasing that connection. A connection
failure during this final drain is reported and ends the terminal connection.
The destination publishes its live attachment after the frontend confirms that
drain and binds to it; new input and audio activation wait for this acknowledgement.
With voice active, this command carries its public conversation and requested
on or muted state. Voice requests use the same command and confirmation rules.
The microphone pauses while history is prepared. The destination uses its own
audio/provider settings and credentials, and starts after the source has retired
its media. Copied originals keep their source identities; queued work and late
source results stay with the source session. The destination's previous voice
conversation is replaced by the carried conversation, followed by current
destination context.
Preparation failure keeps the source attachment and resumes its voice when
still enabled. Before the transfer offer is sent, /voice off cancels preparation
immediately. Afterward, a successful switch leaves voice off in the destination.
Mute changes applied before source release determine the destination's initial microphone state.
An incompatible private-protocol version is refused before acceptance. Use a
compatible binary to attach to an older running owner.
.TP
.BR "/session name NAME" , " /s name NAME"
Save or replace the current session's name, including while work is active.
The remaining text is the name; shell-style quotes are unnecessary here.
The name is retained across detach and restart. Use -N NAME with
--attach or --resume to select it from the CLI.
.TP
.BR "/session detach" , " /session d" , " /s d"
Return to the shell while the native session continues. Terminal loss has
the same lifetime behavior. Detach prints a copyable --resume command for that
session; --attach also reconnects. Use /exit to stop the session.
File attachment commands /attach and /detach keep their existing meaning.
An unfinished file exchange is cancelled when its terminal disconnects;
reattachment restores ordinary input and verifies the new workstation client.
.TP
.B /state
Show session state including the goal and its actions: the
.B /status
report, the durable goal with status and blocker, and the available goal
actions.
.B /goal
is an alias for
.BR /state " goal" .
.TP
.BR /history " [N]"
Show up to N recent turns, oldest first, including unfinished, failed and interrupted
work. Default: 1. Available during active work. N is a nonnegative decimal count;
the bounded scan may show fewer turns or a marked partial oldest turn. Zero shows
the total turn count without scanning. Completion counts cover only shown turns.
Older original events remain available through read_session_history. Uses the same replay as
.B resume_history_turns
without changing configuration or model context.
.TP
.BR /model " [list]"
Read the local provider/model catalog.  No provider request is made.  On an
interactive terminal the displayed catalogue pages through the configured
.BR pager .
The catalog is loaded at startup and by
.BR /configure ;
ordinary requests, context meters and status
use that in-memory copy.  Model commands check for a changed cache file and
load it only when changed.  Missing or invalid replacements preserve the last
valid copy.  Selecting a numbered row through the model tool uses its listed
snapshot; an explicit cache refresh obtains a new one.
Startup also loads the catalog when resuming with the saved model.  If a saved
context selection requires unavailable catalog facts, the idle meter shows
.BR ?% .
Use
.B /model cache
or change the context selection before submitting work that needs those facts.
.TP
.B /model cache
Refresh every configured provider through its authenticated model endpoint,
replace the cache atomically, and report the new timestamp without printing
the catalogue; use
.B /model list
to display it.  A normalized API path ending in
.B /backend-api/codex
uses
.BR /models?client_version=0.159.2 ;
other providers use
.BR /v1/models .
There is no automatic catalog refresh or protocol fallback.  Failure leaves
the previous cache intact.  During active work the refresh starts at the next
safe request boundary.  See
.BR "Model selection and context" .
.TP
.BR /model " " number ", " /model " #" number
Select an exact displayed provider/model/effort row.
.TP
.BI /model " selector" " [save|s]"
Select
.IR model ,
.IR "model / effort" ,
or
.IR "provider / model / effort" .
A final
.B save
or
.B s
writes the selection to the active configuration.  Typed model IDs need not
exist in the cache.  Wrap a component in matching single or double quotes for
an ID containing
.BR / ,
as with
.BR \-m ;
a numbered row avoids selector ambiguity too.
Selection is accepted during active work and changes the next response in that
turn, then later turns until changed. An in-progress streaming request is
interrupted; the new provider request receives the retained conversation and
tool results, including still-running command handles. It persists across
session resume without a save suffix. After a credential error, selecting another
configured provider continues the same unfinished turn using that provider's login.
The final save suffix also
works with numbered rows. A bare model name uses the highest recognized effort
in its configured or cached effort list (or the first unrecognized effort if none are ranked),
then the cached default, then the currently selected effort. This interactive
selection differs from the startup
.B \-m
option, which uses the first configured or cached effort. Two components mean model/effort;
include all three components to name a provider explicitly.
.TP
.BR /fast " [on|off|status]"
Toggle fast service and print the resulting ON or OFF state. The optional
.B on
and
.B off
set it explicitly;
.B status
shows the current selection. The selected model and reasoning effort stay the
same. ON requests the priority service tier; OFF requests standard processing.
The selection applies to subsequent Responses requests, including compaction,
and survives session resume and configuration reload. A request already in
flight continues with its admitted tier.
.sp
Until this command sets a tier, requests retain the provider's default behavior.
Fast service availability and pricing depend on the provider and model; a
provider can refuse or downgrade the request. The confirmation reports the
requested mode. Use
.B /status
to inspect it. Sessions that have used this command require a reader with
service-tier support when resumed.
.TP
.B /configure
Reload the saved configuration, provider credentials, redaction secrets and local
model cache for this session owner at a safe request boundary. Preserve the
selected provider, model and effort. Validation failures leave the current
settings, credentials and cache active. A missing cache retains the last valid
copy; an invalid cache rejects the reload. The command reloads saved values even
when the configuration file is unchanged. Use
.B /model cache
to fetch a new catalog from providers. Stop local audio before reloading.
.TP
.B /config
Open the active configuration with
.BR EDITOR .
Valid changes are reloaded; invalid content remains on disk
while the prior runtime configuration stays active.  Only IRC components changed
since the last successful file load override current process state.  An unrelated
edit preserves runtime additions/removals; editing the client list replaces that
list.  Startup networking flags are not reapplied over deliberately edited fields.
Only affected owners restart.  Failed restoration reports actual remaining roles.
Reload preserves the process's current verbosity and view.  During active work
it starts at a safe request boundary.  The editor owns terminal input while
managed processes and IRC continue to be serviced.  If the program dies during
editing, resume reloads the saved file without launching the editor again.
Presentation and changed network roles apply on reload; provider connection,
authentication, capacity and tool defaults apply to later requests or tool
invocations.  Already launched processes retain their own settings.  The current
turn's working-document paths, read-only restrictions, parallel-call contract
and automatic retry budget remain bound to that turn. Model and effort
selections may change at a safe response boundary in the same turn. Update checking
is a startup action; update settings apply on the next program launch.
.TP
.BR /effort " [" level "]"
Show or set reasoning effort for the next request, including during active work.
The session retains it until changed, across later turns and resume.
Values are provider-defined; the host accepts structurally valid names rather
than enforcing a fixed list. The value
.B default
resolves to
.B medium
when used for a turn.
.TP
.BR /context " [default|max|N [s|save]]"
Show or set the context window for the selected model.  A bare
.B /context
reports the selection with the window, its output reservation, the effective
client percentage and the auto-compaction budget.
.B default
uses the configured model-limit window, or the provider's advertised normal
working window when no context override is configured,
.B max
uses the advertised maximum, and an integer
.I N
selects an explicit total window of N tokens; it is refused above the
advertised maximum or when the output reservation leaves no input.  The
selection overrides a configured model-limit context for the session, is
recorded in the session log, and is restored on resume.  The output
reservation, client percentage and proactive compaction stay derived from the
selected window, so shrinking it compacts before the next request.  During
active work the command ends the current response and rebuilds the turn under
the new window, retaining conversation and completed tool results.
.IP
Append
.B s
or
.B save
to a numeric selection, for example
.BR "/context 900000 save" ,
to also save
.B context_window_tokens
in the selected provider/model's exact
.B [model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL]
section of the active configuration. Other settings and file permissions are
preserved. The saved window applies to new sessions using that model and to
.B /context default
in this process. A numeric selection without the suffix changes only the
current session. Invalid values or a configuration save failure leave the
session selection unchanged. Save requires a number; edit the configuration
with
.B /config
to remove a saved override.
.TP
.BR /nick " [" NICK "]"
Show the model and operator nicks, or rename your operator nick on the selected
IRC destination. For example, /nick alice changes your name to alice.
In a connection or conversation buffer, inspection and changes use its exact
operator connection. A stale connection is rejected. From rollout, inspection
shows the primary identities and changes retain the selected destination scope.
Other clients see the accepted change, which also updates your prompt.
The primary connection's accepted operator name is saved for session resume.
The config file keeps the configured default.
Names follow IRC nick rules; invalid names are rejected and a server refusal
arrives in the connection view. A refused name leaves your accepted identity
usable for subsequent messages.
The model changes its own identity through the irc_nick tool.
.TP
.BR /steering " [mentions|all|clear]"
Show or set which IRC traffic reaches the model as steering: only mentions, or
every message. A session override wins over configuration until cleared;
clear returns to configuration. Without configuration the default admits
mentions only.
.TP
.BR /banner " [TEXT|clear]"
Show, set, or clear the session banner: a short work cursor the model maintains
for itself. The banner is restated in later requests, so it survives compaction
and is present at the next turn. Keep it brief and specific to the ongoing work.
.TP
.BR /verbose " [0..6]"
Show or set this process's current verbosity immediately, even during active
work (Enter or active-turn Tab).  Subsequent output uses the new level;
already visited detail is not replayed. Idle queries use the configured pager;
active-turn queries and all level changes stay in the terminal. It is local
feedback, never chat or model input. The current level is saved on orderly exit
and restored on resume unless explicit -v flags select another level.
.TP
.B /compact
Compact model context at idle or at a safe active-turn request boundary.
The request survives session resume and waits when no complete context boundary
is available.  The full local event log is retained.
.TP
.B /delete
Confirm with the displayed session prefix, then permanently remove the
session through private trash staging.  Reopening an interrupted deletion
finishes cleanup, including after the event log has been removed; unexpected
trash content is preserved and reported.  Shared prompt history is not erased.
Commands at the confirmation prompt remain commands rather than confirmation
text.  Ctrl-C cancels deletion; pending controls can then run.  Opening the queue
editor cancels deletion and retains the queued item.  /exit preserves the session
and leaves it available to resume.
.TP
.B /exit
Preserve the session and exit.  Active work remains available for same-turn
continuation on resume.  Ctrl-C instead records deliberate cancellation.
.SS Yielding tool waits
.TP
.B /yield
Return the active managed-tool batch to the model immediately. The result
identifies the operator as the source of the handoff. Started commands keep
their handles, pending input and captured output; unstarted calls are marked
not_run. The handoff sends no signal and does not restart commands. A previously
requested termination continues in the background and is reported as pending.
The model can inspect the results and choose its next action.
.PP
Available in both views while a managed-tool wait owns the foreground,
including termination waits and prompt transitions, even without a steer
prompt. Outside a tool wait
the command reports that none is active;
it does not queue a future yield or interrupt a provider request.
.SS Attachments
.TP
.B /dictate
Start one microphone interval, up to 60 seconds, using the configured capture
device. Requires a raw interactive terminal. Uses the selected provider's
credentials and transcription defaults; [audio] can override them.
The MIC ON prompt appears before capture starts.
Enter finishes; Escape or Ctrl-C cancels. Capture also finishes at the limit.
The microphone closes before transcription is requested. While transcribing,
Escape cancels; a request already sent may have been billed and is never
retried automatically. Keyboard editing remains available. Submission and
queueing are held until dictation finishes; the transcript is inserted at the
cursor, preserving typed text. Review it before pressing Enter to submit.
Temporary PCM is bounded, discarded on completion/cancel, and never saved as
an attachment or journalled. Returned API usage is displayed and retained in an
audio_usage journal event with its provider, model and operation. It is separate
from coding token counters and model input; unreported usage remains unknown.
A journal write failure is reported and never triggers another paid request.
Device loss, rerouting or capture backlog overflow
stops dictation and discards the sound.
Native terminal detach, loss, switching away or suspension also cancels
dictation. A late transcription is not inserted after the attachment changes;
a request already sent may still have been billed.
.TP
.B /voice devices
List available capture and playback device names without starting capture.
Set capture_device and playback_device in [audio] to an exact listed name;
empty values select system defaults. Ambiguous duplicate names are rejected.
Stop local audio before reloading configuration or preparing attachments.
.TP
.B /voice on
Start a voice conversation using the selected provider and its credentials.
Credential loading and refresh run in the voice connection worker while ordinary
input and coding work continue. The microphone stays off during startup;
.B /voice off
cancels a pending credential wait or connection attempt.
Direct Codex subscriptions and codex-lb use native subscription voice; compatible
BYOK providers use their Realtime API. A codex-lb gateway must use its
/backend-api/codex base for native voice; a bare /v1 base does not select it.
Realtime follows the configured URL: HTTPS uses a certificate-verified secure
WebSocket, and HTTP uses an unencrypted WebSocket. HTTP sends credentials and
voice control traffic without TLS; use HTTPS or a secure tunnel outside a
trusted network.
Native voice resolves credentials at startup and uses the same credential for
call setup and attachment. A configured gateway key needs no separate login.
Native audio playback runs independently of caption timing; interrupting a reply
clears queued speech while keeping the connection ready for the next reply.
Native ASR and generated captions update as transcript segments arrive, with a
separate preview for each speaker. Finalized transcripts retain their utterance
identities; partial captions do not submit coding work.
Conversation continues while accepted coding requests are queued or running.
Ask for work status or quieter narration in ordinary speech. Narration preferences
apply to spoken updates; work cancellation and microphone mute have separate controls.
The session interface can read files, inspect current work, steer an exact active
turn, queue a separate task, request turn cancellation, select a cached model,
or change voice capture mode. Stale turn targets are refused without changing
another turn. File changes and command execution run through the coding agent.
Executable tasks can be described directly, including work outside the CLI's
slash-command help. Relayed requests preserve the original transcript and mark
the voice interface's interpretation separately from working-model results.
Each result keeps its original request identity. The live connection tracks up to eight outstanding
handoffs. At capacity, native voice reports that a new handoff was not submitted;
public Realtime temporarily withholds the session tool. Conversation and accepted
work continue. A slow result consumer delays delivery without stopping voice.
[audio] settings are optional overrides.
The prompt identifies microphone capture. Before capture starts, voice restores
prior public working history, speech and interface outcomes from the saved session
as identified historical observations. The microphone stays off until the initial
history and fresh-state transport writes finish. Reply completion during this wait
leaves device activation pending. Large histories can delay this stage;
/voice off cancels it while coding work continues. Saved actions and audio
are not replayed. Current speech already belongs to the live provider conversation.
Microphone audio goes to the provider's media servers and replies
are synthesized speech. Use a headset to limit speaker feedback alongside software
echo cancellation. Keyboard editing and ordinary submission remain available.
.PP
Capture and incoming reply audio stream concurrently through bounded PCM rings.
The current endpoint uses server-detected speech turns. Reply generation can start
from committed audio before its separate transcription completes. Delegations
wait for their matching finalized transcript. The interface uses separate text
requests with the selected session provider and model, the session working
directory, and existing effective instruction paths. These requests add ordinary
provider usage and run independently of media and the coding request. The session
owner validates tool actions and records their outcomes. Queued work and accepted
steering are acknowledged separately from completed coding results. An unavailable
transcript prevents action admission. Final transcripts and provider-reported usage are retained
separately from coding token counts; raw microphone PCM is not journalled.
Settled interface text requests also record provider/model identity, reported
cached tokens when available, retries, and elapsed, response-ready and first-text
times. Missing observations remain unknown. These client timings include
authentication, transport and retries; they do not isolate provider prefill time.
The records are available through session history. Turning voice off records the
settlement of an outstanding interface request and marks its response as discarded.
Received public output and pending handoff identities remain in history; shutdown
executes no returned tool calls and leaves accepted coding work under the existing
session controls. Interface text requests use their own stable prompt-cache and
transport-affinity identity, separate from working-model requests. Connection
renewal retains the outstanding helper request.
Provider error records retain validated error codes, categories, available messages
and reported context/input counts through the same secret-filtered history path.
Validated native call HTTP rejections also retain status and accepted Retry-After
hints. Partial or invalid metadata cannot publish partial failure facts.
Missing counts remain unknown. A generic error message does not establish the
voice model's context capacity. Recognized temporary failures renew automatically
with the pacing described below. Classified capacity rejections reduce history
before replacement. Other errors stop voice for review before
restarting with /voice on.
.PP
While voice remains enabled, the text interface retains its ordered dialogue and
tool outcomes across spoken handoffs. Its command/help prefix stays in place,
and each ordinary request receives a fresh session-state snapshot, including
requests that continue after a tool action.
Public history and returned replies filter registered secrets, including
credentials reflected in provider output. This protection follows credentials
used by interface requests when a credential file changes during voice use,
including tokens refreshed inside the HTTP transport and responses retained
during shutdown.
History filtering precedes JSON quoting and excerpting, including for old records
whose text predates a configured secret. Saved records stay unchanged.
Before its first delegation, the text interface reads working inputs, public
output and outcomes from session history. It appends newly recorded working
events before subsequent ordinary requests. This catches up in bounded steps
while coding work continues; each observation identifies its session and source
sequence. Pending audio delivery does not hold up this text-history catch-up.
Both native voice observations and text history include command lifecycle,
goal wording/lock/cancellation changes and model-selection changes.
Recorded native voice transcripts and deferred spoken outcomes are also appended
before ordinary text-interface requests, with their source speaker and identities.
When the provider reports exhausted input, or request serialization reaches its
staging limit, the interface summarizes older context and retries the pending
request. If that prefix reduction is insufficient, recovery includes the active
dialogue as well. Summary requests declare no action tools. A rejected summary input is
retried in smaller source fragments with interruptible pacing. Summary adoption
is recorded before replacing the live history; a failed summary leaves the
source history available. These requests add provider usage separately from
coding totals. Turning voice off releases the live text history. The next
text-interface connection restores prior public voice dialogue and actual
outcomes from the saved session as historical observations before handling new
input. This also applies after session reopen. Saved actions and audio are not replayed.
.PP
Native capacity recovery keeps the microphone off while existing helper work
settles. It summarizes the verified public history in bounded pages through a
fixed sequence boundary, carrying the summary between tool-free requests.
The complete summary must be smaller and saved before replacing any live history.
The replacement connection receives that summary, the unsummarized tail and
fresh session state. Original records and accepted coding work remain available;
summary work does not replay speech or admit historical actions.
Saved summaries pass through the current credential filter before restoration
or reuse by another recovery.
Turning voice off cancels maintenance and retains received public output without
adopting a partial summary. Received public output is saved within the session's
journal record limits even when it exceeds the live voice message budget.
A refused, empty, executable, oversized or nonreducing summary
stops voice with its history intact. Byte staging limits provide no model-window
estimate.
.PP
Read-only access uses get_cwd, list_files, read_file and grep with the same path
rules as coding tools. Spoken model selection uses the existing cached catalog;
catalog refresh remains a foreground session operation. Interface request failures
leave the voice connection active. Oversized spoken results are marked excerpts;
the saved session retains the full result. Voice has no separate memory directory
or documentation configuration.
Initial instructions include the same command and keyboard reference as /help.
The voice interface can enter slash commands and explicit replies to UI prompts
through the same input queue as the keyboard. The typed draft is preserved.
An acknowledgement reports admission; the command's normal output reports its
result or asks for confirmation. While voice is on, acknowledged host/help text,
errors, warnings and logical prompt changes enter its public history. UI-local
command feedback follows the same session-owner history path. Registered secrets
are filtered; terminal frames and clock/spinner redraws are not copied. Both voice
histories consume the retained observations, including after reconnection. This
path does not reconstruct UI output from before voice activation.
Voice remains serviceable while a confirmation is open. Existing permissions
still apply, including keyboard-only consent where explicitly required.
A full input queue refuses admission. Input
from an ended attachment is rejected, including queued input not yet dispatched.
The voice interface can read older dialogue, controls and results with the same
paged session-history tool used by the model. History inspection keeps existing
work running and admits no new task.
Session inspection includes paged queue identities, read-only flags and current
text previews of up to 512 bytes. Pages default to 20 entries and support up to
100; continuation follows the immutable enqueue sequence, so removing earlier
entries preserves the cursor. Each page reflects current queue state. Clipped
previews are marked, and inspection leaves queue arming and execution unchanged.
While voice is enabled, new model output, tool outcomes and control changes are
forwarded in journal order as background context. Larger records continue in
bounded fragments. These observations do not start model work or request speech.
Textual host context, including initial and refreshed snapshots, is filtered for
configured secrets before delivery to the voice provider.
Context snapshots identify the current session-state sequence separately from
the historical sequence they have verified. History catches up in bounded steps;
an incomplete historical view is marked and continues on later service steps.
The latest speaker excerpts include their journal sequences for older-detail
retrieval. Updating this view leaves the working model and queue unchanged.
Voice activity persists the session even before the first model turn. Voice
interface actions appear as tools labelled "voice:" at verbosity 1 and above;
verbosity 2 adds the ordinary argument and result previews. Each action's
invocation is saved before execution, followed by its acknowledged outcome.
.PP
Playback initially waits for 60 ms of sound. A confirmed mid-reply gap increases
this prefill by 20 ms, up to 200 ms; eight clean replies reduce it by 20 ms toward
the initial value. Storage and microphone chunk size remain fixed. An audio-end
marker releases short replies and tails immediately. Prefill waiting is bounded
at 200 ms after the first queued sample; prolonged provider or network stalls can
still produce silence. Received captions do not prove the corresponding sound
was heard. Speech interruption flushes queued playback and reports a conservative
played position to the provider.
Native media waits up to 60 ms for a missing packet to arrive out of order.
After that deadline, a known burst of lost packets is concealed together;
each missing packet does not add another wait.
Once capture is active, queued context or result messages do not pause native
microphone forwarding: its media transport is separate from the control socket.
Duplex capture reduces speaker echo using the samples actually rendered as its
reference. The microphone remains available during replies. Echo reduction
depends on the devices and room; headphones can help when speaker audio still
reaches speech recognition.
.TP
.B /voice mute, /voice unmute
Pause or resume microphone forwarding. Unmute discards old capture samples,
including a partial processing frame, and resets the echo filter.
Native voice also clears encoder lookahead at mute and preserves the muted
interval in subsequent audio timestamps. Audio already transmitted
cannot be recalled. Speaker output and coding work
are independent of microphone mute.
The prompt shows starting or muting until the device owner applies the change.
Duplex startup gates forwarding until old capture is discarded; a mute during
startup cannot later release buffered speech. A rapid mute/unmute still clears
the old input before fresh capture is admitted.
.TP
.B /voice off
Stop capture, playback and the voice connection. Accepted coding work stays with
the existing session owner. Use the normal coding controls to interrupt that work.
An advertised connection lifetime ending or a validated temporary provider-service
error starts paced, cancellable renewal. DNS, connection and timeout failures before
an HTTP response, abrupt WebSocket EOF and stalled outbound writes also renew.
Native call setup renews recognized temporary HTTP rejections after validating
the complete error body. Context-capacity rejections first reduce the retained
history; policy rejections stop voice. Accepted Retry-After hints delay replacement;
/voice off cancels that wait.
The microphone stays off during replacement
authentication and history restoration. Requested mute state, the interface helper
and accepted coding work survive; old audio and native call results are discarded
from the transport. Late helper outcomes keep their original source and reach the
new connection as historical context. Voice-off also cancels a pending renewal.
Device loss, overflow, authentication failure, unclassified HTTP or policy rejection, TLS
failures, WebSocket close frames, protocol-integrity errors and other unclassified
connection failures stop voice. Review the diagnostic and restart explicitly with
/voice on.
Native terminal loss, detach or job suspension also stops voice. Final transcripts
and previously accepted coding work remain saved; unaccepted voice handoffs are
discarded. Ordinary reattachment requires an explicit /voice on. An intentional
/session attach while voice is active carries its conversation and requested
mode through the attachment exchange described above.
When the provider supplies a session expiry time, a warning appears within its
last minute.
Final notices already received are saved on shutdown and connection failure.
Turning voice off does not accept a handoff that has not yet entered the coding
queue. A journal write failure is reported; unsaved notices cannot be recovered.
Repeated handoffs for an accepted utterance retain the original queue and turn
identity. Queued or running work remains pending; completed, failed, interrupted
or cancelled work returns its recorded outcome without another execution.
On explicit start, voice receives excerpts of the latest finalized input and
generated reply transcripts, the latest voice handoff's recorded state/result,
and the active-turn identity and queue count. Excerpts are limited to 8 KiB each
and marked when shortened. Task-state changes refresh that context. Historical
text does not resubmit work or replay speech; a result from an older connection
is context, not a function output for a new call.
.PP
Partial input captions and generated reply captions update above the composer
when supplied by the provider. They show recent text and never alter the draft
or authorize coding work. Final transcripts use the durable session record.
Native transcript streams take precedence over turn-level previews, so the
accumulated text is neither replaced nor duplicated.
The rollout labels finalized microphone transcripts as "You [voice, ASR]" and
voice-model replies as "Voice model [generated]" at every verbosity level.
The labels remain with the text in scrollback. Partial captions distinguish
the same speakers above the composer.
Stopping voice clears these previews. Generated captions may precede audible
playback and do not indicate that the words were heard.
.PP
Realtime voice is a development checkpoint. Device lifecycle and portable
runtime qualification remain in progress. Native fixture tests do not qualify
physical devices or live APIs.
.TP
.BI /play " asset:ID"
Play the first 60 seconds (or until EOF) of an accepted audio asset in the
current session, using the configured playback device. Other file paths and
unaccepted attachments are rejected. Linked decoding pulls bounded stereo PCM
chunks into the shared output ring; it does not buffer the entire selection.
Playback opens no microphone or API connection. Keyboard input remains usable.
Device loss or decoding failure stops playback; already played samples cannot
be rolled back. Backend latency can delay the end of audible sound.
Native terminal detach, loss, switching away or suspension stops playback;
the accepted source asset remains in the session.
.TP
.B /play stop
Interrupt file playback. Dictation and file playback share one local device
owner and return busy when the other operation is active.
.TP
.B /receive
Receive workstation files as unsent attachments through the trzsz wrapper.
See Terminal file transfers for setup, drag behavior, verification and limits.
.TP
.B /send PATH
Send one regular file or retained asset to the workstation through the trzsz
wrapper. The remainder is a literal path, including spaces; no shell expansion,
quotes or globbing are interpreted. See Terminal file transfers.
.TP
.BI /attach " path"
Prepare an image or retain a regular file for the next
private message, queued turn or steering input.  The entire remainder is a literal path, including spaces: no shell
expansion, quotes or globbing are interpreted.  Relative paths use the current working
directory; symlinks are rejected. Recognized documents, audio and video retain
their format; other non-image bytes are kept as a generic file reference.
The input prompt shows the attachment count.
.TP
.B /attachments
List unsent attachments by number, format, size and retained asset identifier.
.TP
.BI /detach " number|all"
Remove an unsent attachment from the composer.  This does not remove media already
accepted by a previous turn.  The source file is never modified.
.PP
Enter in rollout submits text and attachments together; Tab or /queue preserves
them with that queued input.  Steering carries the same parts through restart
and compaction.  Automatic goal and IRC turns never consume a private draft.
Chat messages remain text-only; staging an attachment does not broadcast it.
Queued text edits preserve that turn's accepted attachments.  Use /detach before
submission to change the selection; unsent drafts are not restored on restart.
The configured model/provider receives image bytes only when that input is sent.
Other file types carry a retained reference, not their bytes, until explicitly
inspected.  A recognized extension is routing metadata, not proof the file is
valid or supported by a decoder.  At most eight attachments may be staged;
non-image source files are limited to 256 MiB each.
Current image bounds and provider requirements are listed under Image inspection.
.SS Read-only queries
.TP
.BI /ro " query"
Run one read-only query, including multiline text. Every request carries the
complete native tool catalog. A read-only query permits inspection and supported
provider web-search work; a call that would perform a side effect returns a
read-only tool result and performs none. Normal execution permissions apply on
the next ordinary turn. In networked mode the query opens rollout view and sends
no IRC reply.
During active work, Enter and Tab queue the read-only query as a future full
turn.  You can also use
.B /queue /ro query
to run it after the current turn.  Ordinary steering inside a read-only turn
cannot lift its restrictions.  Empty queries are rejected;
.B //ro
is literal text.  One-shot argument and stdin prompts also support this prefix.
.TP
.B /retry
After a turn fails, continue it from retained conversation and tool results.
The original prompt is not duplicated and completed tools are not replayed.
Read-only mode is preserved.  Continuation of an unfinished turn keeps its
frozen provider/model/effort; a new retry turn uses current next-turn settings.
Available in both views and after session resume, until another turn starts.
For an unfinished active turn, request continuation at the next safe boundary.
Pending input that failed preparation can also be retried.  Explicitly cancelled
turns stay cancelled; retained completed or uncertain tool calls are never
blindly executed again.  Paused queues and goals remain paused.
With automatic retry enabled, ordinary turns retry failures up to
.B max_turn_retries
additional times (default five), then display the manual retry hint. A repeated identical definitive provider rejection fails at once instead of replaying.
Automatic retry counts and their original limit survive restart.  A successful
actionable response or manual retry after terminal failure starts a fresh budget.
With automatic retry enabled, active goals recover ordinary failures without an attempt limit.  Same-process
recovery retains live command handles; after restart their outcomes are settled
without reusing old handles.  Policy stops survive restart and require clarification.
.TP
.B /retry auto [on|off]
Toggle automatic transport and turn retries, or set them explicitly. The command
prints ON or OFF and saves a session override that survives restart. The default
comes from [agent] retry_auto=true. /status shows the value and its source.
An explicit /configure reload changes the default when no session override exists.
Disabling during backoff stops the next attempt. Healthy responses finish.
A failed goal keeps its objective and status; view commands and enabling retry
alone do not restart it. Use /retry or submit new work to continue.
Queued work, steering, cancellation and policy stops retain their admission rules.
.SS Input timestamps
New prompts and steers carry separate host-generated timing metadata in model
context.  The original user text and its authority remain unchanged.
.B received_at
records acceptance of the submitted input;
.B first_context_at
records its first admission to an outgoing model request, including an attempted
dispatch that fails.  These UTC timestamps remain fixed through retries and
replay.  Queue delays can make first admission later than receipt; an edit uses
its accepted revision's receipt time.  Metadata is explicitly labelled as host
text, and does not claim provider acceptance or model processing.
Older missing timing data remains unavailable rather than being inferred from
replay time.  A submitted editor line retains its original local prompt clock;
queued dispatch renders a new normal prompt with the dispatch-time local clock.
UTC provenance and the displayed clock describe different events.
.SS Goals
An active goal continues across ordinary turns until complete, paused,
cancelled, or blocked; there is no default per-goal-turn working or step budget.
Queued operator turns run before the next automatic
goal turn.  Ordinary errors retain the active goal and schedule recovery with
interruptible backoff, without an attempt limit.  Policy stops and provider
refusals pause automatic continuation and require clarification.  Turn-only
cancellation also pauses automatic continuation.  Quitting preserves unfinished
work and its goal for resume.  Session resume preserves saved stops.
Goal status changes update the prompt indicator before their notices are shown,
including pauses, completion and blocking. Clearing a draft in the interactive
composer leaves its active goal running; Ctrl-C on an empty composer interrupts
the turn and pauses that goal.
Goal controls accept whitespace around the command word.  Extra wording after a
control is rejected; use quotes or
.B /state goal set
to start goal text with a command word.
.TP
.BR /state " goal, " /state " goal status"
Show the durable goal, blocker and recorded wait channel with its actions.
Queued prompts run before automatic
goal continuation,
without fresh goal reminders throughout those turns.  A retained unarmed queue
cannot be bypassed: use
.B /next
or explicit goal start/resume to arm it.  An open queue editor prevents draining.
.B /goal
is an alias for
.BR /state " goal" .
.TP
.BI /state " goal text"
Start a goal, or reword the unfinished goal.  Quote the text or use
.B /state goal set
when its first word is a goal command.
.TP
.BI /state " goal set text"
Explicitly start or reword a goal.
.TP
.B /state goal pause
Pause automatic continuation at the current turn boundary.
.TP
.B /state goal resume
Resume a paused or blocked goal.
.TP
.BR /state " goal lock|unlock"
Freeze the goal's objective against model changes, or release it.  A locked
goal refuses the model's rewording, blocking and cancelling, in the tool before
dispatch and again in the store wherever the event names the model as actor,
while finishing or resuming a locked goal stays allowed, so it can still reach
a successful end; the operator's own commands remain available.
.TP
.B /state goal complete
Mark the current goal successfully completed.
.TP
.BR /state " goal clear|cancel"
Cancel the current goal and stop automatic continuation.  An in-progress turn
finishes normally; use Ctrl-C to interrupt it.  The cancelled goal remains
visible in status and history.  A new goal can be started afterwards.
.TP
.B /state goal help
Show the complete goal grammar.
.SS Queue
.TP
.BR /queue , " /q"
List future FIFO turns.
.TP
.BI /queue " text"
Queue a future full turn.  During active work it is armed to follow the current
turn; idle additions remain paused until
.BR /next .
Tab during active work is the direct equivalent for a draft.
.TP
.BR /queue " clear|pop, " /q " c|p"
Remove all queued turns or the newest one.
.TP
.BI /queue " n delete, " /q " n" d
Delete queued item
.IR n .
.TP
.BI /queue " n edit, " /q " n" e
Reopen queued item
.I n
in the composer.
.TP
.B /next
Durably arm the queue.  Start the oldest entry at idle, or after the current
full turn when work is active.  Arming survives exit and restart.
.SS Network
All network commands are discoverable and usable at idle and during provider
streaming, silence, retries, token counting and running tools.  They are local
controls, not model input, steering, cancellation or future turns.  Endpoints use
the same syntax as startup flags; an omitted endpoint defaults to localhost:6667.
.TP
.BR /server , " /server start " [ENDPOINT], " /server stop"
Report hosting, start it, or stop only the hosted listener and accepted peers.
Outgoing connections remain unchanged.  Repeating the current listener is a
no-op; replacing it requires an explicit stop first.  Bind failure leaves the
session and unrelated endpoints usable.  Hosting publishes only matching public
room history, never private rollout or another room's history.
.TP
.BR /connect " " [ENDPOINT]
Add an outgoing connection, up to 16.  A duplicate is a no-op, including while
reconnecting.  Connecting is asynchronous and is not reported as a completed join.
.TP
.BR /disconnect " " [ENDPOINT]
Remove the named outgoing endpoint, or all outgoing endpoints when omitted,
including connections created by model IRC tools. Hosting is unchanged. Stop
reconnect attempts and report discarded unsent bytes;
do not transfer them to another endpoint.  Already accepted model input remains
eligible after final disconnection.  Old model sends are not redirected into
replacement rooms; they receive normal not-performed tool results.
.TP
.BR /chat " [" address "]"
Select the current/default channel, including its retained history while offline.
An address selects a known operator channel without joining it. Use #channel,
endpoint/#channel, session/endpoint/#channel or channel:ID. A bare channel uses
the focused conversation's endpoint, or the sole endpoint from rollout.
Retained channels remain selectable during reconnect, including server-specific
channel prefixes.
.TP
.B /rollout
Select local model activity.
.TP
.BR /query " [" address " [" text "]]"
Open and select an operator private chat. Optional text sends once; opening
without text sends nothing. With no address, list existing operator queries
with their exact /query query:ID selectors. Address forms are nick,
endpoint/nick and session/endpoint/nick. Classic mode accepts this session's saved
name or matching ID prefix. A Vim workspace can address another attached session
by saved name or unique ID prefix. Endpoint accepts its configured spelling or destination
number. Bare nicks inherit the selected chat endpoint; from rollout they require
one endpoint. An ambiguous address sends nothing and asks for an explicit
endpoint. Quote an address containing spaces; encode literal slash and percent
in its components as %2F and %25. Nick comparison follows the endpoint's
advertised case mapping. A query:ID selects that retained conversation;
a stale conversation remains readable and rejects sends.
.TP
.BR /connections " [" session "|" address "]"
List connections, operator/agent status and channel/private conversations for
the selected session. An optional session name or ID prefix identifies the current
session in classic mode or an attached session in a Vim workspace. A bare number
selects a connection. Use /connections SESSION/ENDPOINT/ to open its connection view;
an endpoint spelling or destination number also selects it. The connection view
shows status and server notices for both local identities. It becomes an opened
classic tab, included in Tab/Shift-Tab cycling with its own draft. Incoming
connection notices preserve focus. Ordinary text requires /query, /chat or an
explicit /msg target and remains unsent in the draft.
.TP
.BR /whois " [" nick "]"
Request information on the selected operator connection. Without an argument,
use the selected private peer or your operator nick. From rollout, a single
connection is required. External server replies appear in the connection view;
open it with /connections ENDPOINT. The built-in server reports its registered
peer's nick, user and server directly. A disconnected or stale connection is
rejected. Server notices and WHOIS replies remain outside model context and
private-query history.
.TP
.BR /msg " " address " " text
Send a message as the operator, preserving the focused tab. Use the same
nick addresses or query:ID selectors as /query, or channel addresses and
channel:ID selectors for a joined operator channel. Sending never joins a channel. Text after the address is kept
literally, including #, slashes and quotes. Missing text sends nothing.
.TP
.BR /notice " " address " " text
Send a NOTICE as the operator, preserving focus. Uses /msg addressing.
.TP
.BR /me " " text
Send a CTCP action to the selected operator channel or query. Agent conversations
are read-only. /N and /all send ordinary messages to endpoint default rooms.
Incoming channel actions retain their action presentation in live chat and
replayed history.
.TP
.BR /join " " address
Join a channel as the operator and select its conversation. Uses /chat
addressing. The server must confirm membership before messages can be sent.
The built-in server accepts only its configured room and confirms local joins
immediately. The agent keeps its own memberships.
Channel names follow the server's advertised prefixes. Retained history for
those channels survives session resume, including prefixes such as & and $.
.TP
.BR /part " [" address " [" reason "]]"
Leave the selected or addressed operator channel. Queuing PART immediately
invalidates the old membership; a rejected stale send retains its draft. Rejoin
explicitly with /join after the server confirms PART. The built-in server
confirms local PART immediately and keeps its listener and agent running.
A resumed session preserves the operator's choice to leave. Closing a tab or
pane never leaves a channel.
.TP
.BR /topic " [" text "]"
In a channel conversation, show its cached topic and request a refresh, or
change the topic using the captured membership. The built-in server returns
its current state immediately. A private query reports the channel scope
requirement. From rollout, show the IRC snapshot or change
the endpoint default room topic. Live room
policy decides authorization: mode +t requires the local operator to have op
status; without +t any joined member may change it. Hosted topics accept at most
280 bytes. External topics must fit the server's line budget. Newlines are rejected.
.TP
.B /names
In a channel conversation, show its cached topic/member state and request a
NAMES refresh. Repeating /names displays the refreshed cache. The built-in
server returns its current members immediately. Private queries report the
channel scope requirement. From rollout, show numbered destinations, selection,
endpoints, members and operator flags.
.TP
.BR /N " [" text "]"
Select numbered destination N.  With text, send there once without changing
selection.  Numbers remain stable for this process and are not reused after
removal.  A number names the same endpoint across reconnects: when an endpoint
returns with a changed advertised room, or otherwise republishes its topology,
the selection follows that number instead of staying unavailable.  With a sole
destination the same syntax remains valid.
.TP
.B /all text
Broadcast once to the destinations present at submission.  This does not
enable a persistent broadcast mode.  A later connection does not receive it.
An unavailable destination is reported locally; messages are not redirected.
.SH "Models and providers"
.SS Model selection and context
The provider, model, and effort are session preferences.  A typed selector
without a provider stays on the currently selected provider.  An omitted
effort uses configuration.  The value
.B default
for the model resolves to the compiled default;
.B default
for effort resolves to
.BR medium .
Operator-typed /model and command-line -m send other nonblank bounded values
without checking a model catalog. The model's select_model tool is available when
[agent] allow_model_change=true and uses only
entries in the current cache.
.PP
Use
.B /model cache
to discover models and advertised capacities.  Listing the cache is offline;
there is no TTL and no dependency on an installed Codex.  Cached limits and
counting capabilities and observed ceilings apply only to their original provider, URL, protocol, and
model.  An explicit
.B [model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL]
overrides advertised capacities.  Missing capacities remain unknown.
.PP
The direct DeepSeek catalog currently supplies model IDs without capacity or
reasoning-effort metadata. Its Flash API model ID is
.BR deepseek-flash .
Configure its documented 1,048,576-token total context explicitly; this remains a
configuration override rather than an advertised cache value. For an existing
.B deepseek
provider, add:
.PP
.nf
[model-limit deepseek/deepseek-flash]
context_window_tokens = 1048576
.fi
.PP
Select it with
.B snajpagent \-m deepseek/deepseek-flash/high
or
.BR "/model deepseek/deepseek-flash/high" .
The direct API accepts xhigh as an alias for high. Refreshing the catalog visits
all providers in the loaded configuration, including those other than the selected
provider. Reload edited configuration with
.B /configure
or restart before refreshing or selecting a newly configured provider.
.PP
Token accounting prefers exact provider preflight when supported, then uses
completed request usage, source-bound observations, or conservative byte
bounds.  These methods are not interchangeable exact counts.
.B /status
shows the capacity source and accounting method.  Catalog refresh resets the
count-capability probe and preserves observations only for an identical source.
.PP
.B /status
also reports prompt-cache reuse and speed: input tokens split into cached and
uncached, the same totals for the current process beside them (program totals
restart with the process while session totals survive a resume), and tokens per
second for the running turn and for the most recent response.
.PP
Prompt-cache routing uses a stable session/provider/model identity. Input timing,
steering boundaries, recovery notices, response corrections and process closure
travel as labelled host conversation data. Current goal, display, command,
banner and work-note facts form a labelled snapshot at the request tail. Once
sent, that exact snapshot remains before its response in later requests;
unchanged snapshots are not duplicated and the latest snapshot supersedes
older facts. Fixed execution and
IRC policy remains separate, including through gateways that collect system
and developer instructions. Completed history retains its projected bytes
across turns until compaction. Instruction, model, tool-schema and compaction
changes can alter the reusable prefix. A changed snapshot can require one new
cache write; later requests extend that new prefix. Providers determine actual cache reuse.
The labels identify host metadata, not new operator requests or approvals.
Commands still require collection before a final answer or goal completion.

.PP
Automatic compaction uses the provider threshold and resolved input capacity;
setting the proactive threshold to zero does not disable capacity handling.
With a known hard input window, the default automatic threshold is 90% of that
window.  The prompt percentage shows measured input usage of the selected
window. An exact outgoing count refreshes the composer when the response attempt
starts, before provider output. Completed responses refresh it from measured
usage. Compaction and recovery rebases replace the measured context; the meter
shows unknown until a compatible measurement exists. Status retains a historical
observation only with an explicit label when provider source, model, effort or
compaction no longer matches. Context-selection advice uses the same compatibility
check. A matching last observation is not a measurement of later unsubmitted
tool output. Streaming output enters a later request when its input is measured.
The percentage can fall after a completed
compaction; each new attempt announces itself.  A long turn can compact again after a completed response and later
tool progress when its next request exceeds the hard budget.  A recovery rebase clears the
previous usage measurement until a new response supplies one.
Native compaction is used when enabled.  Set
.B native_compaction=false
to summarize through ordinary Responses; the application wraps the returned
summary text in its stored message format. Direct ChatGPT/Codex also falls back
once to Responses summarization when its native endpoint is unavailable;
failed native requests may retry with a smaller complete-history source within
the bounded attempt.  Failures that remain report an error and retain the
previous context.  Manual
.B /compact
is accepted at idle and during active work.  It reports when compaction starts,
waits for a complete context boundary, finishes, or is interrupted.  An idle
request with no new context reports that it was skipped.  Whitespace after the
command is accepted; additional arguments are rejected.
.PP
An active goal keeps retrying failed compaction with paced, interruptible waits,
including after eight consecutive failures. Each retry can attempt compaction
again. Ordinary turns retain the eight-failure bound; fresh input or
.B /compact
starts another attempt. Successful compaction clears that failure count,
including compaction performed after a turn.
.PP
A summary survives a model, provider, effort or account change.  A switch by
itself does not start compaction.  When the selected binding needs a smaller
context, that binding keeps the covered boundary, leads its source with the
retained summary as plain text, and compacts only the uncovered tail.  The
previous binding is not selected again to prepare the new binding's request.
Failed or cancelled turns and process exit retain the selection and compaction
progress; resume continues from that durable state without replaying completed
tools.  When a provider rejects a request for capacity and no uncovered events
remain, the merged summary is condensed once under the selected binding and the
turn is retried; the covered boundary and predecessor chain stay unchanged.
.PP
Automatic and manual compaction announce that context is being compacted.
During compaction, Ctrl-C with an empty draft interrupts preparation or the provider request; a
nonempty draft is cleared first.  Cancelling a turn does not cancel a later
manual compaction.  Text entered during idle compaction is saved as a future
turn and runs afterwards; during active-turn compaction it remains steering.
Interrupted or failed compaction retains the previous context.  Provider errors
leave the interactive session open; use
.B /compact
to retry. Policy rejections remain on the policy path even when their metadata
also contains a context-limit code; they do not trigger capacity recovery.
Accepted requests survive a process restart. Compaction changes
model context and preserves the append-only local log.
.PP
The versioned session checkpoint is a record in the same journal.  It stores
both recoverable session state and the materialized provider conversation, with
the summary and only the uncovered continuation after successful compaction.
On resume, the final complete journal record locates the latest checkpoint;
recovery validates that record and the recent suffix.  Format-2 and format-3
sessions with a long history require one initial scan to gain an indexed checkpoint, without
rewriting old history.  Checkpoints from before the download outbox feature
resume with an empty outbox.  A damaged referenced checkpoint stops recovery with an
error rather than silently starting a full-history replay.  Explicit requests
for old history still read those older records.  The session lock, metadata and
retained media are not alternate checkpoint files.
Cache recovery uses the same checkpoint and recent suffix whenever a provider
view has been saved.
Targeted IRC source and history lookups seek past unrelated checkpoint bodies.
The selected boundary and returned records retain full hash verification;
seeking is not a whole-journal integrity scan.
.PP
Development IRC records named
.B irc_event_v2
carry connection, conversation and local-identity metadata. Their sessions use
checkpoint snapshot revision 3. Earlier binaries reject these records and
checkpoints; retain a matching reader for such journals. Existing sessions keep
their current checkpoint revision until a new-format IRC event is recorded.
Installing a binary or opening a Vim workspace leaves existing journals in
their current format. Live direct-message routing remains under development.
.PP
Repeated request preparation and reopening preserve one copy of the compaction
or recovery prefix. Retry notices continue from the saved attempt count;
older cached views rebuild from their retained events.
Journal growth is limited by available storage and representable file offsets,
rather than a lifetime byte or event quota. Existing sessions that reached the
former closure reserve can continue with an updated executable; keep their
journals intact. Per-record bounds and durable append validation still apply.
Cancelled request preparation can be retried or resumed from a saved checkpoint;
partially prepared context is rebuilt from retained events without duplicating
the conversation.
.PP
If compaction leaves only instructions, outgoing requests include a labelled
host continuation so gateways can keep a nonempty conversation input.  It adds
no user instruction or approval and does not change saved goal state.  Failed
compaction attempts are recorded before recovery so the session can reopen.
.SS Login
Common options such as
.BR --dotdir ,
.BR --config ,
and
.B -m
must precede the login/logout command.
.TP
.BI "login " [provider]
Authenticate a configured provider or choose one of the
.BR codex ,
.BR meta ,
.BR openrouter ,
.BR openai ,
and
.B custom
presets.  Existing environment authentication remains explicit; there is no
fallback to another provider or authentication method.  Login does not change
the default model in an existing configuration.
.TP
.B --openai-device-auth
Use the native device flow of a direct Codex provider.  Open the
printed verification page and confirm or enter the one-time code.  ChatGPT
device login must be enabled in the ChatGPT account or workspace.  Waiting
is bounded by the issued link lifetime; Ctrl-C cancels.
.TP
.B --meta-device-auth
Use the native OAuth2 device flow of a Meta provider
.RB ( https://api.meta.ai
with or without
.BR /v1 ).
Open the printed verification page and confirm or enter the one-time code.
MUSE_AUTH_URL and MUSE_CLIENT_ID override the Meta issuer and client identifier.
Waiting is bounded by the issued link lifetime; Ctrl-C cancels.
.TP
.B --with-api-key
Read one API key from stdin for explicit login.  No key argument is accepted.
Noninteractive first setup also requires
.B -m MODEL
before
.BR login .
.TP
.B --with-access-token
Read one Enterprise Codex personal access token (at-) from stdin.  Validate it
with OpenAI and save its workspace identity for the direct Codex provider.
Use an Admin Console token with Codex permission:
.PP
.nf
printf '%s\\n' "$CODEX_ACCESS_TOKEN" | snajpagent login codex --with-access-token
snajpagent -m codex/gpt-6.1-sol/xhigh
.fi
.PP
For first setup, put
.B -m MODEL
before
.BR login .
Provider names are local labels stored with model selection. An existing name
such as openai can use auth=codex_token and the Codex endpoint. The codex preset
creates a separate provider; existing configurations retain their default.
Select a separate provider with
.B -m
or the session's
.B /model
command.  Token expiration and revocation require a replacement login; access
tokens have no OAuth refresh.  Switching an existing provider between device
login and token login requires logging that provider out first.
FedRAMP workspaces and agent-identity JWTs are unsupported by this login method.
.TP
.BI "login status " [provider]
Report configured credential sources and local state without contacting a
provider or refreshing tokens.  Provider entitlements require a remote check.
.TP
.BI "logout " [provider]
Remove only the selected provider's stored login.  If omitted, select the
configured default provider.  Environment variables and the Codex or Meta CLI credentials
are not modified; other provider logins remain intact.
.PP
Each session owner snapshots provider credentials at startup. Replacing a login
or key file affects new owners; use
.B /configure
to adopt it in a running owner. Logging out removes the saved login; running
owners keep their in-memory credential until a successful reload or exit. Revoke the token
at its issuer when access must end immediately. OAuth expiry and a pre-output
401 still renew the current login automatically; renewal leaves a replacement
shared login intact. API keys and Enterprise tokens have no automatic renewal.
.PP
Configuring a provider probes the native compaction endpoint once and records
the answer as
.B native_compaction
in that provider's section: a route that does not serve
.BR /responses/compact
(for example a local gateway answering 502) is saved as disabled instead of
being attempted on every later compaction.  An endpoint that does not answer
at all leaves the existing setting unchanged.
.PP
Automatic setup never consumes piped task input or runs for execution, resume,
session listing, or a missing explicitly specified configuration.  Invalid
existing configuration produces an error and remains on disk for correction.
.SH "Configuration"
The file is UTF-8 INI syntax.  Lines beginning with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments; there are no inline comments or general shell-style quotes.
Unknown sections, keys, duplicate scalar keys, invalid values, and files over
64 KiB are errors.  Booleans are
.B true
or
.BR false .
The numeric forms 1 and 0 are also accepted for boolean settings.
Command-line values override file values.  A missing default configuration
uses built-ins; an explicit file must exist.  Configuration must be a regular
file, not a symlink.  Section names below go in square brackets.
.SS agent
.TP
.B provider
Provider for new sessions.  Default: first configured provider.
.TP
.B model
Model for new sessions.  Default:
.BR default ,
which resolves to
.BR gpt-5.5-2026-04-23 .
.TP
.B reasoning_effort
Default:
.B default
(resolves to
.BR medium ).
The value is provider-specific; common choices include
.BR low ,
.BR medium ,
and
.BR high .
.TP
.B max_goal_prompt_bytes
Goal wording limit, 1 through 1048576.  Default: 262144.
.TP
.B retry_auto
Enable automatic transport and failed-turn retries. Default: true.
The saved /retry auto session override takes precedence. Disabling also stops
active-goal recovery after failure; explicit /retry remains available.
.TP
.B max_turn_retries
Additional automatic retries per consecutive failure sequence, 0 through
4294967295. Default: 5 (six attempts including the first). Zero disables
ordinary automatic retries. A successful actionable response resets the count.
The configured limit is captured at turn start, including an explicit
.BR /retry .
With automatic retry enabled, active goals ignore this limit and retain unlimited recovery. Paused and blocked
goals keep their state while ordinary prompts retry.
.TP
.B allow_model_change
Permit the model to use the
.B select_model
tool to refresh its model catalog or change provider, model, or reasoning effort.
Default:
.BR false .
When disabled, the tool is omitted from provider requests and calls are rejected
without changing the session selection or refreshing the cache. Set
.B allow_model_change=true
in
.B [agent]
to enable it. Operator
.B /model
and command-line
.B \-m
remain available. The current configuration applies on launch and resume.
.TP
.B read_agents_md
Discover global and project
.B AGENTS.override.md
or
.B AGENTS.md
instruction-file paths before each turn.  Default:
.BR true .
The model chooses relevant files to read with tools; contents are not injected.
Explicit
.B \-d
roots are independent of this setting.  See
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B auto_update
Check and install binary updates in a background thread on ordinary launch.
Default: true in official stable binaries; false in development binaries whose
version has a commit suffix.  Set true to opt a development installation in.
Ordinary local builds have no updater unless explicitly enabled at build time;
setting this option cannot enable a capability omitted from the executable.
Help, version, login and session-listing commands do not run the updater.
Configuration changes apply to the next launch's update attempt.
.TP
.B update_url
HTTPS update URL matching this installation.  The built-in
URL uses the publisher's latest or latest-dev directory and the exact build
target; universal macOS and legacy Linux remain their original variants.
Stable letter-suffixed versions, such as 0.99.8b, follow the plain numeric
version on the latest channel.  Commit-suffixed development versions use
latest-dev; an installed stable version never takes a development update.
The updater reads the URL with .json appended, then downloads the immutable
version URL it describes.  Treat a URL override as a publisher trust decision.
Only HTTPS transfers are allowed; verification checks the declared SHA-256,
size, version and embedded product, target and publisher identity.  Trust comes
from HTTPS and its certificate roots, not a separate release-signing key.
.PP
An update does not delay ordinary startup or provider work.  The existing process
continues running its original version.  After successful installation one
local, distinct banner gives restart advice and a changelog URL.  No automatic
restart, modal confirmation or recurring reminder occurs.  Script output is
unchanged; the banner uses stderr.  The running version remains unchanged until
restart, including the version shown by the current process.
.PP
The executable and its directory must be writable and owned by the running
user; shared-writable, privileged and hardlinked files are rejected.
The updater resolves the running executable, including a launch through a
symlink; a symlink substituted at the resolved install path is rejected.  Prefer a user-owned standalone install.  Do not enable updates
for package-managed installations.  The updater never elevates privileges.
Failed checks/downloads leave the old executable in place and ordinary work
continues; there is one attempt per launch.  The bundled resolver is asynchronous;
a custom libcurl without asynchronous DNS disables updating to preserve exit
responsiveness.  HTTPS proxy and SSL_CERT_FILE settings also apply to updates;
provider API credentials are never sent.
.PP
A private sibling .EXECUTABLE.update-lock coordinates updater instances.  A
partial .EXECUTABLE.update-new is discarded under that lock on the next attempt.
Unix installs use same-directory rename.  Windows keeps the old executable as
\&.EXECUTABLE.update-old.exe when the mapped file cannot be replaced directly.
If interrupted between the renames, run that retained executable with
auto_update enabled to restore the missing original name, or rename it back
while the agent is stopped.  Recovery uses the same lock as installation.
Do not delete it while another instance is using it.  There is no session-format
rollback promise: keeping an older executable does not undo newer session data.
.SS "provider NAME"
Ordered named sections may be repeated with distinct names.  The spelling
.B [provider]
is not accepted. Names contain 1 through 63 ASCII letters, digits, dots,
underscores or hyphens. Names have no privileged meaning. New sessions use the
first declaration unless an explicit provider preference or
.B --provider NAME
selects another. Unqualified model changes stay on the current provider.
.TP
.B base_url
HTTP or HTTPS API base without query or fragment.  Default:
.BR https://api.openai.com .
.TP
.B api_key
Credential source: ${ENV_NAME}, a double-quoted literal with JSON escaping,
or an unquoted file path. A bare environment name means a file, not a variable.
Relative files use the config directory; leading ~/ expands the user's home.
Files may have one final LF/CRLF and may be symlinks for rotation.
Literal secrets require a user-owned configuration with no group/other
permissions; mode 0600 is the normal choice.
Absent means use the provider's managed stored login; empty is invalid.
.TP
.B auth
Authentication method:
.B api_key
(default) uses the explicit source or, when absent, the provider's stored key; and
.B chatgpt
uses stored Codex OAuth credentials with automatic refresh.
.B codex_token
uses a validated stored Enterprise Codex personal access token.
Both Codex methods require the exact base
.BR https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex
and no api_key setting.
.B meta
uses stored Meta subscription OAuth credentials with automatic refresh while
the issuer honors it.
Meta auth requires the exact base
.BR https://api.meta.ai/v1
and no api_key setting.
These tokens are never sent to a custom endpoint.  Existing bearer-auth
Codex-compatible proxies use
.BR auth=api_key .
Missing/bad explicit sources never fall back to a stored login.
.TP
.B connect_timeout_ms
Connect timeout, 1000 through 120000.  Default: 30000.
.TP
.B idle_timeout_ms
No-data timeout, 1000 through 600000.  Default: 120000.
.TP
.B request_timeout_ms
Whole-request timeout, 1000 through 3600000.  Default: 1800000.
.TP
.B auto_compact_input_tokens
Proactive input threshold.  Default:
.BR auto .
Auto uses 90% of the selected model's effective hard input budget, rounded
down with a minimum of one token, after output reservation and safety limits.
When one source advertises both a normal working window and a larger maximum
context, the normal window supplies that hard budget; the maximum remains
visible as a client ceiling that can be selected explicitly.  A source that
publishes only a maximum still uses it.  An explicit
.B context_window_tokens
rule selects another context instead.  The threshold follows model/context
changes and learned lower ceilings.  If capacity is unknown, it uses a labelled
120000-token fallback.
An integer from 1 through 4000000 selects a fixed threshold; zero disables
proactive policy, not the hard capacity guard.  Existing explicit numbers
remain fixed: remove the setting or use
.B auto
to enable scaling.
.B /model
and
.B /status
show the effective threshold and its mode without changing configuration.
.TP
.B exact_token_count
Input-token preflight policy.
.B auto
prefers the provider's Responses count endpoint and falls back only when it is
definitively unsupported;
.B true
requires exact preflight; and
.B false
disables it.  Default:
.BR auto .
An absent endpoint (HTTP 405/501, or a route-level 404 without a semantic
error code) uses the automatic fallback; strict counting still fails.
Authentication failures and named model errors do not disable exact counting.
Counting sends model input, tool schemas and input configuration. Response-only
controls and prompt-cache routing remain on the generation request, including
native and Responses-based compaction.
OpenAI Platform and current llama.cpp servers provide full-request Responses
counting.  Direct ChatGPT/Codex, OpenRouter and other Responses servers without
that endpoint continue using measured response usage and bounded overflow recovery;
no local tokenizer or byte-to-token conversion is substituted.
.PP
Proactive compaction uses exact preflight or the last compatible measured input
usage. Compaction covers complete older event prefixes; each completed summary
records its covered boundary and later compactions start at that boundary, not
at the beginning of the journal. Previously presented image payloads are omitted
from summary requests. Native compaction output can retain user messages together
with its summary; the complete output is accepted within the 12 MiB byte bound.
New uncounted additions may exceed the token limit. If a
provider rejects a request before any output, the next attempt retains the
current turn and controls but omits the earlier provider transcript, compacted
conversation, images and completed tool calls. The model can use bounded
.B read_session_history
and
.B read_tool_output
tools to recover a needed fact without replaying those calls.
.PP
History pages walk verified journal records backwards without replaying the full
session. Use the returned exclusive
.B next_before_seq
cursor for older records. The optional nonempty
.B event_types
array filters exact event names; omitted or null selects all types.
An uncached older boundary uses record-aligned search; consecutive pages reuse
one in-memory position. After locating the boundary, a page scans up to 4 MiB
plus at most one complete record under the existing record limit. Counts describe
only that scanned range, excluding boundary lookup. A filtered page may be
empty with
.BR scan_complete=false ;
continue with its cursor rather than treating it as the end of history.
Checkpoint records show coverage metadata instead of saved transcripts;
provider continuations and encrypted compaction payloads are omitted.
Operator IRC queries and connection notices are excluded from model history
pages, including explicit event-type filters. They remain available in operator
history views. Sharing a private operator message with a model uses ordinary
operator input.
History records are filtered with configured secrets and the request's credential
before their details are shortened. Stored records remain unchanged.
History and goal listing report an output-budget failure, not a false empty/end
page, when no matching entry fits. Reduce history detail or increase the
configured tool output budget as indicated.
.PP
This recovery survives interruption and resume, including a turn without an active goal.
If that same active turn later needs another compaction, its recovery boundary
remains valid even after the turn's start leaves the retained event overlap.
For a current IRC update, the request also retains the referenced room
messages from the durable journal; unrelated historical room traffic stays
available through history tools rather than being replayed.
A rejection of the minimal recovery request fails that attempt rather than
starting another compaction loop. Known hard-budget overflow may compact one
tractable prefix first. The rebuilt request retains that summary and is counted
again; only if it remains too large does it take the same history-oriented
recovery path. Later compactions recognize the covered active turn even after
its start leaves the retained checkpoint overlap. Irreducible current input fails clearly; the
durable history is retained. Normal same-model compaction remains available,
and a provider/model/account switch retains the saved covered boundary and
portable summary where it fits.
.TP
.B native_compaction
Use the provider's native compaction endpoint.  When false, use an ordinary
Responses summarization request instead.  Default:
.BR true .
.PP
An unavailable native compaction endpoint falls back
once to Responses summarization. Authentication failures do not trigger this
fallback, and the configured provider/account and hard context guard stay intact.
.TP
.B parallel_tool_calls
Request native multiple function calls from this provider.  Default: true.
Set false for a provider/model that cannot batch calls.  This does not disable
local yielded-command concurrency or change provider/account selection.
.TP
.B leading_instructions
Set for an endpoint whose chat template only accepts instruction roles at the
start of the conversation, as llama.cpp templates do.  The trailing host
continuation is then sent in the labelled user transport slot instead of a
developer message.  Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.B openrouter_referer
Optional printable ASCII
.B HTTP-Referer
value.
.TP
.B openrouter_title
Optional printable ASCII
.B X-Title
value.
.SS audio
Optional audio preferences. Dictation and voice inherit the selected provider;
explicit values below override its defaults.
.TP
.B provider
Name of a configured provider. Empty uses the selected coding provider.
Credentials, endpoints and timeouts follow that provider. A codex-lb key
authenticates to the gateway; direct Codex uses the subscription login.
.TP
.B listen_model
Audio-capable Chat Completions model for listen_audio.
.TP
.B transcribe_model
Transcription model for transcribe_audio, /dictate and public Realtime input captions.
The default is gpt-4o-transcribe. Native voice supplies its own transcripts.
.TP
.B realtime_model
Conversation model for /voice on. Empty selects gpt-live-1-codex for native Codex
voice or gpt-realtime for public Realtime.
.TP
.B speech_model
Speech-generation model for speak_text. Set together with voice.
.TP
.B voice
Voice identifier. Empty selects cove for native Codex voice or marin for public
Realtime. A configured speech_model can use this value for speech generation.
.TP
.B capture_device, playback_device
Exact device names from /voice devices. Empty values use system defaults.
These settings alone authorize no capture or playback.
.PP
Subscription audio uses subscription access; BYOK audio uses that provider's
API billing. Failures do not switch billing routes. Unsupported operations are
reported explicitly. File-audio calls carry the selected
sound or synthesis text, without coding history or executable tools. Realtime
voice also receives its attributed coding results through the existing owner.
A failed or
interrupted request is not automatically retried; a request already sent may
have been billed. Media contents cannot receive textual secret redaction.
.SS "model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL"
Rules may be repeated with distinct provider and model selectors.
Set provider-wide defaults with [model-limit PROVIDER], patterned rules with
[model-limit PROVIDER/PATTERN], and exact rules with [model-limit PROVIDER/MODEL].
The first slash separates the provider; remaining slashes belong to the model.
Set at least one numeric field below, reasoning_efforts, or steering:
.TP
.B context_window_tokens
Total context tokens, 1 through 4000000000.  This explicit rule overrides a
catalog's larger maximum-context selection as well as its normal working-window
value.
.TP
.B max_input_tokens
Maximum input tokens, 1 through 4000000000.
.TP
.B max_output_tokens
Maximum output tokens, 1 through 4000000000.
.TP
.B image_tokens
Provider-documented per-image input ceiling in tokens, 1 through 4000000000,
for the matched local model.  Local image-request bounding uses it on any
provider route when exact counting is unavailable, superseding the
provider-generic resized-image budget it otherwise applies.
.PP
For example, DeepSeek documents a 1,024-token ceiling per image for
deepseek-flash:
.PP
.nf
[model-limit deepseek/deepseek-flash]
image_tokens = 1024
.fi
.PP
Execution fields may also override the corresponding
.B [tool]
defaults for a matched model:
.BR default_yield_ms ,
.BR max_wait_ms ,
.BR max_parallel_commands ,
.BR default_timeout_ms ,
.BR max_timeout_ms ,
.BR tool_output_bytes ,
and
.BR output_cache_bytes .
Their ranges and meanings match the tool settings below, except
.B tool_output_bytes
is the per-model name for the model-context result ceiling configured globally
as
.BR max_output_tokens .
The effective set is frozen with each admitted turn and shown to the model.
.TP
.B steering
IRC mid-turn admission, honored only in an exact
.BR PROVIDER/MODEL
entry: the default
.B mentions
admits a room message as steering only when it mentions the accepted model nick;
.B all
admits every room message.  Non-admitted messages remain ordinary room context;
local submissions and turn-wake behavior are unchanged.  Provider-wide and patterned
entries do not contribute to a model's steering, and there is no global setting.
.PP
Rules merge per field: provider-wide, matching patterns in file order, then
exact model. The star matches zero or more characters over the whole local
model name. Duplicate targets/keys are errors. Unspecified fields inherit
catalog metadata or the global tool settings. Explicit values supersede the
catalog, but output headroom and learned backend ceilings still constrain usable
input.  A default yield must not exceed its effective maximum wait, and a
default timeout must not exceed its effective maximum timeout.
.PP
.B reasoning_efforts
sets an ordered JSON array of effort names for the model picker, for example:
.PP
.nf
[model-limit example/fast-model]
reasoning_efforts = ["max", "high", "low", "none"]
.fi
.PP
The list overrides the provider's advertised efforts, including when discovery
supplies none. It uses the same provider/pattern/exact precedence as the numeric
fields and may be the only setting in a rule. Aliases match their local names.
Lists contain 1 through 32 distinct, nonempty UTF-8 strings of at most 63 bytes;
ASCII whitespace, control characters and slash are rejected. Names are
provider-defined. The program forwards selected names without provider-specific
translation; the existing effort shorthand default still means medium.
.PP
Numbered selection follows list order. Startup \-m without an explicit effort
uses the first listed effort. Interactive /model MODEL chooses the highest
recognized level, or the first listed name when all are unfamiliar. Explicit
/effort and model/effort selections remain usable even when absent from the
list. A configured list also applies to typed models before cache discovery;
listing an undiscovered model still requires a model-alias declaration.
.PP
The array remains in configuration; /model cache stores only provider metadata.
Reload configuration with /configure or start a new process to apply an edited
list. Invalid lists reject the reload while preserving the active configuration.
Existing turn/session effort preferences remain unchanged until selected again.
.SS "model-alias PROVIDER/NAME"
The local name uses the provider-name syntax. The required model key is a
literal upstream model ID. These declarations define ordinary provider-local
models with independent limits; several names
may route to the same upstream model. Targets never resolve recursively.
Selection, sessions and limits use the local name; provider requests use the
upstream ID. Upstream names do not add another limit-rule matching tier.
.PP
For example, give one provider-local model a smaller context budget:
.PP
.nf
[model-alias openai/review]
model = gpt-5.5

[model-limit openai]
max_output_tokens = 16000

[model-limit openai/review]
context_window_tokens = 128000
.fi
.PP
Select it with
.B --provider openai -m review
or
.BR "/model openai/review/high" .
.SS ui
.TP
.B color
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
Default:
.BR auto .
.TP
.B markdown
Render supported terminal Markdown.  Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.B resume_history_turns
Turns to show on resume.  Default: 1.  Accepts any nonnegative decimal count;
large counts show all turns.  Zero shows only the counts.  Replay ends with
shown, completed and total session turns.  Unfinished, failed and interrupted
turns include their saved public output.  Completed refusals and goal turns
are included.  History before compaction remains available.  Network chat
also restores its room history.
.TP
.B typing_pause_ms
Output pause after an input edit, 0 through 5000.  Default: 500.
.TP
.B pager
Interactive command-report and /cat paging:
.B on
(the default) uses $PAGER. When $PAGER is unset or exactly
.BR less ,
it uses
.B less \-FX
if less is available. An explicitly empty $PAGER disables paging;
.B off
prints command reports directly and disables /cat; any other value is a command template. A
template may contain
.B %s
where the quoted file path goes; without it the path is appended. Reports
use a private temporary file, while /cat passes the requested file to the
pager directly.  Voice-submitted reference commands display their reports directly.
Successful paged command reports remain available to voice as UI observations;
/cat contents remain operator-only.  The command runs only in interactive mode with terminal
input and output; a command that cannot start leaves the report to the normal display,
while /cat reports an error.  Example:
.BR "pager = less -R %s" .
.TP
.B prompt
Data-only prompt template containing exactly one
.BR {chat:TEXT} ,
.BR {rollout-idle:TEXT} ,
and
.BR {rollout-active:TEXT}
case.  Ordinary fields are
.BR {provider} ,
.BR {model} ,
.BR {effort} ,
.BR {operator} ,
.BR {model_nick} ,
.BR {session_name} ,
.BR {host} ,
.BR {context} ,
.BR {mode} ,
.BR {queue} ,
.BR {hour} ,
.BR {minute} ,
and
.BR {second} ;
optional status fields are
.BR {goal_spinner}
and
.BR {activity_spinner} .
The old separate provider/tool placeholders are removed pre-1.0; explicit
templates must replace them with one shared activity placeholder.
Backslash escapes literal braces and backslash.  No shell, environment, or
command expansion occurs.  snajpagent appends one space after the selected
label.
.PP
.B {operator}
and
.B {model_nick}
show the local operator and agent IRC identities.  Connected endpoints supply
their accepted nicks, including server-assigned suffixes and later renames.
Chat uses the selected endpoint; rollout uses the first endpoint.  Without an
endpoint, the local nick values are used.
.B {model}
continues to mean the provider's model identifier.
.B {session_name}
shows the saved name set by
.B -N
or
.BR "/session name NAME" ,
and expands to empty text for an unnamed session.  Renames update the prompt,
and resume restores the saved name.  Names are inserted literally, including
spaces and braces.  These optional fields do not change the default template.
.PP
.B {queue}
expands to the plain number of pending future prompts, including 0 when empty.
It includes paused prompts, excludes the running turn, and updates as prompts
are queued, started or removed.
.B {context}
expands to the plain percentage number, or ? when unknown; it includes no percent
sign.  Punctuation and spacing belong to the template.
The optional
.B {queued:TEXT}
section renders TEXT only when the queue count is nonzero.  It accepts ordinary
fields and may appear inside a mode case or in shared text.  Its contents are
validated even when hidden.  Default rollout prompts use
.B {context:3}% {queued:({queue}) }
after provider/model/effort, before the idle/active marker.  Default chat and
queue-edit prompts are unchanged.
Explicit pre-1.0 templates must replace the old decorated fields: use
.B {context:3}%
instead of
.B {context:4}
and
.B {queued:({queue}) }
instead of a bare
.BR {queue} .
.PP
Numeric fields accept a minimum space-padded width, for example
.B {context:3}%
renders "  0%", "  9%", " 10%", "100%", or "  ?%".
Queue counts also accept a minimum width, such as
.BR {queue:3} .
Clock components are natural decimal digits and additionally accept zero-fill:
.B {hour:02}:{minute:02}:{second:02}
is the default local clock.  Bare fields are unpadded.  Failed time capture
renders each component as "--", padded with spaces even for a zero-fill format.
Widths never truncate, exclude literal punctuation, and must be positive decimal
integers at most 510.  The complete label, input space, and largest spinner
frames must still fit 512 bytes including NUL.  Signs, whitespace, extra leading
zeros, empty widths, and formats on text/spinner fields are rejected in every
mode.  Context and queue zero-fill are invalid.  Invalid reloads retain the previous config.
The pre-1.0 combined
.B {time}
field is removed: manually replace it in explicit templates with clock components.
.PP
For a minimal prompt without spinners, use this single-line value:
.nf
prompt = {chat:{operator}:}{rollout-idle:›}{rollout-active:»}
.fi
.PP
To show both nicks and the session name in every view, use:
.nf
prompt = [{session_name}] {operator}/{model_nick}{chat: :}{rollout-idle: ›}{rollout-active: »}
.fi
.PP
The full expanded label must fit the limit described above.  An interactive
session rename that would exceed it is rejected and keeps the previous name.
A long name remains valid when the template does not display it.
.PP
Templates must be printable valid UTF-8, at most 1024 bytes.  All three cases
are required and nonempty; text and fields outside them are shared.
A case may omit fields.  Each spinner may occur at most once in each mode
after combining shared fields with its selected case.  The default appearance
is described under Terminal, Views and prompt.
.TP
.BR prompt_spinner_goal , " prompt_spinner_provider" , " prompt_spinner_tool"
Quoted inactive-item plus active-frame strings.  The first safe one-column
Unicode item is the inactive rendering.  Exact leading file characters
.B \e0
instead select zero-width inactive rendering; no NUL byte is stored.  A space
therefore reserves a stable column, while
.B \(dq\e0\(dq
removes a present field and
.B \(dq\e0⚑\(dq
appears only while active.  The remaining one-column items are active frames,
bounded only by the value's byte limit.  One active frame is static and
schedules no periodic refresh;
two or more animate.  Adjacent active frames must differ.  A blank active
frame still occupies one column.  Omit a placeholder to remove it in one mode.
Literal spaces are never trimmed, and numeric padding never restores an absent
spinner.  The activity slot uses the tool sequence during a tool call,
otherwise the provider sequence during provider operations and its inactive
item while no provider operation is running, including a parked turn. A selected
sequence with no active frames uses its inactive item.  Defaults:
.PP
.nf
prompt_spinner_goal = " ⚑"
prompt_spinner_provider = " ◴◷◶◵"
prompt_spinner_tool = " ⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏"
.fi
.TP
.B prompt_spinner_per_second
Shared animation rate, 1 through 60.  Default: 8.  Hidden and static indicators
do not animate.
.TP
.B prompt_tool_spinner_off_delay_ms
Keep the tool indicator visible for this many milliseconds after a tool call
finishes. Default: 500; range: 0 through 60000. Zero disables the delay.
A new tool call resets the delay when it finishes. After expiry the current
provider, goal and idle state is shown normally. Uses the existing spinner
refresh timer, including when the configured tool frame is static.
Only presentation is delayed; tool execution and result delivery are unchanged.
.SS tool
.TP
.B shell
Absolute executable used by
.BR exec_command .
Default on POSIX:
.BR /bin/sh ;
Android uses
.BR /system/bin/sh .
Experimental native Windows builds use the system cmd.exe path.  Commands
must use the configured shell's syntax; changing shells does not translate them.
The target must resolve to an executable regular file, but its configured
path is preserved so symlink-selected shell behavior (such as BusyBox sh)
is retained.
.TP
.B default_yield_ms
Default per-invocation yield interval, 0 through 600000. Default: 10000.
Zero waits for completion subject to max_wait_ms and operator input.
.TP
.B max_wait_ms
Maximum time each managed-tool invocation waits before returning control to
the model, 1 through 4294967295 milliseconds. Default: 60000 (60 seconds).
Applies to exec_command and every write_stdin call, including termination,
zero yields and longer model-requested waits. Shorter yields still apply.
The budget starts anew for each invocation; it measures elapsed wait time,
including time with output, rather than inactivity or total command lifetime.
.PP
Expiry returns status=running, reason=wait_timeout, the live handle and captured
output. The model text includes elapsed wait time, the configured limit and
pending termination when applicable. The process stays owned by the session;
the model decides whether to wait again, inspect, send input or terminate.
The limit sends no signal. Command-specific timeout_ms remains a separate
one-shot handoff deadline. Interactive operator cancellation retains its
existing shutdown behavior.
.TP
.B max_parallel_commands
Maximum unsettled command handles, including yielded and ready-but-uncollected
jobs.  Range: 1 through 4294967295.  Default: 4.  One runs commands serially
without restoring the former tool-catalog lock.  This is not a subprocess or
CPU limit.
.TP
.B default_timeout_ms
Foreground handoff deadline when the model supplies none, 0 through 4294967295.
Zero means no deadline.  Must not exceed
.BR max_timeout_ms .
Default: 0.  Reaching the deadline does not kill the command.
.TP
.B max_timeout_ms
Maximum positive model handoff deadline, 1 through 4294967295. Default: 86400000.
Out-of-range tool arguments are rejected before execution with the allowed range;
they are not silently reduced.
.TP
.B max_output_tokens
Command-result model-context ceiling in retained UTF-8 bytes, 1 through
4000000000.  Default: 6000.  The legacy key name is not a token-count unit;
provider/model output limits with the same name are separately token-based.
A tool call selects a smaller positive bound or
.B null
for this ceiling. Larger requests are capped at this value, with requested and
applied values reported. A separate per-result context-safety ceiling may reduce
the excerpt further and is also reported. These limits leave durable capture
unchanged. Host argument corrections and applied-limit feedback are supplied
separately from the bounded command-output body, including with very small
output budgets.
.TP
.B output_cache_bytes
Maximum command-output bytes kept in the in-memory paging cache, 0 through
67108864.  Default: 1048576 (1 MiB).  Zero disables the cache.  Complete
redacted stdout and stderr remain in the durable session journal independently;
cache misses reload bounded ranges for
.BR read_tool_output
without rerunning the command. Completed-command reads walk verified journal
records backwards and stop when the requested range is complete. Active commands
use their retained collection cursor when it covers the requested bytes.
.TP
.B max_output_bytes
Maximum result bytes displayed per tool call, 0 through 4294967295.  Zero
means unlimited.  The
durable session retains complete redacted output; model context has its own
tool-call bound.  Default: 0.
.TP
.B secret
Repeatable additional value to protect, using the same source syntax as api_key.
Environment references are removed from child environments;
files/literals are not exported. Values are retained at startup and explicit
configuration reload; changes to their files affect that owner after reload.
Missing required sources fail before protected work and reject an explicit reload.
The obsolete secret_env key is rejected.
.SS irc
.TP
.B listen
Enable the server and set its listener.  Absent by default.  A bare command-line
.BR \-s
uses
.BR localhost:6667 .
.TP
.B client
Outgoing endpoint.  Repeat up to 16 distinct endpoints.  Command-line
.B \-c
occurrences replace this entire list, preserving their order.
.TP
.B model_nick
Preferred model nick.  Default:
.BR agent0 .
.TP
.B operator_nick
Local operator nick.  Default: valid
.B USER
plus
.BR 0 ,
then a distinct
.B operator0
or
.BR localop0 .
.TP
.B room_name
Preferred room for current or future hosting.  Default: hostname.  Leading
.B #
is optional.
.TP
.B history_lines
Server and snapshot history, 1 through 1000.  Default: 200.
.PP
snajpagent peers negotiate incremental reconnect history using durable stream
IDs and event sequences.  Reopening the same server session preserves its IDs;
new server sessions have a new stream.  A client's saved cursor requests only
missing events.  Duplicate events are not appended, displayed or introduced as
new model input.  Current topic, membership and accepted nicknames still refresh.
An expired cursor reports a retention gap and replays the available newer tail.
New clients receive one bounded initial history.  Interrupted delivery is
recovered from the client's session until a model response consumes it.

.SS "rule NAME"
Filter model tool calls before they run.  Each
.B [rule NAME]
section declares one rule in a single ordered list; the first matching rule
decides.  Rules are validated when the configuration loads: unknown keys,
invalid regular expressions and duplicate names fail startup instead of
becoming silent policy.  Keys from the 0.99.7 chains, jumps, thresholds,
templates and helper verbs are refused at load; see the migration table below.
.TP
.B action
.B allow
or
.BR deny .
Put allowed tools first and a match-all
.B deny
last to build an allowlist.
.TP
.B match
A JSON object mapping each
.I /json/pointer
to a POSIX extended regular expression; every pair must match.
.TP
.B message
The message shown to the model for
.BR deny .
A value that starts with a double quote is decoded as a JSON string.
Refused on
.BR allow .
.PP
The envelope exposes the immutable facts
.BR boundary ,
.BR kind ,
.B surface
and
.BR tool ,
with the tool argument object as
.B value
and its canonical JSON as
.BR text .
A rejected call answers a factual not-run result; it is never silently dropped.
Rules are filtering, not containment: matching a command string is not a
sandbox, so use read-only turns and separate accounts for real isolation.
.PP
.nf
[rule deny-recursive-delete]
match   = {"/tool":"^exec_command$","/text":"rm[[:space:]]+-[^[:space:]]*[rf]"}
action  = deny
message = "Recursive force-delete is disabled; delete explicit paths."

[rule allow-commands]
match  = {"/tool":"^exec_command$"}
action = allow

[rule deny-everything-else]
action  = deny
message = "Only the audited command tool is permitted."
.fi
.PP
Rule count is bounded by the configuration file; 63-byte names and 64 KiB per message.  The engine is
stateless: no rule fires once, rate-limits or counts across evaluations.
Every matched rule records one fixed audit line,
.IR "rule=<name> decision=<allow|deny> tool=<tool>" ,
as a durable
.B rule_log
event, including rules after the deciding one, so a trailing match-all
.B allow
audits the whole session without changing any verdict.
.PP
0.99.7 rule files need three renames:
.B chain
lines are deleted,
.B reject
becomes
.BR deny ,
.B accept
becomes
.BR allow ,
and
.B text
becomes
.BR message .
Jump chains are pasted inline in order,
.B return
is deleted,
and thresholds, log templates and the insert, command, confirm and value
verbs have no replacement: configurations using them fail startup with a
pointer to
.IR design/io-rules.md .

.SH "Scripting"
.SS Automation and integration
Use
.B \-e
for scripts. It runs the supplied turn and any explicitly requested goal
continuations before exiting. With --resume and no new prompt, terminal or empty
stdin continues saved unfinished work directly; completed or explicitly stopped
work stays stopped. Nonempty piped stdin remains new input. For example:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent -e --resume SESSION_ID </dev/null
.fi
.PP
A prompt after
.B \-\-
preserves shell argument boundaries as spaces; a prompt from non-terminal stdin
preserves internal newlines and removes one final LF or CRLF.
.PP
Standard output carries one-shot model text.  Diagnostics and the resume hint
use standard error.  Redirected model text remains byte-exact and contains no
terminal color or Markdown presentation.
Use > /dev/null to discard model text while keeping diagnostics on standard error.
.PP
The normal coding-tool surface is
.BR exec_command ,
.BR write_stdin ,
.BR apply_patch ,
.BR list_files ,
.BR read_file ,
.BR grep ,
.BR write_file
and
.BR edit_file .
Both normal and read-only turns can inspect retained images, documents and
sampled video through
.BR view_image ,
.BR read_document ,
and
.BR view_video .
Configured audio routes add perception, transcription and speech generation.
Read-only queries instead expose the native functions
.BR list_files ,
.BR read_file ,
and
.BR grep .
They also expose provider-hosted
.BR web_search ,
executed by the provider, never as a local function or external command.
Listed AGENTS guidance remains subordinate to the read-only query and its
restrictions.  Other file and web contents are untrusted data, not instructions.
All other local tool dispatch, including process control, patches,
goal lifecycle, and IRC, is unavailable for that turn.
.PP
Native inspection accepts literal cwd-relative or absolute paths and
never follows symlinks, including parent components.  Listings include hidden
entries; special files and symlinks are labeled but not opened.  Reads require
regular UTF-8 files without NUL bytes.  Search uses POSIX extended regex or
explicit literal matching, with optional case folding, and reports skipped
non-text/special files.  It does not interpret ignore files or shell syntax.
Local readable files remain accessible.
Session/history persistence and filesystem access-time updates remain normal;
independently running processes are not stopped or made read-only.
.PP
Results are bounded to 256 KiB, lines to less than 64 KiB, each scan to 128 MiB,
directory entries to 100,000, and traversal depth to 32.  Listing/search use
offset/limit pagination (default 200, maximum 1,000 results); output identifies
incompleteness and the next offset.  Scan limits and I/O errors require a
narrower path.  Oversized file output fails explicitly: use smaller line ranges.
Directory order is sorted per directory; pagination observes the live tree,
not a filesystem snapshot.
.PP
Tool arguments can arrive as streamed bytes or completed snapshots.  An empty
in-progress placeholder leaves the arguments pending.  Conflicting argument
bytes or completed snapshots fail before tool execution.
.PP
Commands that yield, reach a foreground handoff deadline, or receive urgent
input continue under a process handle; the model can wait, interact, or
explicitly terminate them with
.BR write_stdin .
.PP
In interactive sessions, a final model reply while commands are running keeps
the turn open and waits for operator input or command completion.  This lets
the model ask for input needed by an interactive command.  Output continues
to be buffered, and the same command handles remain available.  No provider
requests or automatic retries run during this wait.
.B /yield
returns control to the model; Ctrl-C interrupts the turn and closes its commands.
Urgent IRC input also returns control to the model.
.PP
Ready command results must be collected before a final answer completes the
turn.  One-shot execution requires every command to settle before answering.
Recovery points the model to the current process snapshot and
.B write_stdin
to collect outstanding results.  A terminally failed turn closes its commands.
.PP
The current foreground process owns its command handles.  Turn interruption, closure, or process exit closes managed commands;
resume does not revive an operating-system process.
.PP
Every
.B exec_command
and
.B write_stdin
call selects a positive
.B max_output_bytes
context bound or
.B null
for the configured
.BR "[tool] max_output_tokens" " ceiling (default: 6000)."
Larger requested bounds are capped at that ceiling; smaller bounds are honored.
For backward compatibility the option keeps its name, but the bound is a
retained UTF-8 byte limit, not a token count or estimate. It is persisted
with the result and enforces byte/memory safety.  Truncated context retains bounded
provenance; complete redacted output remains in journal chunks.  Polls return
only newly collected output, with exact stream-range references.  Multiple
independent command calls may overlap; emission order does not establish a
dependency.  A batch shares the smallest positive yield interval.  Steering
does not discard valid calls already emitted in an accepted response: those
calls cross their durable admission boundary, then running commands are handed
back alive immediately.  Capacity-blocked calls retain that factual reason.
Invalid or repeated same-handle interactions are not run.  All handles must
be settled before a final answer or goal completion.  Existing commands keep
their tool catalog; unrelated work does not require an immediate poll.
.PP
Each command result identifies its durable output handle and exact stdout and
stderr byte ranges.
.B read_tool_output
accepts that 32-character handle, one stream, a zero-based byte offset and an
optional model-facing page ceiling.  It reports total and next offsets, EOF,
cache disposition and either UTF-8 text or base64 for binary bytes.  Pages are
bounded by the effective common result ceiling; settled output remains readable
after compaction, cache eviction and session resume.  Unknown handles, streams
or offsets fail without running a replacement command.
Failed or cancelled cache fills preserve the previous cached page and its handle.
Unreadable or corrupt history is reported as a read failure, not as proof that
output does not exist. Uncollected output can use the live process's verified
journal cursor; older and settled ranges still require historical lookup.
.PP
.B set_command_shell
selects an absolute executable regular file for later
.B exec_command
calls in this session.  The event survives resume and preserves a symlink path
whose name selects shell behavior.  It does not translate command text, alter
already-running commands, expand authorization, expose secrets or replace the
existing workdir and environment rules.
Hosted web search is a provider tool.  When the active provider URL has the
exact hostname
.BR openrouter.ai ,
snajpagent automatically uses
.BR openrouter:web_search ;
other providers retain
.BR web_search .
Hostname matching is case-insensitive and accepts an optional terminal DNS
dot and port, but not userinfo, subdomains, or lookalike hosts.  The provider
section name and model name do not control detection.  Use the usual
.B https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
base URL, model, and API credentials; no extra search setting, web plugin,
.B :online
suffix, or separate search credential is required.  This applies equally to
normal and read-only turns.  OpenRouter chooses the search engine by default;
search incurs provider charges in addition to model usage.
.PP
IRC controls include
.BR irc_state ,
.BR irc_send ,
.BR irc_nick ,
.BR irc_topic ,
.BR irc_connect ,
.BR irc_host ,
.BR irc_disconnect ,
.BR irc_sleep ,
and
.BR irc_compact .
Sending, live nick changes and topic changes require a nullable string
.B destination
argument: a number from
.BR irc_state ,
.B all
for explicit broadcast, or
.B null
only for a sole destination.  Model routing is independent of the operator's
selection.  Results distinguish queue acceptance from failures, not remote receipt.
.PP
Explicit goal requests expose
.B create_goal
when no unfinished goal exists. An unfinished goal exposes
.B update_goal
for rewriting, completion, blocking or resuming under the current state's rules.
Blocking requires both a nonblank reason in
.B text
and an explicit single-line
.B wait_for
value: operator, timer, irc: endpoint/nick, process: handle, or
external: concrete dependency. Each text field permits up to 64 KiB for blocking.
Use operator only when operator input is actually needed. For example:
.PP
.nf
{"action":"block","text":"Waiting for the build result",
 "wait_for":"irc: team.example:6697/secretary"}
.fi
.PP
The label records the dependency; existing IRC and timer admission determine
when another ordinary turn runs. Managed command handles must still be collected
before ending a turn. Other actions omit wait_for or set it to null.
The durable notice, idle line, goal status, goal list and retained model context
preserve this destination. Resume, completion and cancellation clear the wait.
A locked goal refuses the model's rewording, blocking and cancelling;
completion and resume stay available, so a locked goal can still finish.
.PP
IRC is the network integration.  The event log is durable internal state, not
a stable external API while snajpagent remains pre-1.0.
.SS Signals
.TP
.B SIGINT
Interactive Ctrl-C follows the composer rules above.  Outside that handler it
requests graceful shutdown. On POSIX hosts, spawned commands start with SIGINT
unblocked and its default action, independently of the parent's signal state.
.TP
.BR SIGHUP , " SIGTERM"
Request graceful shutdown, terminal restoration, IRC closure, and a resume
hint.  Exit status is 128 plus the signal number.
For a Linux native terminal frontend these signals disconnect only that
frontend; its session owner continues. /exit stops the owning session.
.TP
.B SIGTSTP
Ctrl-Z restores the terminal before suspension and redraws after continuation.
.TP
.B SIGPIPE
Ignored; write failures are handled as errors.
.SS Exit status
.TP
.B 0
Success or normal interactive exit.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line, configuration, locale, credential, prompt, or selection error.
.TP
.B 3
Durable state, local runtime, or IRC failure.
.TP
.B 4
One-shot provider, protocol, context, or tool-cycle failure.
.TP
.B 6
Terminal/output failure or one-shot interruption that cannot return to a prompt.
.TP
.B 128+N
Graceful exit after signal
.IR N .
.SH "Model tools"
.SS Visibility and execution
These are model-callable tools, not slash commands to type into the composer.
Every provider request advertises the configured native catalog. The
.B allow_model_change
setting controls whether
.B select_model
is available; it defaults to false. Read-only mode,
goal and queue state, network configuration, continuation mode and current
endpoint state do not remove a schema. A declaration describes a call shape;
runtime validation and tool results report whether the current turn can perform
that call. Arguments use strict JSON objects: required fields are present, with
.B null
where a nullable field should use its default. Unknown fields are rejected.
Pre-1.0 tool and event formats are not a stable third-party automation API.
.SS Tool catalog
The following names are the functions shown to the model. Their operands and
results are documented below. Slash commands are operator input; they are listed
under Commands. Hosted search is advertised in the provider's native form.
.TP
.B Working directory and model
get_cwd, cd, select_model.
.TP
.B Commands and process control
exec_command, write_stdin, read_tool_output, set_command_shell.
.TP
.B Filesystem exploration
list_files, read_file, grep.
.TP
.B File modification
write_file, edit_file, apply_patch.
.TP
.B Media and workstation delivery
view_image, read_document, view_video, listen_audio, transcribe_audio,
speak_text, send_file, download_queue.
.TP
.B Saved history and continued work
read_session_history, list_goals, create_goal, update_goal, timer,
defer_steering.
.TP
.B IRC
irc_state, irc_send, irc_topic, irc_nick, irc_connect, irc_host,
irc_disconnect, irc_sleep, irc_compact.
.TP
.B Search
Provider-hosted web search, when supplied by the provider request format.
.SS Argument validation and applied limits
Every provider receives the same native tool contract: required operands,
optional controls, types, ranges and parameter descriptions. Object key order
is irrelevant. Omitted optional controls use their documented defaults;
nullable fields also accept JSON null. The string "null" remains literal data.
Fixed policy uses system messages for every provider. Each model request ends
with an explicit developer-level host continuation, separate from operator input
and approval. Automatic goal turns additionally carry a labelled host request in
the user-role conversation slot. It keeps the current goal request alongside
retained history when a gateway moves instruction messages out of the input.
This transport marker creates no operator input, approval, receipt timestamp or
goal transition; paused goals remain paused on resume. Saved reasoning stays in
its compatible order before the developer boundary;
history with non-thinking replies also remains resumable. Summary requests use
the same separation. User requests and tool results retain their provenance.
Media parameter descriptions include crop and sheet coordinates, index origins,
interval defaults and configured audio behavior. Coding requests also identify
the current working directory, configured shell, wait/deadline defaults, parallel-command
maximum, output ceiling and goal text limits.
.PP
The canonical command fields are command, yield_ms and max_output_bytes.
The legacy spellings cmd, yield_time_ms and max_output_tokens respectively
remain accepted. Supply one spelling per field: supplying both is rejected,
even if the values are equal or null. Other unknown fields, including shell,
are rejected. Original arguments remain unchanged in the journal.
.PP
Argument errors identify missing operands, unexpected fields, invalid types,
ranges and path or cross-field restrictions. A not_run result records an
unexecuted attempt with no new process or handle. At verbosity 1 and above,
private rollout shows its tool name, status and short reason in both live and
history views. Full arguments and output remain governed by their detail levels.
Skipped calls also explain slot limits, stale/busy handles and control handoffs.
.PP
Each request identifies the current working directory, configured shell, wait/deadline defaults,
parallel-command maximum, output ceiling and goal text limits. Output requests
above the configured byte ceiling are capped with requested/applied feedback.
Excessive waits, timeouts and other out-of-range controls are rejected rather
than capped. Latest-batch corrections and applied-limit feedback remain visible
to the model even under a tiny command-output budget and after interrupted
provider requests. Complete redacted output remains in the session journal.
.SS Working directory and model selection
.B get_cwd
has no arguments and returns the session's current absolute working directory.
New sessions start at the home directory; a saved session continues from its
last recorded directory.
.PP
.B cd
requires path to an existing directory. Absolute paths and relative paths,
including
.BR ./ ,
resolve against the current directory. A successful change is saved before
later tool calls, including calls in the same response batch; existing processes
keep their own launch directory.
.PP
.B select_model
requires selector, either a configured
.IR "provider/model/effort" ,
a shorter model selector, or a numbered row from the cached model listing.
It is available when
.B [agent] allow_model_change=true
(default: false). When disabled, the tool is absent from the model's catalog and
calls fail without side effects.
The provider, model and effort must match a row in the current model cache;
an empty or unreadable cache leaves the session selection unchanged. Selector
.B cache
refreshes every configured provider and returns the available cached rows.
A failed refresh retains the previous cache and model selection. Operator-typed
.B /model
and command-line
.B -m
may still choose uncached identifiers.
A model choice selects the next response in the current turn and remains selected for
later turns. An already running command handle stays live; completed tool
results are retained in the next request's context. This tool does not modify
the configuration file. The
.B /model
command uses the same persistent selection and can interrupt a streaming
response to start a request on the new model.
.SS send_file
Requires path, a literal cwd-relative or absolute regular-file path without
symlinks, or asset:ID for an accepted retained file. Journals a workstation
export in the current session and sends it when a live native wrapper replies.
Returns a terminal result distinguishing queued intent from delivery; there
is no managed-process handle to poll. Completion requires the client's digest
acknowledgement and final EXIT. Native completion receipts list actual local
paths. File bytes are outside tool output and never uploaded to the model.
See Terminal file transfers for setup, destination, cancellation and recovery.
.SS download_queue
Requires action=list, remove or clear. List returns pending IDs, source paths,
byte counts and SHA256 digests. Remove also requires the item's id; clear
removes all pending exports. An optional reason is saved with mutations.
Stale sources can be removed without opening them. Removing an export changes
neither its source nor any landed workstation file. Read-only permits list
and rejects remove and clear. Pending state belongs to the existing saved
remote session and survives resume and checkpoint replay.
.SS exec_command
Requires command, the source text for the configured shell. Workdir is optional:
omission or null uses the session's current working directory; a supplied
existing directory may be absolute or start with ./ relative to it. Other controls are optional. For example:
.PP
.nf
{"command":"pwd"}
.fi
.PP
.B stdin
is omitted/null to leave input available for later interaction, or UTF-8 text up to
1 MiB to send and then close input.  An empty string sends immediate EOF.
.B pty
is true for a pseudo-terminal; false or null uses ordinary pipes.  PTY output
is a terminal stream rather than separate stdout/stderr, and terminal line
editing/echo rules may affect interactive input.
.PP
.B yield_ms
is 0 through 600000, or omitted/null for the configured default.  It bounds the
foreground wait before returning a handle; it is not a process lifetime.
An explicit 0 yields after the accepted call wave starts; a configured default
of 0 waits for completion subject to max_wait_ms and operator input.
.B timeout_ms
is a positive foreground handoff deadline, bounded by the configured maximum,
or omitted/null for the configured default.  The built-in default has no deadline.
A deadline yields control without terminating the child.
.PP
.B max_output_bytes
is a positive retained UTF-8 byte bound for result text sent to model context,
or omitted/null for the configured ceiling (default 6000). The legacy input
spelling max_output_tokens retains these byte semantics. This argument selects
the model excerpt; the similarly named [tool] max_output_bytes setting controls
terminal display only. Larger requests are capped with requested/applied
feedback. Complete redacted
process output remains in the session log; the bounded excerpt identifies
truncation and its source.
Returned status distinguishes running, successful/failed exit, and termination
by signal.  A running result means the invocation returned, not that the
command completed.  Keep its handle and use
.BR write_stdin ;
do not start the same command again merely to wait for it. The turn cannot
complete with a running handle: collect its terminal result or close it through
an authorized cancellation/failure transition. A rejected premature final
does not erase the process or claim completion.
.PP
After command exit, output capture drains for at most two seconds. If another
process still holds a writer open, remaining capture streams close and the
result preserves the command's exit code or signal with
.B reason=output_drain_timeout
and an explicit warning that output may be incomplete. Already captured output
is retained and secret-redacted. The completed handle is released; unrelated
processes holding passed descriptors are not signalled. Continued output does
not extend the drain limit. A live command remains governed by its ordinary
wait and termination controls.
.SS write_stdin
Requires a live handle. Omitted data is an empty string: it polls without
sending bytes or closing input. Supplied data must be a string, not null.
The eof and terminate flags default to false; optional wait/output controls
use the same defaults and legacy spellings as exec_command. Minimal polling:
.PP
.nf
{"handle":"<live handle returned by exec_command>"}
.fi
.PP
.B handle
identifies a currently managed command.
.B data
is UTF-8 input up to 1 MiB; use an empty string to wait without writing.
.B eof
true closes input after the accepted data; false or null leaves it open.
.B terminate
true requests termination and requires empty data with eof false or null.
A yield, wait limit or operator /yield can return a running result and the
same handle while termination or output drain remains pending.
Yield and output bounds follow
.BR exec_command .
.PP
Pending input and output are bounded.  Accepted input may still be waiting to
reach the process; output references distinguish accepted, written, pending
and open-input state.  Do not resend accepted data or send to a closed input.
Each handle can participate at most once in a model response's call batch.
The runtime rejects invalid, stale or concurrently occupied handles rather
than guessing which process was intended.
.PP
Unsettled commands count against
.BR max_parallel_commands ,
including commands that have exited but whose final result is not collected.
The model may perform unrelated work while a command runs, but must settle
all handles before its final answer or goal completion.  Shutdown cleans up
managed commands and descendants; saved session history cannot revive them.
.SS apply_patch
The patch is required. Optional workdir defaults to the current working
directory on omission or null; another existing absolute or ./ directory is
accepted.
.B patch
contains one bounded patch with this structure:
.PP
.nf
*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: example.c
@@
-old line
+new line
*** End Patch
.fi
.PP
Also supported are
.B *** Add File:
with added lines prefixed by plus, and
.B *** Delete File:
without a body. File paths may be absolute or relative to the current working
directory, including ./; parent traversal, duplicate targets and symlink
traversal are rejected.
Update hunks locate changes by matching context.
There is no move/rename operation in this patch grammar.
.PP
The tool validates targets and computes replacements before installation,
checks for concurrent changes, and reports rejection or I/O failure explicitly.
Do not assume a multi-file transaction survives every crash atomically.
Inspect the result and current files after failure before retrying.
Limits are 2 MiB patch text, 1 MiB per patch line, 256 file operations,
2048 hunks in total, 16 MiB per regular file and 48 MiB aggregate file data.
Result text and diff previews are bounded.  Use normal command tools for work
outside this patch grammar. Their authority is the launching user's OS
permissions.
.SS Native exploration
.B list_files
accepts path, recursive, offset and limit.  Path is required; null recursive
means false.  It lists hidden entries and labels directories, symlinks and
special files without following them.
.PP
.B read_file
accepts path, start_line and end_line.  Non-null line bounds are inclusive,
1-based and at most 2147483647.  Null bounds select the start/end of the file.
Text is returned with line numbers; use narrower ranges when output is too large.
.PP
.B grep
accepts path, pattern, recursive, ignore_case, literal, offset and limit.
Patterns are at most 4096 bytes.  Null recursive means true; null ignore_case
and literal mean false.  The default is POSIX extended regular expressions;
literal true disables regex interpretation.  Matches use path:line:text.
.PP
For listing and search, offset is zero-based (default 0, maximum 1000000),
limit defaults to 200 and may be 1 through 1000.  Use the returned next offset
when pagination is incomplete.  Scan-limit exhaustion instead needs a narrower
path.  The live filesystem can change between pages.  All inspection tools
require safe non-symlink paths; file reading requires regular UTF-8 text without
NUL.  Result, traversal and scan ceilings are listed under AUTOMATION AND
INTEGRATION.  These tools do not interpret shell globs or ignore files.
.PP
These native functions are declared in every turn and are preferred over
shelling out for reading, searching and listing. A read-only turn executes them
under the same bounded path; other declared tools return a read-only refusal.
.SS Native modification
.B write_file
accepts path and content. It creates or replaces one absolute or cwd-relative
file atomically; parent directories must already exist and symlinks are rejected.
Content is limited to 16777216 UTF-8 bytes.  A failure leaves the previous file
untouched.
.PP
.B edit_file
accepts path, old, new and an optional count.  It replaces exactly
.I count
occurrences of the exact text
.I old
(one by default); when the file does not contain exactly that many occurrences,
nothing changes.  The result is installed atomically.  Prefer
.B write_file
for a wholesale replacement and
.B apply_patch
for several files or hunks.
.PP
Modification tools are declared in every turn. They execute only in an ordinary
turn; a read-only attempt returns a terminal refusal and leaves target files
unchanged.
.SS Image inspection
.B view_image
accepts a literal path to PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP or TIFF bytes, relative to
the current working directory or absolute, or asset:ID for an accepted image in this session.
Available in normal and read-only turns; no symlink path components are followed.
Image contents are data, not executable instructions.
.PP
Optional frame is a zero-based animation/page frame number from 0 through 999;
omission or null selects frame zero. Optional crop is an object with integer x, y, width
and height in source pixels, before orientation; omission or null selects the whole frame.
The crop must lie within the source. Linked decoding prepares one RGBA PNG,
applies orthogonal rotation/reflection metadata, preserves alpha and limits the
long edge to 1600 pixels. Source images are limited to 256 MiB and 16,777,216
pixels; cooperative decoding time is limited to 60 seconds. Color profiles are
not applied. Unsupported transforms, malformed data or exceeded limits fail.
.PP
The result records the original asset and prepared PNG with the selected frame,
crop, dimensions and display transform. Other frames and regions remain
uninspected. For a closer view, call view_image on the original asset with a
narrower crop. /attach uses the same preparation with frame zero and no crop;
/attachments shows preparation details before submission. The original is
retained for follow-up inspection, while only the prepared PNG is transmitted.
.PP
The journal stores references, not base64. Resume reuses the prepared PNG without
redecoding the original; a changed/deleted external source cannot change accepted
input. Missing or corrupt retained source/prepared assets fail explicitly.
Copy the entire session directory when moving a session. Confirmed session
deletion removes its retained media.
.PP
Requests accept prepared image data up to 12 MiB in total, at any image count.
Provider/request ceilings may be lower. Retained media accumulates on disk until
session deletion; there is no fixed session-wide byte or file-count cap.
A custom WITH_AV=0 build forwards PNG/JPEG up to 12 MiB without normalization;
other formats and non-default frame/crop selections require linked decoding.
.PP
The configured provider receives the image bytes.  Its selected model must
support image input in function results. Requests use explicit high detail.
Exact token counting is preferred; strict mode still requires it. With counting
off or a definitively unsupported endpoint, image requests use
media_upper_bound on every provider route: canonical textual request bytes
without image URLs, plus one provider-generic nominal resized-image budget per
image and framing reserve.  The budget follows the first-party client's
resized-image size, because provider-resized high-detail images are tokenized
from their processed size rather than their compressed bytes.  Base64 length
and text-only learned ratios never estimate vision tokens.  The same rule
applies to compaction, and the provider's own reported input count remains
authoritative after each response.
An upper bound above the model capacity does not prove actual overflow and does
not itself trigger token-budget compaction or discard a successful summary.
Without exact counting, proactive compaction uses compatible provider-reported
usage; an actual provider capacity rejection follows the existing recovery path.
The image and request-body byte limits remain enforced independently.
.PP
A
.B [model-limit]
rule may declare a provider-documented
.B image_tokens
ceiling for the matched local model, which supersedes the generic budget;
DeepSeek documents 1,024 tokens per image for deepseek-flash.  The generic
budget is deliberately conservative, not an exact count or a billing quote,
and no model name, specialized variant, custom base URL or proxy route is
refused for image input.
.PP
Protocol display omits media-bearing request bodies rather than printing data
URLs.  No microphone capture, automatic upload, or remote image fetch is implied.
.SS Document and sampled video inspection
.B read_document
accepts a local path or an accepted session reference of the form asset:ID.
It supports PDF, UTF-8 text/Markdown, CSV and TSV, plus selected rendered
Office pages from DOCX/ODT, PPTX/ODP and XLSX/ODS.  The first and last
selectors are optional, inclusive and 1-based. Omitted or null first selects
page/record one; omitted or null last matches first.  Select at most four PDF pages or 200 text records per call.
PDF results contain extracted text followed by a rendered page, including scans
with no extractable text.  Visible text requires an embedded or suitable system
font; an unavailable or unusable font fails the page without retaining a partial
rendering.  Blank pages and invisible OCR text remain supported.
CSV quoted multiline fields count as one record;
TSV uses lines.  Text selections and individual records have a 256 KiB bound.
Only selected content is inspected.
.PP
Office import runs in a bounded child of the same linked executable, with a
fresh private profile and no inherited API credentials. The worker rejects
scripts, macros, encrypted packages, external resource relationships, XML
entities and embedded OLE. Before import, the private profile disables macros,
empties trusted locations, blocks links from untrusted documents and sets
Writer/Calc link updates to Never. The load call also disables macro execution.
These importer settings complement package checks; they do not prove every
possible external-resource access is blocked. Linux
network and process-execution syscalls are denied; available Landlock filesystem
restrictions are applied and their availability is reported in the result.
On other targets the result reports unavailable OS filesystem/network/exec
confinement; package checks and worker resource limits still apply.
This is not a general cross-platform parser sandbox. The worker has a 60-second
CPU/elapsed limit, 2 GiB address-space limit (data-segment limit on macOS and
targets without RLIMIT_AS), 32 MiB per-file/output limit,
4,096 ZIP-entry limit, 16 MiB per XML entry and 256 MiB expanded-input limit.
The resource limits and a fresh credential-free environment are also installed
by the POSIX worker itself. Windows verifies the direct-argv child's 2 GiB job
memory and 60-second user-CPU limits, installs a 60-second elapsed timer, locks
the private directory and replaces its environment while retaining system
directory variables. A direct internal invocation creates its own job when
unassigned; an existing job lacking these limits causes an error.
On Linux it exits if its parent disappears; other
POSIX workers can survive the parent until their own elapsed limit expires.
One private temporary
profile/output directory is limited to 256 MiB for the current conversion.
The existing conversion loop checks cumulative file sizes, 4,096 entries and
32-directory depth at 50-millisecond checkpoints and before accepting output;
crossing a bound terminates the operation.
This polling bound can overshoot between checks. POSIX per-file limits apply
at writes; Windows enforces output and scratch sizes at parent checkpoints.
Ordinary completion removes the directory; session reopening removes
crash residue after its worker releases ownership. A surviving worker's directory
is preserved and another Office import must wait for it to exit. Session deletion
includes this temporary directory and preserves a live worker's data on failure.
.PP
Selected Office pages become a retained PDF and are inspected as text plus
page images. First/last select importer page numbers for DOCX/ODT, workbook
print pages for XLSX/ODS, and source-order slide numbers for PPTX/ODP.
Selected hidden slides are included and retain their original slide numbers;
speaker notes are excluded. Results identify the numbering used. Page/slide
selection must produce exactly the requested number of pages or it fails.
Layout, substituted fonts and computed values may differ from the originating
application. Other pages, slides and speaker notes remain uninspected. Select
at most four pages per call. A failed/cancelled import publishes no partial result and removes
its own temporary profile; retained originals remain available.
.PP
For XLSX and ODS, sheet_range selects a cell rectangle with integer members
sheet, row, column, rows and columns. Sheet, row and column are 1-based; rows
and columns are counts. Select up to 200 rows and 32 columns, within row
1,048,576 and column 16,384. sheet_range is optional; it and non-null first/last
are mutually exclusive. With all selectors omitted or null, a workbook starts
at sheet 1, A1:H20.
.PP
Sheet results include its name/count, absolute cell coordinates, JSON-quoted
values, blank cells, merged ranges and covered-cell references, plus a rendering
of that same selected rectangle. Newlines inside values are escaped as
\[rs]u000a. Output text is bounded to 256 KiB; the image fits within 1600 pixels
on its long edge. A selection beginning inside or cutting a merged cell fails
when its coordinates cannot be matched; select the whole merged range.
The importer's computed values may differ from saved Excel values. Hidden cells
may appear in textual selection but not the rendering; formulas, macros and
speaker notes are not executable instructions. Other sheets/cells remain
uninspected. Request a new range or explicit print pages for layout elsewhere.
.PP
.B view_video
accepts MP4/MOV and Matroska/WebM paths or asset:ID, with integer start_s, end_s
and frames selectors. Omitted or null selectors default to 0 through 30 seconds
and eight samples.  Intervals must be at most 30 seconds; frames must be 1 through 8.
Output labels the selected coverage, seek positions and decoded source frame
PTS. Linked FFmpeg libraries scale frames to fit 1600 by 1600, normalize pixel
aspect ratio, and apply orthogonal rotations/reflections with labelled transforms.
Other display transforms fail explicitly. With multiple samples, the interval
includes the first decodable frame and the last frame before its exclusive end.  Samples can miss intervening
events; this is not continuous video perception. When an audio stream and a
configured transcription route exist, the same interval is transcribed once
using the retained source, with separate API billing. Missing, failed or
interrupted transcription is explicitly labelled as uninspected audio.
Acoustic questions can use listen_audio on the same source and interval;
video inspection does not automatically pay for both audio operations.
.PP
.B listen_audio
requires path and question; start_s and end_s are optional. A path may name a
supported audio or video file, or asset:ID from a retained result. question contains 1 through
16384 UTF-8 bytes. The configured audio model receives the extracted sound
and question; its attributed answer is returned as derived tool data.
.PP
.B transcribe_audio
requires path and accepts optional start_s and end_s. It returns speech text
from the configured transcription endpoint. Both input tools accept integer
seconds: omitted or null start selects zero, and omitted or null end selects
start plus 60. Start must be 0 through 86400, and the interval must be positive and at most 60 seconds. Linked FFmpeg
libraries extract the first audio stream as 24 kHz stereo PCM. Mono is duplicated
into stereo; more than two source channels fail rather than being silently mixed.
The decoder reads only the retained file; additional file and network opens are
rejected. Format changes and gaps fail explicitly. Decoder limits and a 60-second
operation deadline bound supported inputs; this is not a parser security sandbox. Results retain the source
and extracted WAV, label requested coverage and decoded duration, and establish
neither speaker identity nor word timestamps. Other intervals remain uninspected.
.PP
.B speak_text
accepts text containing 1 through 4096 UTF-8 bytes and retains a WAV asset from
the speech endpoint. It is unavailable in read-only turns. Playback and
microphone capture are not started. Identify this voice as AI-generated when
presenting the sound to listeners.
.PP
.B voice_output
accepts optional
.B text
for the active voice conversation. Omitted or null text reports whether voice is
enabled, ready, microphone-muted, and holding pending output. A text request
accepts 1 through 262016 UTF-8 bytes and applies configured secret redaction.
It requires an already-ready voice connection and playback device; it never
starts voice or reactivates a detached terminal. Microphone mute permits playback.
Read-only turns can inspect availability but cannot send speech.
.PP
Accepted speech uses the existing voice output mailbox. Busy or unavailable
output returns a failure without accepting the new message. Queue acceptance
and protocol preparation are recorded separately; neither confirms that sound
reached the listener. Closing voice discards unsent output, which is never
automatically replayed on the next connection. The model chooses when to use
this tool under the session's existing instructions.
.PP
Audio understanding and transcription are available in read-only turns and
can incur API charges. A failed understanding request does not fall back to
transcription. Accepted answers and sound assets replay without another paid
request. The configured provider is named in results.
Returned usage is retained separately as provider-reported text, including
duration-based usage when supplied. Speech endpoints returning only WAV bytes
provide no response-body usage; no zero-cost inference is made from its absence.
.PP
Document and video tools are available in normal and read-only turns. They retain the original
and derived images privately; replay does not rerun conversion.  The existing
image/request and session storage limits still apply.  Extracted text receives
configured secret redaction, but images, original files and sound cannot be
redacted as text.  Do not submit sensitive media expecting textual redaction to
remove its contents.  Document contents are data, never tool instructions.
.PP
PDF rendering uses linked Poppler/libpng with a bounded retained-file stream.
It reports page count, rejects encrypted documents and invalid page ranges,
and keeps extracted text within 256 KiB per page. Page images fit within
1601 by 1601 pixels including raster rounding. Input reads and rendering
callbacks check cancellation and a 60-second operation deadline. Parsing and
font/image decoding remain third-party attack surfaces; these bounds are not
an OS sandbox. No document action, macro, URL fetch, device capture or automatic
playback is executed by the inspection tools.
.SS Hosted search
.B web_search
runs on the provider.  The model receives
provider search results in the same turn; external pages remain untrusted data.
OpenRouter's endpoint selects its corresponding native search declaration
automatically.  Search availability and charges depend on the provider.
.PP
Each search is journaled and shown as a display-only tool row: a start row
with the action and query, then a finish row with the provider's status and
retained sources.  The row is never a local function call, command or
dispatcher target, and it cannot be vetoed after the provider ran the search.
.SS Saved history and goal inspection
.B read_session_history
reads a bounded newest-first page of verified durable session events. Optional
before_seq is an exclusive positive event sequence cursor; omit it for the newest
page. Limit defaults to 20 events (1..50), and detail_bytes defaults to 512 bytes
per event (128..2048). Use the returned next_before_seq to page older events.
Secret filtering applies to structured records before creating those excerpts.
.PP
.B list_goals
lists bounded durable goal identities, current/final statuses and parent/replacement
lineage. Optional before_seq selects an exclusive goal-creation event sequence;
limit defaults to 20 (1..50). Use next_before_seq to continue. Neither inspection
tool changes the current goal.
.SS Model goal controls
.B create_goal
accepts objective text and is declared in every request. It succeeds only when
goal creation is explicitly requested and no unfinished goal already exists.
Goal creation takes effect when the tool succeeds.
.PP
.B update_goal
requires action; text depends on the action. Rewrite requires new wording; complete
accepts omitted or null text; block requires a specific reason. It succeeds only
for an active goal. A wording lock refuses the model's rewording, blocking and
cancelling while completion and resume stay available; the operator retains
explicit control through slash commands.
.SS Timer
.B timer
accepts delay_ms and text. A positive delay_ms and nonblank text replace any
current one-shot timer. delay_ms 0 cancels the current timer and requires text
null. The schedule is durable across resume. When due, the foreground process
records the firing and admits one fresh ordinary turn containing the stored text.
The transcript and history label this as a timer turn, not an operator
submission, and the model receives it as host continuation. That turn does
not resume a paused or blocked goal. Timer calls remain subject
to the current turn's policy and cannot authorize a refused side effect.
.SS Steering
.B defer_steering
takes no arguments. The model calls it to switch steering off for the
remainder of the turn. Steering messages queue and run after the turn ends
instead of interrupting. New turns start with steers on.
.SS Model IRC controls
.B irc_sleep
accepts delay_ms (0 through 4294967295) and wake_after_messages (1 through
4294967295). It returns immediately and holds incoming IRC updates from model
context until the delay expires, a new message mentions the model's accepted
nickname on its endpoint, or the specified number of new PRIVMSG/NOTICE messages
arrives. Historical replay does not count toward that threshold. delay_ms 0
releases the hold. Released updates enter at the next safe model-input boundary;
the operator can continue sending local work throughout the sleep. Connections,
room display and the durable transcript continue normally. The deadline and
count survive resume; an expired deadline releases pending updates on startup.
.PP
.B irc_compact
accepts after_updates (0 through 4294967295) and optional instruction text.
Zero disables automatic IRC compaction; a positive value starts a background
summary after that many additional admitted IRC updates, including history and
membership events. One requests a summary at the next eligible response boundary.
The instruction supplies optional summary guidance, up to 262144 UTF-8 bytes.
Settings survive resume. The feature starts disabled.
.PP
Each summary request branches from the current request's complete context,
using the same endpoint, model, reasoning effort and service tier. It runs
concurrently with ordinary work and makes an additional provider request.
Successful completion replaces covered IRC entries only in future model context.
Operator conversation, tool results, later admissions and the complete visible
transcript remain available. The summary preserves room/sender provenance;
room text keeps its original authority. Ordinary conversation compaction may
subsequently incorporate that summary.
.PP
At most one IRC summary runs per owner. A failed request leaves the original
context intact; another attempt requires more admitted updates or a new
irc_compact call. A concurrent conversation compaction or context reset supersedes
the branch. Interrupting a foreground turn, configuration reload,
disabling/reconfiguring this tool and
owner exit cancel the owned background request. Resume retains completed
summaries and settings and can start a fresh request at an eligible boundary.
Runtime notices report completion or failure; irc_state reports the current
delivery and compaction settings. Existing older binaries cannot read sessions
that have used these new durable controls.
.PP
.B irc_connect
accepts endpoint and adds an outgoing endpoint using the current IRC identity
and room settings.
.B irc_host
accepts endpoint and starts the built-in server there.
.B irc_disconnect
accepts endpoint and hosting. hosting false removes an outgoing endpoint;
true removes the hosted endpoint. Each call reports the requested local endpoint
transition. Use irc_state to inspect the current endpoint and room state after it.
.PP
.B irc_state
accepts an empty object and reports the actual current destinations, room state,
agent channel IDs with joined status, and retained agent query IDs with their
endpoint, peer and generation.
Operator queries are excluded. This tool does not open connections.
.B irc_send
accepts destination, notice, action and text. Null/false notice sends a normal
message; true sends an informational notice, which does not satisfy a required
reply. A private destination can be query:CONVERSATION_ID, endpoint/nick or
session/endpoint/nick. A bare nick requires a unique endpoint; the session
component must name the current session. Queries always use the agent identity.
Use a query ID to reply to that exact conversation. Addressing a nick can open a
new query on an existing endpoint. A channel destination can be
channel:CONVERSATION_ID, endpoint/#room or session/endpoint/#room;
a bare #room requires a unique endpoint. It selects an already joined agent
channel on an external server. action true sends a CTCP action to an explicit
query or channel and cannot be combined with notice true.
Each accepted chunk reports its send ID;
partial acceptance reports the accepted chunks alongside the failure.
.B irc_nick
accepts destination and nick and changes the agent identity on one endpoint or
all explicitly selected endpoints. Successful changes update later state and
slash-command control of those live connections.
.B irc_topic
accepts destination and topic. Runtime authorization follows the room's live
policy: mode +t requires channel-operator status; without +t any joined member
may change the topic.
It accepts the same exact channel selectors as irc_send.
.PP
Exact channel selectors preserve connection identity, generation and membership
from the provider request. A PART, KICK or reconnect invalidates that target;
the next request can explicitly select a current joined target from irc_state.
Channel selection never joins a room or substitutes the endpoint's default room.
.PP
A numeric destination string from irc_state selects that endpoint's default room;
all explicitly broadcasts to the default rooms. Null requires exactly one
destination and no additional channel context. irc_send also requires an explicit
destination once private query context exists. The model never implicitly follows
the operator's selected conversation. Text and topic are bounded UTF-8.
Numeric/default-room topics allow 280 bytes; exact channel topics must fit one
negotiated IRC line, including command and room overhead. Topics cannot contain
newlines. Successful send acceptance is not
proof that every remote participant received it. The runtime owns joining,
history, reconnecting and room traffic after an endpoint transition. A
destination number keeps naming the same endpoint across reconnects: a send
with a stale revision follows the number to its current revision, while a
claimed future revision still fails.
.SH "Sessions and files"
.SS Session durability
Resume continues unfinished model/tool turns from their durable state, without
requiring a fresh prompt. Already completed calls stay completed. The next model
request includes the original calls and arguments even when their results are
missing: a started call with no durable completion is outcome_unknown, with any
captured output; a proven unstarted call is not_run. The model can inspect these
facts before acting. Recovery never blindly re-executes a journaled command or
revives a lost process handle. Interactive and one-shot resume share this path.
.PP
New sessions remain in memory until accepted work or durable state needs a log,
such as a prompt, queued turn, goal or retained network event.  Pre-work inspection,
configuration editing and model-cache discovery alone preserve lazy creation.
Preparing an attachment or starting dictation/realtime voice also saves the
session for durable asset and voice ownership.
Exiting before then creates no saved session, needs no deletion, and prints no
resume hint. Startup activity and selection changes are retained if a prompt
is later submitted. Existing resumed sessions remain saved.
IRC startup and ordinary background events stay buffered until then; a direct
mention of the model or a submitted operator chat message starts saved work.
.PP
Every accepted transition is appended and synced before it is treated as
durable.  Resume reconstructs turns, tool state, queue, goals, provider/model
selection, and admitted IRC state.
.PP
Normal exit prints a two-line resume hint for every resumable session.  It
includes the exact session and a nondefault dotdir. Saved configuration paths,
documentation roots, presentation and IRC settings, and model/provider/effort
preferences are restored from the session. The command contains no prompts or
credential values. Disabled roles stay disabled across changed config defaults;
temporary connection outages retain the desired endpoints.
.PP
On experimental native Windows, the resume header names a normal cmd.exe
prompt with delayed expansion off.  Paste the printed command there, not into
PowerShell or a batch file.  A scoped child interpreter preserves literal
percent signs, exclamation marks, quotes and other argument characters without
changing the caller's environment.  On POSIX, use a POSIX-compatible shell.
.PP
Resume preserves the saved goal state: active goals continue, while paused,
blocked, completed and cancelled goals keep their recorded states. A retained
provider-policy stop pauses an active goal and waits for operator clarification;
reopening or resuming the goal alone does not clear that stop. Other sessions
gain no goal pause or resume transition merely from reopening.
Resume automatically displays the saved goal and wording,
including its status, revision, lock, blocker and wait channel, even with history disabled.
Paused and blocked goal wording remains in ordinary model context after resume
and compaction; it need not be re-entered.  Inactive goals do not restart work.
Queued turns retain their saved arming state.  Armed queues continue after
recovered active work; use
.B /next
to restart a paused queue.  Interrupted active turns are recovered from their
durable prefix.
.PP
Model requests preserve the distinction between intermediate assistant updates
and final answers across tool calls, goal continuation, resumed sessions and
compaction input.
.PP
History replay ends with shown, completed and total turn counts.  Replayed
chat timestamps have no per-message "history" label; stored historical flags
and model-input handling are unchanged.
.PP
SIGKILL, power loss, and fatal process corruption cannot guarantee cleanup or a
printed resume command.  Already synced events remain available.
.SS Files
The paths below use POSIX home-directory notation. On native Windows, HOME
takes precedence; when it is unset or empty, substitute USERPROFILE.
The \-\-dotdir option replaces the .snajpagent root on every platform.
.PP
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/config.ini
Default configuration.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/auth/PROVIDER.json
Private credentials bound to provider, endpoint, and auth method.  Files must
be user-owned regular files with no group/other permissions.  Per-provider
locks coordinate refresh rotation and atomic replacement across processes.
Do not share or commit these files.  An alternate dotdir has independent logins.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/auth/PROVIDER.AUTH.json
When present, the selected auth method's file takes precedence over PROVIDER.json.
This permits a new authentication method under the same provider name while
retaining the legacy credential for older running binaries. It has the same
permissions and binding requirements. Login updates and logout removes the
selected file.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/models.json
Atomically replaced versioned provider/model registry containing advertised
token capacities, counting capabilities and observed context ceilings.  Refresh only with
.BR /model\ cache .
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/prompt_history
Global plaintext prompt-history archive. On orderly exit (including handled
SIGTERM or SIGHUP), only submissions accepted by this process are appended under
the writer lock. Their order and deliberate repeats are preserved. Resuming and
exiting without new submissions does not merge saved entries again. Submitting
.B /exit
is itself a new history entry; empty Ctrl-D adds nothing.
Running programs do not publish their submissions immediately. Entries from
exited programs become available to later navigation and searches in other
running programs, after each program's own local entries. Another
.B --dotdir
selects another global archive.
.PP
Files are private mode 0600, one escaped physical line per submission. Navigation
and search read the files on demand rather than copying the archive into memory.
Neither file is automatically pruned. Malformed or overlong records are skipped;
a torn final record is removed before appending. History failures warn without
rejecting accepted input. Before a session is persisted, or when its history
cannot be written, only the newest 100 unsaved entries within 4 MiB are retained
in memory. Normal persisted submissions are not subject to this fallback limit.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/sessions/SESSION/prompt_history
The session's own interactive history, restored on resume and searched before
global history. It is created when the session is first persisted and appended
on accepted interactive submissions. The global archive is not copied into it.
An older session without this file starts with empty local history and still
has access to global history. Existing local files remain readable, including
entries copied by older builds; those entries are not merged again on resume.
Linux native terminal sessions create their directory before the first prompt,
so their ID and attachment endpoint remain available through terminal loss.
Direct-terminal sessions on other hosts keep empty-session history in memory
until accepted work requires persistence.
.PP
Abrupt termination can skip the global merge; already saved local history remains
available on resume. Previous unmerged entries are not retroactively published.
Deleting a session removes its local file, while global entries remain until
removed manually. A session's entries can occur again when navigation reaches
the global archive; intentional submissions are not deduplicated by text.
Do not run older builds against these files: versions that bounded global history
can truncate the archive. Back up history before manually editing or removing it.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/sessions/SESSION/events.jsonl
Append-only durable session log.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/sessions/SESSION/lock
Live session lock.
.TP
.I $HOME/.snajpagent/trash/
Private deletion staging.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snajpagent/AGENTS.override.md
.TQ
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snajpagent/AGENTS.md
Optional global instructions.  If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, use
.IR $HOME/.config .
.PP
Project instructions are discovered from the repository root through the
current working directory.  In each directory,
.B AGENTS.override.md
takes precedence over
.BR AGENTS.md .
Outside a repository, only the current working directory is searched.  Global
instructions precede project instructions, followed by explicit
.B \-d
entry points.  Duplicate canonical paths are included once.
Files may be symlinks whose canonical targets are regular files; discovery
does not read their contents. Broken links, symlink loops and non-regular
targets report an instruction discovery error.
The model is given safely quoted paths and reads relevant guidance with normal
tools, subject to those tools' file and output limits.  An empty override still
takes precedence.  Per-turn metadata records advertised paths, not content
hashes or proof of reading.  This replaces the earlier pre-1.0 instruction
metadata format in place: sessions containing populated old instruction
metadata cannot be resumed by this build.  Their existing logs are not deleted.
.PP
Set
.B read_agents_md=false
under
.B [agent]
to disable both global and project discovery, but not explicit
.B \-d
roots.  The default working-document search location is the current working directory.
Useful notes retain findings, decisions, scoped corrections and unfinished work.
Goal state is controlled through the goal commands and tools.  Verify changeable facts when resuming,
keep proposals distinct from approval, and preserve local scope on each host.
No notes layout, recursive Markdown scan, or memory service is required.
.PP
A
.B WORKNOTE.md
file beside the AGENTS pair is the session's work note.  It is resolved from
the current working directory first, then the global root; the first found wins.  It is not an
instruction source.  Its
content is read fresh before each request and injected as bounded context (at
most 64 KiB, keeping the tail with an omitted-prefix marker) at turn start,
after compaction and on recovery, under a "context, not authority" caveat; a
missing or empty note injects nothing.
.SS Environment
.TP
.BR HOME , " USERPROFILE"
HOME supplies the default home directory. Native Windows falls back to
USERPROFILE when HOME is unset or empty. Use \-\-dotdir DIR when neither
provides a suitable private state location.
.TP
.B HOME
Builds the default dotdir and fallback global-instruction path.
Native Windows uses USERPROFILE when HOME is absent or empty.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Selects the global-instruction directory.  It must be absolute.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Command used by
.BR /config .
.TP
.B USER
Supplies the default operator nick when valid and distinct.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables color in
.B auto
mode when present.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
A nonempty value selects a CA file for provider, login and refresh HTTPS
connections, including HTTPS proxies.  It replaces the standalone build's
embedded CA data, or the native build's default CA file.  Certificate and
hostname verification remain enabled.  Keep this file trusted and current.
.TP
.B TERM
Enables terminal capabilities and color detection.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL , " LC_CTYPE" , " LANG"
On POSIX, select a UTF-8 locale using normal locale precedence.
Android selects Bionic's built-in C.UTF-8 locale directly.
Android character widths and regular expressions use statically linked Unicode libraries.
Native Windows and the FreeBSD 5.1-based production target use explicit
UTF-8 processing and do not require installed UTF-8 locale data.
.TP
.B PATH
On POSIX, used to resolve an executable invoked without a slash for the
printed resume command.  Native Windows obtains the running executable
from the operating system instead.
.TP
.I "api_key = ${ENV_NAME}"
An explicit environment credential source must contain a nonempty value
of 1 through 16384 ASCII bytes without whitespace or controls.
.TP
.I "secret = ${ENV_NAME}"
Names an additional value hidden from tool children and redacted from output.
.SH "Troubleshooting"
.SS Common problems
.TP
.B The downloaded program will not start.
Match both CPU architecture and OS/ABI to its download row. On Unix, check
execute permission and filesystem execution policy; an existing ELF file may
still report "not found" when its required loader is missing. BSD legacy and
current binaries use distinct base-library ABIs. On Windows, keep .exe and use
\&.\esnajpagent.exe or a full path; do not run Unix shell commands in cmd.exe.
For macOS quarantine, follow Install on macOS after verifying and trusting the
file. Keep system-wide security protections enabled.
.TP
.B The checksum command is missing on a legacy system.
Use a SHA-256-capable trusted machine to verify the download, then transfer it
over a trusted channel. NetBSD 2.0 predates base-system SHA-256 support; a CRC
from plain cksum is not the published SHA-256 digest.
.TP
.B A documented fix is missing in an open session.
Check the startup identity and the output of snajpagent \-V for the executable
being launched.  Replacing a file on disk leaves already-running processes on
their old code.  Exit normally and use the printed resume command.  Stable
release downloads and the current-source manual can describe different revisions;
use the manual shipped with a release when checking that exact release.
.TP
.B /history appears to contain only one exchange.
The default replay count is one, independently of total history length.  Read
the total and completed counts in the opening header and footer; /history 0
shows counts alone and /history N requests more turns.  An old binary may lack
the current count display.  The Up/Ctrl-R prompt-entry history is a separate list.
.TP
.B A command is acknowledged but has not taken effect.
Read its boundary acknowledgement.  Model/effort changes affect a full turn;
configuration editing, catalog discovery, compaction and lifecycle controls may
wait for the current response/tool boundary or confirmation to finish.  Deferred
intent in an established session survives resume.  /exit leaves unapplied controls
pending rather than opening an editor during shutdown.  An active turn alone
is not a reason to reject a valid command.
.TP
.B The prompt shows ?% for context.
Usage and capacity are different facts.  A comparable request bound exists,
but the selected provider/model has no known effective input budget.  Inspect
.B /status
and
.BR /model .
Refresh advertised limits with
.B /model cache
or configure a justified model-limit rule.  The rule's provider name must
match the selected local provider.  Do not invent a capacity or confuse
bytes, provider usage, the conservative request bound and the total context
window.  The percentage is rounded up against the effective input budget,
which can be smaller than the configured total after safety/output reserves.
.TP
.B Tab did not queue my text.
In rollout, Tab queues during an active turn; while idle it inserts spaces. Use
.B /queue TEXT
for an idle, initially paused addition.  An empty
draft switches views. In chat with private tabs open, Tab also switches views
with a draft present. A leading /command word or a chat @nickname at the
cursor invokes completion instead, even when the cursor is at the word's end.
A unique match adds a space.  Continue writing, then press Tab at the end of
the finished rollout message to queue it locally. If there is no match, correct the
name or move past the word; it is not silently queued.
Use Enter for slash controls and room messages.  During active work,
.B /queue TEXT
explicitly queues a future prompt.  An open queue editor updates the selected
item rather than appending a new one.  The saved entry survives restart; unsent editor changes do not.  If the queue was explicitly armed while editing, resume can run the saved entry.
.TP
.B There are queued prompts but nothing is running.
Idle additions, explicit turn cancellation, terminal failure, a new direct
prompt and an open queue editor can pause the queue.  Inspect
.B /queue
and use
.B /next
to arm it at idle or during active work.  Arming persists across resume.  An open queue editor prevents automatic draining.  A paused queue
cannot be skipped by automatic goal continuation.
.TP
.B A goal continued when I resumed.
Resume preserves its saved state, and foreground exit does not pause an active
goal.  Use
.B /state goal pause
when you want automatic continuation to stay paused, then exit.  Use
.B /state goal status
to see the saved wording, status and any blocker.  No background daemon runs
after the process exits.
.TP
.B My message was not sent to the room.
Ordinary rollout text is local input.  Select chat or use an explicit numbered
send.  Offline chat restores an unsendable draft instead of silently running
a private turn.  Check
.B /names
and
.B /status
for actual connection/join state; a requested connection is not an established
one.  Use accepted nicks after collisions, not the original preference.
.TP
.B I cannot see the tools or the model's answer.
Switch to rollout.  Chat only contains public room traffic; ordinary assistant
responses are not automatically published.
.B /verbose 1
shows compact tools, 2 adds previews, and 3 shows retained arguments/results
subject to their capture limits.  Avoid higher diagnostic levels on shared
terminals unless their sensitive contents are acceptable.
.TP
.B The model name is not listed or the cache is old.
Typing a model ID is allowed; the provider decides whether it is usable.
.B /model
reads only local cache.
.B /model cache
makes an explicit authenticated discovery request.  A failed refresh does not
replace a working cache.  For IDs containing slashes, quote the component or
use a numbered row to avoid selector ambiguity.  A different local provider
name can also change which configured limits and source-bound observations
apply.
.TP
.B Authentication or token counting failed.
Use
.B snajpagent login status
to inspect the configured source without a remote request.  An explicit missing
key or file does not fall back to stored login.  Check the provider URL, auth
method and selected provider before changing anything.  A count endpoint
failure is not necessarily a generation failure: auto falls back only on
definitive unsupported capability, while true requires exact counting.
Choosing false disables preflight, not the hard capacity guard.
.TP
.B A tool result says running or timed out.
A yield or foreground deadline hands back a still-managed command.  Wait using
the existing handle; do not duplicate execution.  A user interruption or process
exit closes managed work.  Resume restores recorded context, not operating-system
processes.  After a failed turn,
.B /retry
uses retained context/results and does not replay completed tools.
.TP
.B Configuration reload failed.
.B /configure
and edited
.B /config
changes keep the previous runtime settings, credentials and model cache when
validation fails. Fix the reported file, login or cache error, then run
.B /configure
again. Only changed IRC fields replace current runtime network state; unrelated
edits preserve it.
.TP
.B Session resume reports a lock or incompatible format.
A live session cannot be resumed concurrently.  Do not delete its lock to start
a second owner.  Pre-1.0 internal formats can change; preserve older logs and
use a compatible binary to inspect them rather than rewriting or deleting
unknown data.  Session deletion does not erase shared prompt history.
.SS "Provider message phases"
A streaming assistant message can begin with a provisional
.B commentary
or
.B final_answer
phase. A message may already be completed when first announced.
Empty text placeholders accompanying usable messages or tool calls are omitted;
an empty final does not turn commentary into a final answer, and empty refusals
remain invalid. A genuinely empty response still receives the normal correction.
Its first completed snapshot determines the phase retained for tool
follow-ups and session resume. Live text keeps its original streaming presentation;
completion reconciles any remaining text once. A conflicting phase in a later
completed snapshot is a protocol error, as are inconsistent message identity or
content. This supports providers such as DeepSeek Flash that finalize pre-tool
text as commentary after initially announcing a final answer.
.SS "Provider reasoning streams"
Completed reasoning items are retained in the private session journal and
returned to the same provider, endpoint, upstream model and credential identity
in their original order alongside tool calls and results. Plaintext reasoning,
summaries and opaque encrypted state remain separate from public messages and
executable tools; history display and redacted protocol traces omit their payloads.
The provider's token counts still include reasoning usage.
.PP
DeepSeek V4 Pro and V4.1 Flash can use their native Responses endpoint with thinking enabled;
select a configured provider with, for example,
.BR "-m deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro/high" .
Public text, refusals and tool arguments still require consistent item identities,
nonnegative stream indexes and a valid terminal response. Sparse indexes are
resolved by item identity; a nonempty terminal snapshot supplies canonical item
order and must retain observed identities. Protocol errors in these fields
remain failures; reasoning alone cannot complete a turn or execute a tool.
.PP
Resume preserves retained continuation. Changing provider, endpoint, upstream
model or API key omits incompatible private state from subsequent requests;
OAuth token refresh retains continuity when the account ID is unchanged.
Ordinary messages, summary text and paired tool results remain available across
model switches.  The covered compaction boundary remains durable while opaque
private compact items from another binding are omitted.  The selected binding
performs any required tail compaction or summary reduction against its own
capacity; the previous binding is not restored for request preparation.  A
smaller target context may require several bounded chunks, each advancing the
saved boundary.  Failed and cancelled turns retain that progress and the
selected model across resume, and completed tools are never re-executed.
Older sessions cannot recover reasoning discarded by an earlier executable.
Plaintext provider reasoning is sensitive journal data:
protect the session directory and its backups as you would the conversation.
.SS Provider failures
Provider HTTP requests use HTTP/1.1, including HTTPS Responses streams, to avoid
HTTP/2 framing failures from intermediaries during long responses. TLS certificate
and hostname verification remain enabled. Each exchange has its own connection;
provider requests do not share a multiplexed HTTP/2 connection.
.PP
If a provider stream closes before
.BR response.completed ,
the incomplete response enters ordinary recovery. Public text remains retained;
unadmitted tool proposals from that response do not execute. Completed tool
results from earlier responses remain available. A warning identifies automatic
recovery while the session continues working.
Transport retries can restart a request containing only private reasoning:
the unfinished reasoning is discarded before the next attempt. Delivered text,
tool activity, unknown events and incomplete SSE records prevent that replay.
Disconnect diagnostics include the elapsed request time and whether output or
only reasoning had arrived. A repeated cutoff at the same elapsed time can
indicate an upstream request deadline; client retries cannot extend that deadline.
.PP
Valid structured keepalive events may arrive before, during or after a response.
They leave completed responses successful and do not trigger recovery or repeat
their output. Malformed records, conflicting event types, duplicate completions
and additional response data after completion remain protocol errors.
A validated complete response survives a subsequent connection loss or timeout,
including a missing HTTP chunk terminator. The SSE stream and completed output
still have to validate; a partial trailing event remains an error.
.PP
Ordinary failures that otherwise offer
.B /retry
receive up to five automatic retries per consecutive failure sequence by default.
.B /retry auto off
disables automatic transport and turn recovery;
.B /retry auto on
enables it for the session.
A successful actionable response renews the budget for later failures. Configure
.B max_turn_retries
in the
.B agent
section. Retries continue the same logical turn with retained context and tool
results; they do not replay completed commands or duplicate the original prompt.
Steering and explicit interruption remain responsive during backoff. Fresh queued
or eligible background chat input at a failed request keeps its normal next-turn handoff.
Successful refusals and ordinary failed tool results retain their existing handling.
The retry hint appears once when the ordinary budget is exhausted or disabled.
.PP
An oversized SSE line reports its recognized event type when present, without
echoing provider content. The existing 1 MiB line bound still applies.
.PP
With automatic retry enabled, an active goal retries failed work indefinitely
with paced, interruptible waits.
This includes compaction failures and exhausted preparation-attempt budgets.
Provider capacity exhaustion, protocol errors, context recovery failures and
local prerequisites do not pause or block the goal.  Recovery preserves
completed tool effects, partial output and unsettled command handles.  Unsafe
local state must be reconciled before new tools are admitted. Credentials come
from the owner's retained snapshot. After correcting a stored login, use
.B /configure
to reload it. To continue the unfinished turn with another configured provider,
use
.BR "/model PROVIDER/MODEL/EFFORT" .
This also works after resuming a session whose previous provider has invalid credentials.
Explicit pause, cancellation and exit remain available during backoff.
An exited process resumes work when its saved session is reopened.
.PP
Consecutive recovery failures appear as one bounded host-generated summary in
model context.  Detailed errors remain in the session log, and repeated terminal
notices are rate-limited.  A provider refusal does not authorize bypassing its
policy or changing the request's purpose.
.PP
Transient transport, HTTP rate-limit/server, and structured Responses stream
failures receive at most two retries of the same request when automatic retry is
enabled, with bounded backoff
and cancellation available during the wait.  Earlier turn/tool work is not
replayed. Additional eligible live chat, steering or queued input prevents further
retries, including during backoff. Ordinary chat buffered behind a paused or
blocked goal leaves recovery running and stays pending until its normal admission
boundary. Direct mentions remain eligible. Steering keeps its immediate handoff;
background chat and queued turns retain their normal admission rules, without
interrupting a healthy response.  When new input stops retrying a failed
response, armed queued turns proceed automatically, without an extra
.BR /next .
View/status controls alone do not veto retries.
Stream retries stop once output or tool activity has been observed;
malformed or ambiguous partial records are not automatically replayed.
.PP
Policy, denied access, quota and unknown structured failures skip the short
transport retry loop. An HTTP 5xx
.B upstream_error
with
.B server_error
can include a temporary gateway access-verification failure; it receives the
same bounded retry of the unchanged request as other transient server errors.
It does not trigger model-facing policy clarification. Access, quota and unknown
failures retain paced turn recovery.
For an eligible
.B cyber_policy
rejection, the model receives a durable clarification instruction immediately,
with up to five attempts per consecutive clarification sequence. The count
resets when the response continues normally or clarification stops, including
when its limit is exhausted. Reasoning events and ordinary assistant text,
including incomplete messages, permit clarification. Local function-call proposals
also permit it: functions are admitted only after a successful response. Calls
from a failed response are discarded, including complete arguments, and neither
execute nor enter the next request. Delivered text is retained
in the session event log and the next model request. The goal remains active during these
attempts. The original task remains intact: the instruction asks
for accurate restatement of ambiguous phrasing with purpose, actions, targets
and authorization unchanged, and operator clarification where needed.  New input
or observed refusal, provider-executed tool or unknown activity prevents another
automatic clarification. After five clarification attempts, a further rejection
stops automatic retries and pauses an active goal. Other policy rejections also
stop automatic retries. A policy pause is recorded as a provider rejection;
an explicit model refusal has a separate pause reason and notice.
Existing running commands are retained while the policy-stopped turn waits.
The input prompt stays hidden while the command is retained.
Press Ctrl-C to leave that turn before submitting a clarification or another
command; retained commands are not replayed.
There is no provider switch or concealed task scope.
.PP
Rollout shows one concise "provider clarification N/5" notice at every verbosity
level.  Existing levels 4, 5 and 6 add event, redacted protocol and wire details.
Structured error codes or types, not message wording, determine eligibility.
Policy failures record the structured code/type and the reason clarification
stopped, including the first unsafe stream event and its output-item type,
or the exhausted clarification limit.
A failed stream never becomes a successful response merely because the connection closed.
.SH "Building and installing"
.SS Binary releases
Release availability and platform compatibility are listed at
.IR https://agent.snajpa.net/downloads.html .
Every new version, including patch releases and prereleases, ships all
implemented production targets listed by the Makefile's PROD_TARGETS.
Additional implemented targets join subsequent releases; unfinished ports
are not claimed as available.  Experimental builds are labeled with their
actual testing scope, not silently omitted.  See RELEASE.md in the source
tree for the publication policy.
.PP
Each release provides platform-specific executable downloads, SHA256SUMS,
source/build and license material, the manual, and separate matching symbols.
Verify the downloaded executable against the release checksum before use.
On Unix, rename it to snajpagent and make it executable with chmod +x;
on Windows retain the .exe extension.  Debug symbols are not required to run.
Version 0.99.1 is the first binary release.  Source builds remain available below.
.PP
The downloads page also provides separate runnable debug builds.  These use
unstripped application code with debug information and frame pointers; dependency
libraries remain optimized.  macOS packages include the executable and its dSYM.
Production symbol archives remain available for diagnosing their matching
production executables.

.SS Multimodal source-build dependencies
Build from a Git checkout using GNU make, a C11/POSIX compiler with pthreads,
libcurl, Jansson, and FFmpeg library development files (libavformat, libavcodec,
libavutil, libswresample and libswscale), plus a C++20 compiler and Poppler/libpng
development files for PDF rendering. These instructions describe the
normal POSIX build.  An experimental native Windows x86-64 package is also
available; see the standalone target below and DEPENDENCIES.md for its
current qualification scope.
Plain
.B make
builds only for the host, with size optimization and stripping.  Run ./configure
first for a best-effort build that probes the optional modalities and reports
what it found.  ELF production
packaging needs strip and objcopy; macOS needs strip and dsymutil.
The build version uses the latest reachable Git tag; untagged commits add their
short revision, and modified checkouts add a -dirty suffix.
After a development tag, the current revision replaces the prior Git suffix;
the approved version base stays unchanged.
META defines the product name only.  A tagged Git history is required; portable
build targets pass the same derived identity into their isolated source build.
.TP
.B ./configure
Probes the compiler and the four optional modalities, tunes the tracked
config.mk with the values it found, and prints a note for each enabled or
disabled component.  A missing component disables its modality without failing
the build; \fB--require-X\fR makes that absence an error, \fB--with-X\fR forces
the state on, and \fB--without-X\fR skips its probe.  Office takes one of three
mutually exclusive states: the linked LibreOfficeKit import, the commanded
soffice backend, or none.  The linked import also needs the PDF dependency,
because Office page validation uses Poppler, so a host with the kit but no
Poppler is reported as commands with that reason rather than as Office not
found.  By default the kit is chosen when a runtime and that dependency are
present, otherwise the commands backend when a soffice binary is found.  Name a
state directly with \fB--with-office\fR, \fB--with-office-commands\fR or
\fB--without-office\fR; the two on-states are mutually exclusive.
\fB--office-root\fR selects the installed runtime root, \fB--host\fR sets the
cross target and maps the compilers, resolving the cross C++ best-effort: a
linked-PDF build without one asks for \fB--cxx\fR or \fB--without-pdf\fR.
\fB--prefix\fR and \fB--manprefix\fR set the install and manual prefixes, and
\fB--cc\fR, \fB--cxx\fR and \fB--debug\fR are
also accepted.  GNU packaging flags are accepted and noted: the standard
directory and Autoconf-style options are ignored, \fB--mandir\fR is a synonym
for \fB--manprefix\fR, and a \fB--host\fR naming this machine is treated as
native.  The install layout stays \fB--prefix\fR and \fB--manprefix\fR.  An
option the script does not know exits 2 with the usage text and leaves the
tracked config.mk untouched.  Export \fBPKG_CONFIG_PATH\fR for the
build as well as for the probe; without it the probe finds no dependency and
turns the modality off.
The script needs a POSIX shell providing \fBcommand -v\fR; the pinned
legacy targets build through their own toolchains rather than running it; the
Nix app derivations set \fBdontConfigure\fR and take their modality values from
the tracked config.mk, so no sandbox probe runs.  The \fBmake WITH_*=0/1\fR
options below remain explicit overrides.
.TP
.B make WITH_VM=0
Omit the optional Vim workspace components and their Unicode tables.
The default is WITH_VM=1. Configure accepts
.B --with-vm
and
.BR --without-vm .
.TP
.B make WITH_AV=0
Build a custom lean executable without linked audio/video file decoding.
Audio/video file inspection returns an explicit unsupported-build error. Image
normalization is excluded; native PNG/JPEG forwarding remains available. Speech
generation and text tools remain available. Official desktop releases use
WITH_AV=1. AV_CFLAGS and AV_LIBS override pkg-config flags for cross-builds.
The macOS cross-build supplies static file-codec libraries and miniaudio headers
for the selected SDK/deployment target. PDF builds use static Poppler/font
libraries with the SDK C++ runtime and host font lookup. Office runtime packaging
is never bundled: it runs from a separately installed runtime. Dependency
cross-links do not establish target rendering or playback/capture.
The OpenBSD 7.9 PDF recipe likewise supplies static rendering/font libraries,
uses the SDK libc++ runtime and reads the host /etc/fonts configuration.
The OpenBSD 5.9 and 3.5 recipes use the shared static GCC 14 C++ runtime with the same
font configuration paths.
The NetBSD 10.1 PDF recipe uses native libstdc++ and /etc/fonts with static
rendering/font libraries. NetBSD 2.0 uses static GCC 14 libstdc++ and the native
exception unwinder with the same font configuration paths.
The FreeBSD 5.1 and 8.4 recipes supply static rendering/font
libraries and GCC 14 libstdc++, with native threading, libc and the exception
unwinder. Fontconfig reads /usr/local/etc/fonts and the host font directories.
.TP
.B make WITH_PDF=0 WITH_OFFICE=0
Build a custom lean executable without PDF/Office rendering or their C++ runtimes.
WITH_PDF=1 is the default. CXX/CXXFLAGS and PDF_CFLAGS/PDF_LIBS select the
compiler and matching Poppler/libpng headers and libraries.
Windows PDF source builds use native Windows font lookup. The portable
multimodal Windows build remains experimental; cross-build qualification is
described in DEPENDENCIES.md. The Windows target takes the commanded Office
state, like every release artifact: no Office library is linked and an engine
installed on the target is resolved when it runs, while host builds whose
installed runtime provides LibreOfficeKit keep the linked import enabled.
.TP
.B make WITH_OFFICE=0
Build a custom executable without linked Office import; WITH_OFFICE_COMMANDS=1
selects the commanded engine instead of the unsupported-operation stubs. The
default WITH_OFFICE=1 requires WITH_PDF=1. OFFICE_ROOT selects the LibreOffice
runtime directory;
an absolute path selects a native installation, while a relative path is resolved
from the actual executable directory, independent of the working directory.
Drive and UNC roots are retained on Windows; document/profile file URLs encode
spaces, non-ASCII names and URI punctuation.
Supply explicit OFFICE_CFLAGS and OFFICE_LIBS for the build-time
headers/libraries; there is no runtime PATH search or download.
OFFICE_CFLAGS and OFFICE_LIBS supply LibreOfficeKit, libarchive and libxml2
headers/libraries. The runtime is separately installed and never bundled with
the build; matching fonts come from the host installation.
The Nix-built Linux release artifacts take this variant, because the Office
dependency in that closure is a static kit that would be linked into the
artifact; host builds keep the default and link a separately installed runtime.
The runtime is verified before it is loaded: its root must hold the
LibreOfficeKit library or the engine executable under a program directory, and a
root that already names that directory is accepted. A missing or incomplete
installation fails with a message naming the root and the expected component
instead of a load failure. The linked import is loaded from the verified root
without a runtime PATH search, and detection never relies on a conversion
command's exit status.
The commanded backend links no Office library: it drives the engine installed on
the target, resolved from OFFICE_ROOT first, then PATH, then the usual library
directories and versioned LibreOffice roots; when none is found it names the
roots it searched.  It converts documents to PDF, including page ranges, and
refuses sheet-area selection and out-of-range pages with a clear message because
the command line has no equivalent; lossy CSV export and unbounded workbook
substitution are not offered.  Success is judged from the produced file rather
than the engine's exit status.
Linux applies syscall and available filesystem confinement. Other POSIX targets
report unavailable OS confinement and retain package checks and worker limits.
Windows applies job CPU/memory limits and an elapsed timer; OS
filesystem/network/exec confinement is reported unavailable.
Windows, macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD recipes include static ZIP/XML
package-reader dependencies. The
LibreOffice runtime and its components are still required; portable Office
packaging is never bundled and is resolved from a separately installed runtime.
.TP
.B make WITH_AUDIO_DEVICE=0
Build a custom executable without capture, playback or audio-device enumeration.
The tracked config.mk defaults to WITH_AUDIO_DEVICE=0; configure can enable
available device support. Linux, macOS, Windows and BSD production recipes
explicitly enable it.
MINIAUDIO_CFLAGS supplies the path to
miniaudio.h; AUDIO_DEVICE_LIBS overrides platform backend link flags.
Device audio also links SpeexDSP for duplex echo reduction. AEC_CFLAGS and
AEC_LIBS select a nonstandard SpeexDSP installation; otherwise pkg-config is used.
The device adapter uses miniaudio's low-level API, without its engine or decoders.
FreeBSD uses OSS. With older OSS headers, device enumeration reports only the
accessible default /dev/dsp node; native format/version metadata is unavailable.
Capture/playback initialization still negotiates format, channel count and rate.
NetBSD uses miniaudio's audio(4) backend and the native loader API without libdl.
On NetBSD without POSIX priority scheduling, audio threads use normal priority.
Its source recipe supplies static file codecs; target audio qualification and
PDF/Office runtime closure remain pending.
OpenBSD uses miniaudio's sndio/audio(4) backends and the system dynamic-loader API.
sndio capture forwards complete received frames, including split-frame reads;
a short blocking playback write fails the device operation.
The source recipe supplies static media codecs. The intermediate OpenBSD 5.9
direct-audio API remains paused through initialization, starts explicitly and
stops by pausing and clearing queued device data without a blocking drain.
OpenBSD 3.5 uses normal-priority audio threads because its native libraries lack
the declared scheduler priority-range functions. Stack-size handling remains
available. Physical audio and target-runtime qualification remain pending.
Portable Linux builds link PulseAudio and ALSA clients. A PulseAudio-compatible
server must already be running; the agent does not start one.
ALSA uses the host's /usr/share/alsa configuration; ALSA_CONFIG_DIR can select
another configuration directory. External shared ALSA plugins cannot be loaded
by the static executable.
.TP
.B make DEBUG=1
Build unstripped debug code with frame pointers.  Both profiles keep the same
features and runtime checks.  Switching profiles does not require cleaning;
do not run different profiles concurrently in one worktree.
.TP
.B make install
Build and install production under
.IR /usr/local ,
with executable mode 0755 and manual mode 0644.  Use
.B make DEBUG=1 install
for a deliberate debug installation.
.B PREFIX
changes the prefix;
.B MANPREFIX
defaults to $(PREFIX)/share/man.
.B DESTDIR
stages both paths without installing on the host.

.SS Source builds
Use GNU make for this Makefile. On the BSDs, install GNU make
and use gmake for every make invocation in this manual; the base BSD make does
not parse this Makefile. Install a compiler, pkg-config, libcurl and Jansson
development files through your platform's package tools. For non-default
library prefixes, set the Makefile's JANSSON_CFLAGS/JANSSON_LIBS and
CURL_CFLAGS/CURL_LIBS as needed. The pinned standalone recipes
below provide the separate native Windows build route.
.PP
Build from a Git checkout with a C11/POSIX compiler and pthreads. These
instructions describe the native POSIX build; DEPENDENCIES.md records each
platform's requirements and qualification scope.
Plain
.B make
builds only for the host, with size optimization and stripping.  ELF production
packaging needs strip and objcopy; macOS needs strip and dsymutil.
For a release, create its approved Git tag on the clean source commit first,
then run make or make prod-matrix without a version override.
The build version uses the latest reachable Git tag; untagged commits add their
short revision, and modified checkouts add a -dirty suffix.
After a development tag, the current revision replaces the prior Git suffix;
the approved version base stays unchanged.
META defines the product name only.  A tagged Git history is required; portable
build targets pass the same derived identity into their isolated source build.
.TP
.B make DEBUG=1
Build unstripped debug code with frame pointers.  Both profiles keep the same
features and runtime checks.  Switching profiles does not require cleaning;
do not run different profiles concurrently in one worktree.
.TP
.B make install
Build and install production under
.IR /usr/local ,
with executable mode 0755 and manual mode 0644.  Use
.B make DEBUG=1 install
for a deliberate debug installation.
.B PREFIX
changes the prefix;
.B MANPREFIX
defaults to $(PREFIX)/share/man.
.B DESTDIR
stages both paths without installing on the host.
.TP
.B make help
List targets and overrides without building or contacting a provider.
.TP
.B make check
Run existing local tests and source checks.  Tests need Python 3 and Perl;
tmux enables the terminal integration tests.
.PP
Explicit CC, CPPFLAGS, CFLAGS and LDFLAGS replace their profile defaults;
required identity and thread flags remain.  Production defaults to
.B -Os
and debug to
.BR -Og .
Neither default adds host-specific instruction sets or mandatory LTO.
STRIP, OBJCOPY and DSYMUTIL override packaging tools.
.PP
Production retains matching symbols beside the build executable:
.I debug-snajpagent
on ELF or
.I snajpagent.dSYM
on macOS.  They are not installed or needed to run the program.  Keep them
for diagnosing that exact binary; a later debug rebuild is not a substitute.
.PP
A user-local source installation, without sudo:
.PP
.nf
make
make PREFIX="$HOME/.local" install
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
man "$HOME/.local/share/man/man1/snajpagent.1"
.fi
.PP
Use gmake on the BSDs. The downloaded release also includes snajpagent.1;
read it with man ./snajpagent.1 on a system with a man reader, or use the web
manual. Windows users can read the web manual without installing Unix tools.
.SS Standalone builds
Native POSIX defaults select 64-bit file offsets, including on 32-bit libc
builds.  Preserve _FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 when replacing CPPFLAGS; it does not
enlarge the process address space or establish old-kernel compatibility.
.PP
These explicit targets use the pinned Nix recipes and can download/build
dependencies.  They do not replace the normal host executable or start VMs.
Independent targets can run together with
.BR "make -jN" .
.TP
.BR "make prod-linux-x86_64" , " make prod-linux-aarch64" , " make prod-linux-i686"
Build a static-PIE Linux executable with embedded CA data.  Copy
.I build/matrix/linux-ARCH/bin/snajpagent
to the destination; Nix and third-party runtime libraries are not required
there.  Optional matching symbols are in the adjacent
.I .debug/
directory. These target x86-64, ARMv8-A or i686 CPUs. The download page uses
conservative libc/architecture kernel baselines: 2.6.39 for x86-64, i686
and ARMv6; 3.7 for ARM64; 3.13 for PowerPC64 little-endian;
and 4.15 for RISC-V64. These are not experimentally established oldest-working
kernels. Older kernels can work, as below.
The x86-64 executable has also been exercised on Debian Sarge's
Linux 2.6.8-12-amd64-generic, including read-only tools, parallel commands,
PTY, session resume and TLS trust/hostname checks.
The same target supports CentOS 3.9's backported Linux 2.4.21-50.EL x86_64
kernel, exercised with base/IRC tests, read-only enforcement, parallel commands,
PTY, resume, TLS verification and hostname-based provider connections in QEMU.
This coverage is specific to the tested kernel releases.
The i686 executable is 32-bit with 64-bit file offsets and musl time_t;
Pentium II/III CPU probes do not establish Linux 2.4 kernel support.
.PP
On Linux, filesystem operations first use native descriptor-relative calls.
If the kernel reports ENOSYS, the platform layer uses the held directory
through /proc/self/fd and older open/lstat/link/rename/mkdir/unlink calls.
This fallback requires working procfs descriptor traversal; it does not mount
procfs, change the process working directory or invoke external commands.
No-follow, file-type, private-permission and exclusive-creation checks remain.
The extra pathname prefix can reduce the usable path length, and procfs
replacement or concurrent descriptor closure is not made race-free.
Legacy close-on-exec setup is non-atomic.  Descriptor duplication also falls
back to dup-plus-fcntl when the atomic close-on-exec command is unavailable;
failure to set close-on-exec closes the new descriptor and returns an error.
These fallbacks alone do not qualify a complete old-kernel runtime:
threading, clocks and dependency syscall requirements still apply.
The standalone Linux libcurl uses its nonblocking pipe-and-fcntl wakeup
backend, avoiding a runtime requirement for eventfd or pipe2 while retaining
wakeup and asynchronous DNS support.
If Linux lacks waitid, the process adapter checks its own child's parent and
zombie state through procfs without reaping.  Normal collection still uses
waitpid, retaining the process-group leader until family cleanup is finished.
This fallback requires readable /proc/PID/stat files.
The OS must supply secure entropy.  Mbed TLS retains /dev/random; on Linux
before 5.6, it may block when entropy is depleted even after initial seeding.
Maintain the system's entropy source rather than weakening TLS randomness.
Official Linux recipes select Mbed TLS for provider HTTPS, native voice DTLS
and SRTP.  They build libarchive with Nettle cryptography.
.PP
The x86-64 and legacy i686 targets share the old-kernel clock fallback
described below.  Musl's translated clock error is checked with a raw syscall;
emulation requires the kernel to report ENOSYS.
.TP
.B make prod-linux-armv6
Build the shared ARMv6KZ/VFPv2 hard-float static PIE using the pinned muslpi
recipe.  It covers Raspberry Pi 1/Zero-class ARMv6 and ARMv7 systems with a
hard-float environment; other ABIs need a separate build and qualification.
Libraries, TLS and trust roots are embedded.  Output is
.IR build/matrix/linux-armv6/bin/snajpagent .
Existing QEMU checks cover Alpine 3.22.5 ARM1176 and Cortex-A7 guests; physical
boards and other kernels need separate qualification.  Emulator device-tree and
clock adjustments in those checks are not physical-board performance evidence.
The configured shell and external commands still belong to the target system.
.TP
.B make prod-linux-riscv64
Build an RV64GC/LP64D static PIE with the pinned musl recipe, application
libraries, TLS and trust roots included.  Output is
.IR build/matrix/linux-riscv64/bin/snajpagent .
Existing QEMU RV64 checks cover Alpine 3.22.5/Linux 6.12.94, including native
inspection, commands, PTY, resume and TLS.  Earlier kernels and physical-board
performance remain unqualified.
.TP
.B make prod-linux-ppc64le
Build the little-endian POWER8 ELFv2 static PIE with the pinned musl toolchain.
Application libraries, TLS and trust roots are included.  Output is
.IR build/matrix/linux-ppc64le/bin/snajpagent .
Existing QEMU POWER8 checks cover Alpine 3.22.5/Linux 6.12.94.  Big-endian,
pre-POWER8 and earlier-kernel systems require separate qualification.
.TP
.B make prod-linux-ppc32
Opt-in unsupported build outside the production matrix.  It is not shipped as
a 0.99.8 executable or update channel.  Build a 32-bit big-endian PowerPC
hard-float static PIE, with static compiler
atomics and embedded application libraries, TLS and trust roots.  Output is
.IR build/matrix/linux-ppc32/bin/snajpagent .
Existing QEMU PowerPC 750 checks cover Debian 8.11/Linux 3.16.0-6-powerpc.
Soft-float systems and earlier kernels remain unqualified.  The target must
provide secure OS entropy; old Linux /dev/random can block if depleted.
.PP
These three production targets and the optional PPC32 build need no ELF
interpreter or shared application libraries.
Matching symbols are kept beside each matrix executable.  See DEPENDENCIES.md
and QUALIFICATION.md for the scope of earlier tests; a documentation refresh
or a new compilation does not establish additional runtime coverage.
.TP
.B make prod-linux-i686-legacy
Build a separate static non-PIE i686 executable using uClibc-ng/LinuxThreads
and GCC's pthread-key TLS emulation, with embedded CA and locale data.
Output is
.IR build/matrix/linux-i686-legacy/bin/snajpagent ,
with optional symbols in the adjacent .debug directory.
Actual Linux 2.4.27-3-386 on QEMU Pentium III has exercised base/IRC checks,
read-only enforcement, parallel commands, PTY, resume, TLS verification and
a hostname-based local provider connection.  This is not a claim for every
2.4 kernel or a paid provider test.  Prefer the modern i686 static build
where it runs; the legacy variant requires working procfs, secure OS entropy,
and devpts for PTY commands.  LANG=en_US.UTF-8 selects an included UTF-8 locale.
.PP
Native kernel interfaces stay first.  Rejected atomic socket flags fall back
to checked fcntl nonblocking/close-on-exec setup.  If kernel clocks are absent,
realtime uses the old gettimeofday syscall and monotonic time uses /proc/uptime
at 10 ms resolution.  The standard i386 jiffies wrap is extended while late
samples are clamped; samples must be less than about 248 days apart to
disambiguate wrap.  Wall-clock time is never used as a monotonic substitute.
Legacy flag setup remains non-atomic; no external helper command is invoked.
.TP
.BR "make prod-macos-arm64" , " make prod-macos-x86_64"
Cross-build for the selected macOS deployment target with static application
libraries and CA data. Capture, playback and realtime voice are enabled;
the executable uses the system libraries and audio frameworks. Output is
.IR build/matrix/macos-ARCH/bin/snajpagent ,
with an adjacent matching dSYM.
.TP
.B make prod-macos-universal
Combine both Mac slices into a native Mach-O universal executable and dSYM
under
.IR build/matrix/macos-universal/bin/ .
.PP
The macOS cross-builds are experimental: cross-built and inspected, not yet
runtime-qualified on macOS.  No Developer ID signing or notarization is implied.
ARM64 has a linker-generated ad-hoc signature; the Intel slice is unsigned.
Cross-compilation alone does not establish runtime support.
.TP
.B make prod-matrix
Build all currently implemented standalone production targets: Linux
x86-64/AArch64/ARMv6/RISC-V/PPC64LE/i686, macOS ARM64/Intel/universal,
Windows x86-64/ARM64, and FreeBSD/NetBSD/OpenBSD with separate legacy ABI
targets. The deferred Linux i686 legacy and unsupported opt-in PPC32 recipes
remain available separately.
Targets use separate build/matrix outputs and shared immutable Nix inputs.
Concurrency is selected from online CPUs, current host load and available
memory, reserving 4 GiB for the host and 4 GiB per target; GNU make's load
limit delays new target starts if host load increases. Missing resource data
defaults to one target. Each recipe permits one Nix job and core. SDK
availability does not silently remove targets. Use make \-k prod-matrix to
continue independent targets after a failure; the command still fails and
preserves successful outputs.
Reruns reuse completed Nix builds.  DEBUG=1 is rejected.
This does not install, replace the native executable, boot VMs, or contact a
model.  Cross-build success does not establish runtime qualification.
.TP
.B make prod-freebsd-amd64
Build a FreeBSD amd64 executable with static application libraries and embedded
CA data. The system must provide libc.so.7, libthr.so.3 and /libexec/ld-elf.so.1.
Copy
.I build/matrix/freebsd-amd64/bin/snajpagent
to the target; optional matching symbols are in the adjacent .debug directory.
Runtime qualification covers FreeBSD 8.4 and 14.4 base/IRC tests, internal read-only
inspection, parallel commands, PTY execution, interactive resume and TLS
trust/hostname verification with local fixtures. Use a UTF-8 locale and mounted
devfs. The executable is non-PIE at this old ABI baseline. Other releases
remain unverified.
.PP
Source builds targeting pre-8 FreeBSD use validated pathname fallbacks for
missing descriptor-relative filesystem calls. Avoid concurrent directory
renames: external renames can race validation and access, and moves to a
different parent require reopening the directory. Close-on-exec flag setup is
non-atomic on these old kernels.
.TP
.B make prod-freebsd-amd64-legacy
Build the FreeBSD 5.1/5.5 amd64 agent with static application libraries and
embedded CA data. Copy
.I build/matrix/freebsd-amd64-legacy/bin/snajpagent
to a system providing libc.so.5, libc_r.so.5 and /usr/libexec/ld-elf.so.1.
Matching symbols are in the adjacent .debug directory. Mount devfs before use.
This target includes UTF-8 regex and display-width support and uses the C
character locale; installed UTF-8 locale files are optional. This legacy executable is non-PIE and its old system runtime
has no stack-protector support; its stack is non-executable.
.PP
Actual FreeBSD 5.1 and 5.5 qualification covers base/IRC/SSE tests, internal read-only
inspection and denied writes, parallel commands, PTY execution, interactive
resume and TLS trust/hostname verification with local fixtures. Other releases
remain unverified for this artifact. The pathname race limits above apply.
.TP
.B make prod-openbsd-amd64
Build the experimental OpenBSD 7.9 amd64 agent with static application
libraries, UTF-8 regex/display-width support and embedded CA data. Copy
.I build/matrix/openbsd-amd64/bin/snajpagent
into the target system; matching symbols are in the adjacent .debug directory.
The PIE executable requires libc.so.103.0, libpthread.so.28.1 and
/usr/libexec/ld.so. It uses the C character locale with its static Unicode
engine. Other OpenBSD releases require separate ABI qualification.
.PP
OpenBSD 7.9 checks cover base/IRC/SSE tests, read-only inspection and denied
writes, parallel commands, PTY execution, durable resume and TLS trust/hostname
verification with local fixtures.
.TP
.B make prod-openbsd-amd64-legacy
Build the experimental OpenBSD 5.9 amd64 agent at
.I build/matrix/openbsd-amd64-legacy/bin/snajpagent
with matching symbols in .debug. It requires libc.so.84.2, libpthread.so.20.1
and /usr/libexec/ld.so. Application libraries, Unicode support, compiler
thread-local storage support and CA data are static. This PIE executable uses
a separate ABI from the 7.9 output. Child exit observation preserves the
owner's final wait and exit status. Base/IRC/SSE tests, read-only enforcement,
parallel commands, PTY, interactive resume and TLS trust/hostname checks have
been exercised on 5.9.
.TP
.B make prod-openbsd-amd64-early
Build the experimental OpenBSD 3.5 amd64 agent at
.I build/matrix/openbsd-amd64-early/bin/snajpagent
with matching symbols in .debug. It requires libc.so.30.3, libpthread.so.2.4
and /usr/libexec/ld.so, a separate ABI from the 5.9 and 7.9 outputs.
Application libraries, Unicode, C99 formatting, compiler support and CA data
are static. The native startup ABI uses a non-PIE executable with a
non-executable stack and no compiler stack protection.
.PP
The 3.5 filesystem adapter validates directory identity and rejects symlinks,
but pathname-based operations retain an external rename race; moving a
held directory across parents requires reopening it. Close-on-exec setup is
not atomic. Native /dev/urandom supplies entropy, and fsync supplies file
durability. Child exit observation preserves the final wait and status.
Base/configuration/SSE/IRC, read-only enforcement, parallel commands, PTY,
CLI/interactive resume and TLS trust/hostname checks were exercised on 3.5.
.TP
.B make prod-netbsd-amd64
Build the experimental NetBSD 10.1 amd64 agent at
.I build/matrix/netbsd-amd64/bin/snajpagent
with matching symbols in .debug. It requires libc.so.12, libpthread.so.1
and /libexec/ld.elf_so. Application libraries, Unicode, TLS and CA data are
static. The executable uses PIE, full RELRO, stack protection and a
non-executable stack. Native directory-relative filesystem operations,
non-reaping child exit observation and PTY commands are supported.
Base/configuration/SSE/IRC, read-only enforcement, parallel commands,
CLI/interactive resume and TLS trust/hostname checks were exercised on 10.1.
.TP
.B make prod-netbsd-amd64-legacy
Build the experimental NetBSD 2.0/5.2.3 amd64 agent at
.I build/matrix/netbsd-amd64-legacy/bin/snajpagent
with matching symbols in .debug. It requires libc.so.12, libpthread.so.0
and /libexec/ld.elf_so. NetBSD 10.1 uses a different pthread ABI.
Application libraries, Unicode, compiler thread-local support and CA data are
static. The native loader requires a non-PIE executable with two load segments;
the build retains a non-executable stack without RELRO or stack protection
(the NetBSD 2.0 runtime lacks the stack-protector symbols).
.PP
The filesystem adapter validates directory identity and rejects symlinks,
but pathname-based operations retain an external rename race; moving a
held directory across parents requires reopening it. Close-on-exec setup is
not atomic. Native entropy and fdatasync supply randomness and file durability.
Child exit observation preserves the final wait and status. Base/configuration/
SSE/IRC, read-only enforcement, parallel commands, PTY, CLI/interactive resume
and TLS trust/hostname checks were exercised on 2.0 and 5.2.3.
.TP
.BR "make prod-windows-x86_64" , " make prod-windows-arm64"
Build the experimental native Windows x64 or ARM64 executable using pinned Nix
dependencies.  Copy
.I build/matrix/windows-ARCH/bin/snajpagent.exe
to Windows.  Application libraries and CA roots are included; Nix and
third-party runtime DLLs are not needed there.  Optional matching symbols
are in the adjacent .debug directory.  Runtime qualification is limited
to tested modern Windows PE guests (x64 build 26100, ARM64 build 28000);
full desktop and older Windows
remain in progress.  Plain make still builds only the host platform.
.PP
Windows terminal output selects native VT processing when available and
native cursor, color, and erase operations for classic consoles.  The classic
path preserves the initial console palette and supports prompt editing and
wrapping.  Glyphs and text decorations depend on the console font; italics
use underline where supported.
.PP
On systems without synchronous I/O cancellation, output uses an authenticated
same-executable helper.  Inheritable standard console buffers are retained at
helper creation, including inactive buffers; other descriptors use exact
handle transfer.  An unsupported transfer reports an error.
.PP
Windows x64 and the internal i686 build use a hidden-console collector for PTY
commands when ConPTY is unavailable.  The same executable hosts collection;
input and output use authenticated private pipes, and command descendants
belong to the agent's cleanup job.  The collector translates native console
cells and keyboard events; font and code-page limitations of the underlying
console still apply.  ConPTY is selected whenever available.
.PP
See the repository's DEPENDENCIES.md for static dependency licenses, embedded
trust, corresponding-source obligations and platform qualification details.
.SH "Examples"
Start an interactive session in the current directory:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent
.fi
.PP
Execute a task from a script:
.PP
.nf
printf '%s\en' 'run the tests and summarize failures' | snajpagent -e
.fi
.PP
Resume the newest saved session:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent --resume --last
.fi
.PP
Host a room and connect another agent:
.PP
.nf
snajpagent -s localhost:6667 -n builder -o alice -r work
snajpagent -c localhost:6667 -n reviewer -o bob
.fi
.SH "Security"
The model and its tools run with the launching user's OS permissions across
the filesystem. Calls require no per-call approval and are hidden at default
verbosity. New sessions start with HOME as the working directory; OS process
and filesystem isolation applies normally. snajpagent requires its state
directories to be mode 0700 and creates state files mode 0600.
.PP
Prompt history intentionally retains submitted operator text across sessions
and chat/rollout modes.  It cannot identify a secret deliberately
typed by the operator; remove or protect the history file with the same care as
shell history.  Session deletion does not erase it.
.PP
Provider credential variables and configured
.B secret
sources are removed from tool environments when environment-backed, and resolved
values are redacted before tool output is
stored and displayed.  This does not decide which file contents are sent to a provider or published.
.PP
Provider redirects are not followed.  The IRC server is unauthenticated and
unencrypted; keep its default local listener unless the surrounding network is
trusted or independently secured.
.PP
Verbosity 5 exposes prompts, source, tool output, and model content.  Review
terminal capture before sharing it.
